/*
 * Eduqas GCSE Geography B (C112) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Real Eduqas GCSE Geography B (C112) questions are built on
 * resources (described figures, tables, maps and photos) and named case studies,
 * with fieldwork questions on sampling, presentation and evaluation. Every item
 * here opens with a fully described resource or a located context.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Part tariffs follow the real mix taken from the site's own past-paper
 * data for this specification.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. Board style: point-marked items one point per line; extended
 * items give indicative content and the board's level ladder, including SPaG
 * where the board awards it.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const GEOGRAPHY_EDUQAS_PRACTICE = {
  '1.1': {
    name: 'Urbanisation in Contrasting Global Cities',
    questions: [
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nThe proportion of the world's population living in towns and cities passed 50% for the first time in about 2007.\n\nChoose the correct definition of the term 'urbanisation'.\nA  The growth in the physical size of a city into the surrounding countryside\nB  An increase in the proportion of a country's population living in urban areas\nC  The movement of people from cities to small towns and villages`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1) (AO1)\nDo not credit more than one answer chosen.\nA describes urban sprawl; C describes counter-urbanisation.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.1: a table of the projected populations of six megacities in 2030, in millions (rounded figures written for this practice question).\n\nCity | Population 2030 (millions)\nDelhi | 38.9\nTokyo | 36.6\nShanghai | 32.9\nDhaka | 28.1\nKinshasa | 21.9\nLagos | 20.6\n\nUsing Figure 1.1, calculate the range of the projected populations.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `18.3 million (1) (AO4)\n(38.9 − 20.6 = 18.3)\nUnits not required. Do not credit "20.6 to 38.9" without the calculation.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nTokyo, Delhi, Shanghai, São Paulo and Mumbai are all described as megacities.\n\nComplete the sentence:\n"A megacity is an urban area with a population of more than ______ million people."`, marks: 1, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `10 (1) (AO1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nLondon is often described as a global city.\n\nWhich of the following is a characteristic of a global city?\nA  Most of its workers are employed in farming\nB  It is a major centre of finance and business with connections to cities around the world\nC  Its population has fallen by more than half in the last 50 years\nD  It is the capital city of an LIC`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1) (AO1)\nDo not credit more than one answer chosen.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.2: a map of India. Mumbai is marked as a black dot on the west coast of India, on a peninsula jutting into the Arabian Sea. It lies in the state of Maharashtra, roughly 1,150 km south-west of the capital, New Delhi, and north of the city of Goa. A scale bar and north arrow are shown.\n\nUsing Figure 1.2, describe the location of Mumbai.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid locational point, up to 2 marks (AO4):\n• on the west coast of India (1)\n• on the coast of / beside the Arabian Sea (1)\n• in the state of Maharashtra (1)\n• (approximately 1,150 km) south-west of New Delhi (1)\n• north of Goa (1)\nDo not credit "in India" alone.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.3: a table of the percentage of the population living in urban areas by world region (rounded figures based on United Nations estimates).\n\nRegion | 2018 (%) | 2050 projected (%)\nNorth America | 82 | 89\nLatin America and the Caribbean | 81 | 88\nEurope | 74 | 84\nOceania | 68 | 74\nAsia | 50 | 66\nAfrica | 43 | 59\n\nChoose the two correct statements.\nA  Africa was the most urbanised region in 2018\nB  All regions are projected to become more urbanised by 2050\nC  Europe is projected to be more urbanised than North America in 2050\nD  Half of Asia's population lived in urban areas in 2018\nE  Oceania has the largest projected increase`, marks: 2, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)\nD (1)\n(AO4) If more than two letters are chosen, deduct 1 mark for each incorrect letter.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nCensus figures for Greater Mumbai, India: population in 1971 = 5,970,575; population in 2011 = 12,442,373.\n\nCalculate the percentage change in the population of Greater Mumbai between 1971 and 2011. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: (12,442,373 − 5,970,575) ÷ 5,970,575 × 100 (1)\nAnswer: 108.4% (increase) (1)\n(AO4) Accept 108% or 108.39%. Correct answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nDharavi is a large informal settlement in central Mumbai. It covers an area of about 2.1 km². Estimates of its population vary widely; one estimate is 850,000 people (estimate written for this practice question).\n\nUsing this information, calculate the population density of Dharavi in people per km². Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 850,000 ÷ 2.1 (1)\nAnswer: 404,762 people per km² (1)\n(AO4) Accept 404,761.9 or answers rounded to 405,000 / 400,000. Correct answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nRavi is 19 and lives in a farming village in Bihar, a rural state in north-east India. Last year the monsoon rains failed and his family's crops died. His cousin has a job on a building site in Mumbai, more than 1,500 km away.\n\nGive one push factor and one pull factor that might cause Ravi to migrate to Mumbai.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Push factor — 1 mark for one of: crop failure / drought / failed monsoon; poverty / low income from farming; lack of jobs in the village; few services such as schools or hospitals (1)\nPull factor — 1 mark for one of: jobs available, e.g. in construction or factories; higher wages; family / cousin already there to help find work and housing; better access to education or health care (1)\n(AO2) Factors must be correctly identified as push or pull.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nIn many cities in LICs and NICs, such as Lagos in Nigeria, natural increase now adds more people to the population each year than rural-urban migration does.\n\nSuggest one reason why natural increase is high in cities in LICs and NICs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason, plus 1 mark for development (AO2):\n• most migrants are young adults (1) who are of child-bearing age, so many children are born in the city (1)\n• better health care in cities than in rural areas (1) so death rates, especially infant mortality, fall while birth rates stay high (1)\n• families may want many children (1) to earn money for the family / to care for parents in old age (1)\n• limited access to family planning / contraception for poor families (1) so birth rates remain high (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFact box — London:\n• The City of London and Canary Wharf contain the headquarters of many international banks and insurance companies.\n• Heathrow is one of the busiest international airports in the world.\n• Over 300 languages are spoken by London's residents.\n• The city hosts major cultural and sporting events, such as the 2012 Olympic Games.\n\nUsing the fact box, describe two features that make London a global city.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each feature described, up to 2 marks (AO4):\n• a world financial / business centre with headquarters of global banks and companies (1)\n• a global transport hub — Heathrow links London directly to cities around the world (1)\n• a multicultural city / attracts international migrants, shown by over 300 languages spoken (1)\n• a world cultural / sporting centre that attracts global audiences and tourists, e.g. the 2012 Olympics (1)\nDo not credit simply copying a bullet point without saying how it makes London global — allow if the link to global connections is clear.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.4: a photograph of shacks made of corrugated iron, plastic sheeting and wood squeezed into a narrow strip of land between a railway line and a polluted creek in Mumbai. Some shacks are built on stilts over the water.\n\nGive two reasons why informal settlements are often built on land like that shown in Figure 1.4.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each reason, up to 2 marks (AO2):\n• the land is unwanted / unsuitable for official building, e.g. liable to flood, polluted (1)\n• the land is public / not owned by anyone who will build on it, so people can occupy it without being moved immediately (1)\n• it is the only space left / available in a crowded city (1)\n• it is close to jobs in the city centre / near transport such as the railway (1)\n• migrants cannot afford to rent or buy formal housing (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nYou have carried out fieldwork in an urban area to investigate how the quality of the environment changes from the city centre to the edge of the city.\n\nName one technique you could use to collect primary data on environmental quality and give one reason why it is suitable.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Technique (1): e.g. an environmental quality survey / bi-polar scoring sheet; a litter count; a noise meter reading; a building quality survey; field sketch or annotated photographs.\nReason (1): a valid reason linked to the technique, e.g. a bi-polar survey turns opinions into scores so sites can be compared; a noise meter gives an objective numerical reading; the same sheet can be used at every site so results are consistent.\n(AO3)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.5: a bar chart of the projected populations of megacities in 2030 (y-axis "population (millions)", 0–40, with lines every 5 million). Bars are already drawn for Delhi (38.9), Tokyo (36.6) and Shanghai (32.9). The bars for Dhaka and Lagos are blank.\n\nDescribe how you would complete Figure 1.5 to show Dhaka = 28.1 million and Lagos = 20.6 million.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each point, up to 2 marks (AO4):\n• draw a bar for Dhaka to just above 28 on the y-axis / between the 25 and 30 lines (1)\n• draw a bar for Lagos to just above 20 / between the 20 and 25 lines (1)\n• bars drawn the same width as the others, with gaps between them, and shaded / labelled in the same way (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.6: a table of data for five London boroughs (data written for this practice question).\n\nBorough | Children in poverty (%) | Male life expectancy (years) | Median weekly pay of people working in the borough (£)\nTower Hamlets | 48 | 78.8 | 820\nNewham | 42 | 78.4 | 640\nKensington and Chelsea | 26 | 83.9 | 780\nRichmond upon Thames | 15 | 82.6 | 760\nBarking and Dagenham | 39 | 77.9 | 610\n\nUsing Figure 1.6, calculate the mean percentage of children in poverty for the five boroughs. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: (48 + 42 + 26 + 15 + 39) ÷ 5 = 170 ÷ 5 (1)\nAnswer: 34% (1)\n(AO4) Correct answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nAn estimated one third of Mumbai's workers are employed in the informal economy, for example as street vendors, rubbish recyclers in Dharavi, or dabbawalas who deliver home-cooked lunches to office workers.\n\nGive two advantages of the informal economy for people living in Mumbai.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each advantage, up to 2 marks (AO2):\n• provides an income for people who cannot get formal jobs, e.g. new migrants with few qualifications (1)\n• easy to start — needs little money or skill (1)\n• provides cheap goods and services for other poor residents (1)\n• recycling reduces waste / provides cheap raw materials for industry (1)\n• people can work flexible hours / work from home (1)\nDo not credit disadvantages such as no tax being paid.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nA student wants to show the percentage of people living in informal settlements in each of the 24 municipal wards of Mumbai.\n\nSuggest one reason why a choropleth map would be an appropriate technique to show this information.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason, plus 1 mark for development (AO3):\n• the data is for areas / wards with clear boundaries (1) so each ward can be shaded according to its percentage (1)\n• the data is in percentages / ratios rather than totals (1) so wards of different sizes can be compared fairly (1)\n• it shows the spatial pattern clearly (1) so you can see whether wards with the most informal housing are clustered, e.g. in the suburbs or near the centre (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.3: a table of the percentage of the population living in urban areas by world region (rounded figures based on United Nations estimates).\n\nRegion | 2018 (%) | 2050 projected (%)\nNorth America | 82 | 89\nLatin America and the Caribbean | 81 | 88\nEurope | 74 | 84\nOceania | 68 | 74\nAsia | 50 | 66\nAfrica | 43 | 59\n\nUsing Figure 1.3, complete the sentences:\n"In 2018 the most urbanised region was ______."\n"Between 2018 and 2050, the percentage of Africa's population living in urban areas is projected to increase by ______ percentage points."\n"The region with the smallest projected increase is ______."`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `North America (1)\n16 (1) (59 − 43)\nOceania (1) (increase of 6)\n(AO4)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.7: a line graph of the population of Lagos, Nigeria, 1950–2030 (figures written for this practice question). The population was about 0.3 million in 1950 and rose slowly to 1.4 million in 1970. Growth then speeded up: 4.8 million in 1990, 7.3 million in 2000, 10.6 million in 2010 and 14.4 million in 2020. The line is dashed after 2020 and reaches a projected 20.6 million in 2030.\n\nDescribe the trend shown in Figure 1.7.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid descriptive point, up to 3 marks (AO4):\n• the population increased throughout the period / continuous growth (1)\n• growth was slow between 1950 and 1970 (1)\n• growth was more rapid after 1970 / the rate of growth increased (1)\n• the population passed 10 million / became a megacity by 2010 (1)\n• it is projected to reach 20.6 million by 2030 (1)\n• use of data, e.g. an increase of about 14 million between 1950 and 2020 (1)\nMaximum 2 marks if no data is used.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nAbout 84% of the UK's population lives in urban areas. In Nigeria the figure is just over 50%, but its urban population is growing much faster.\n\nExplain why the rate of urbanisation is now slow in HICs such as the UK.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid point and 1 mark for development, up to 3 marks (AO1/AO2):\n• most people already live in urban areas (1) so there are few rural people left to migrate (1)\n• urbanisation happened earlier, during the Industrial Revolution (1)\n• birth rates are low (1) so there is little natural increase in cities (1)\n• counter-urbanisation (1) — some people move out of cities to rural areas / small towns, e.g. to retire or commute (1)\n• improved transport and internet (1) allow people to work in cities while living in the countryside (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nMumbai Metro Line 1 is an elevated railway built above existing roads. It opened in 2014 and links Versova in the west of the city with Ghatkopar in the east, a journey that used to take up to 90 minutes by road.\n\nSuggest how projects like this can improve quality of life for people living in Mumbai.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid point and 1 mark for development, up to 3 marks (AO2):\n• faster journeys (1) giving people more time for family / leisure, or reaching jobs further away (1)\n• fewer cars and buses on the roads (1) so less air pollution / fewer breathing problems (1)\n• less overcrowding on suburban trains (1) so travel is safer / fewer accidents (1)\n• built above roads (1) so few homes had to be demolished (1)\n• air-conditioned / more comfortable travel (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.8: a table of the world's ten largest urban areas in 1990 and projected for 2030 (written for this practice question).\n\n1990: Tokyo (Japan), Osaka (Japan), New York (USA), Mexico City (Mexico), São Paulo (Brazil), Mumbai (India), Kolkata (India), Los Angeles (USA), Seoul (South Korea), Buenos Aires (Argentina).\n2030 (projected): Delhi (India), Tokyo (Japan), Shanghai (China), Dhaka (Bangladesh), Cairo (Egypt), Mumbai (India), Beijing (China), Mexico City (Mexico), São Paulo (Brazil), Kinshasa (DR Congo).\n\nDescribe the pattern of expected changes in the location of the world's largest cities shown in Figure 1.8.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid descriptive point, up to 4 marks (AO4):\n• fewer of the largest cities will be in HICs / more will be in LICs and NICs (1)\n• no cities in North America in 2030 (New York and Los Angeles drop out) (1)\n• more cities in Asia, e.g. Delhi, Shanghai, Dhaka and Beijing appear (1)\n• cities in Africa appear for the first time, e.g. Cairo and Kinshasa (1)\n• Asia remains the continent with the most large cities (5 in 1990, 6 in 2030) (1)\n• South America has fewer, e.g. Buenos Aires drops out (1)\n• some cities stay in the top ten, e.g. Tokyo, Mumbai, Mexico City, São Paulo (1)\nMaximum 3 marks if no named examples from Figure 1.8 are used.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nCities in LICs and NICs often have serious traffic congestion and overcrowded public transport.\n\nDescribe how a city in an LIC/NIC you have studied is tackling transport problems. Name the city.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `AO1 (4 marks). Credit place-specific detail. City named but no description = 0.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A clear description of two or more strategies with place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A basic description of one or more strategies; generic or lacking detail.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content (Mumbai):\n• Mumbai Metro — Line 1 elevated railway (Versova–Ghatkopar, opened 2014); further lines under construction, including an underground line\n• Mumbai Monorail (opened 2014) linking eastern suburbs\n• Bandra–Worli Sea Link bridge (2009) and the Mumbai Coastal Road, which take traffic away from congested inner roads\n• upgrading the suburban railway, e.g. longer 15-car trains, air-conditioned trains, automatic doors\n• flyovers and road widening; electric buses introduced by BEST\nCredit other valid cities, e.g. Lagos (Bus Rapid Transit, Blue Line rail), Rio de Janeiro (BRT, metro extension), Curitiba (BRT).` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nEvery day, hundreds of migrants from rural areas arrive at the main railway stations in Mumbai.\n\nExplain why rural-urban migration happens in LICs and NICs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (2 marks), AO2 (2 marks).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of push and/or pull factors, with reasons linked to developed points. Uses an example.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; may list factors with little development.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content:\n• push: rural poverty, low income from subsistence farming; crop failure, drought or floods; mechanisation of farming reduces jobs; lack of schools, hospitals, electricity and clean water; land shortages as rural population grows\n• pull: more jobs in factories, construction and services; higher wages; better education and health care; family and friends already living in the city provide contacts and a place to stay; the "bright lights" — perceived better quality of life\n• e.g. migrants to Mumbai from Uttar Pradesh and Bihar.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nIt is estimated that over 40% of Mumbai's population lives in informal settlements such as Dharavi.\n\nExplain why informal settlements develop in cities in LICs and NICs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (2 marks), AO2 (2 marks).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation with linked reasons, e.g. rapid growth leading to housing shortage leading to self-built homes.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; reasons listed with little linkage.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content:\n• rapid population growth from rural-urban migration and natural increase\n• city authorities cannot build enough affordable housing / provide services fast enough\n• migrants are poor and cannot afford formal rents or land prices, which are very high in Mumbai\n• people build their own homes from scrap materials on unused or unsafe land (railway sides, marshes, steep slopes)\n• people want to live close to jobs in the city centre, so they stay in crowded central areas such as Dharavi\n• settlements grow over time as relatives join migrants.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nIn London, Kensington and Chelsea is one of the richest areas in the UK, while parts of nearby boroughs such as Tower Hamlets and Newham have some of the highest rates of child poverty in the country.\n\nGive two reasons why inequality exists within a global city such as London.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Award up to 2 marks for each reason: 1 mark for the reason and 1 mark for development (AO2).\n• very high house prices and rents (1) so low-paid workers live in overcrowded or poor-quality housing, or are forced out of central areas (1)\n• a divide between highly paid jobs in finance / business (1) and low-paid service jobs such as cleaning, retail and catering (1)\n• differences in education and skills (1) — people without qualifications cannot get the well-paid jobs in the City or Canary Wharf (1)\n• decline of traditional industries, e.g. the London docks closed in the 1960s–1980s (1), leaving high unemployment in East London (1)\n• gentrification (1) pushes up prices so original residents cannot afford to stay (1)\n• some migrants arrive with few resources / face discrimination (1) so start in low-paid work (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nLondon has more international flights, foreign-owned companies and overseas students than almost any other city in the world.\n\nExplain how a global city in an HIC is connected to the rest of the world.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (2 marks), AO2 (2 marks).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of two or more types of connection, with development and place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; connections listed with little development.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content (London):\n• financial connections — the City of London and Canary Wharf trade currencies, shares and insurance worldwide 24 hours a day; headquarters of transnational corporations\n• transport — Heathrow, Gatwick and other airports link to hundreds of destinations; Eurostar from St Pancras to Paris and Brussels; Port of London / London Gateway\n• migration — people from all over the world live and work in London; over 300 languages spoken\n• culture and media — BBC World Service, film, music, fashion; global sporting events such as the 2012 Olympics and Premier League football watched worldwide\n• education and tourism — international students at London universities; millions of overseas tourists each year\n• digital / communication links — internet data centres, telecommunications.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.9: a photograph of a lane in Dharavi, Mumbai. Two- and three-storey buildings made of brick and corrugated iron are packed closely together and the lane is less than two metres wide. Open drains run along both sides, plastic waste is piled in sacks outside workshops, electric cables are tangled overhead and a woman is filling plastic containers at a single standpipe.\n\nUsing Figure 1.9, describe the characteristics of this informal settlement.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each characteristic described from the photograph, up to 4 marks (AO4):\n• buildings very close together / high density / overcrowded (1)\n• built from cheap or scrap materials such as corrugated iron (1)\n• narrow lanes / no proper roads (1)\n• open drains / poor sanitation (1)\n• limited water supply — a shared standpipe (1)\n• informal / illegal electricity connections (tangled cables) (1)\n• waste piled in the street (1)\n• homes also used as workshops / small industry / recycling (1)\nDevelopment, e.g. linking open drains to the risk of disease, can be credited within the 4 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nIn parts of Dharavi, Mumbai, it has been reported that hundreds of people share a single toilet block and water is only available for a few hours each day.\n\nExplain why it is difficult to provide clean water and sanitation for people living in informal settlements in a city in an LIC/NIC.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of two or more difficulties, with linked reasons.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; difficulties listed.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content:\n• population grows faster than the authorities can build pipes and sewers\n• settlements are illegal / unplanned, so authorities may not want to provide services or recognise them\n• very high density and narrow lanes make it hard to lay pipes and sewers\n• residents are too poor to pay for water connections or bills\n• lack of government money / competing demands for investment\n• land is often marshy or prone to flooding, so sewers are hard to build and drains overflow in the monsoon.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.6: a table of data for five London boroughs (data written for this practice question).\n\nBorough | Children in poverty (%) | Male life expectancy (years) | Median weekly pay of people working in the borough (£)\nTower Hamlets | 48 | 78.8 | 820\nNewham | 42 | 78.4 | 640\nKensington and Chelsea | 26 | 83.9 | 780\nRichmond upon Thames | 15 | 82.6 | 760\nBarking and Dagenham | 39 | 77.9 | 610\n\nChoose the correct answer in each statement:\nThe borough with the highest percentage of children in poverty is (Tower Hamlets / Newham / Barking and Dagenham).\nThe difference between the highest and lowest male life expectancy is (4.5 / 6.0 / 8.0) years.\nThe borough with the lowest median weekly pay is (Newham / Barking and Dagenham / Richmond upon Thames).\nThe borough with the highest median weekly pay is (Kensington and Chelsea / Tower Hamlets / Richmond upon Thames).`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `Tower Hamlets (1)\n6.0 (1) (83.9 − 77.9)\nBarking and Dagenham (1)\nTower Hamlets (1)\n(AO4)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nIn 2022 the Maharashtra state government chose a private company, the Adani Group, to redevelop Dharavi. Under the plan, many residents would be moved into new high-rise flats, and some would be rehoused away from Dharavi.\n\nExplain why some people living in Dharavi oppose plans to redevelop it.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of two or more reasons, with linked development.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; reasons listed.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content:\n• many businesses (pottery, leather, recycling, garment making) operate from homes and would lose space or customers in high-rise flats\n• residents who cannot prove they lived there before the cut-off date may not get a free flat and could be moved far from their jobs\n• moving away breaks up close-knit communities, family and religious networks\n• high-rise flats bring maintenance charges and bills that poor families cannot afford\n• distrust that a private company will put profit (selling valuable land) before residents' needs\n• residents feel they have not been properly consulted.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nThe Mayor of London aims for London to become a net zero carbon city by 2030.\n\nDescribe how a city in an HIC you have studied is trying to become more sustainable. Name the city.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `AO1 (4 marks). Credit place-specific detail. City named but no description = 0.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A clear description of two or more strategies with place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A basic description of one or more strategies; generic or lacking detail.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content (London):\n• Ultra Low Emission Zone (ULEZ) — charges older, polluting vehicles; expanded to all London boroughs in August 2023; Congestion Charge in central London\n• public transport — Elizabeth line opened in 2022; Underground, Overground and electric / hybrid buses\n• cycling — Santander Cycles hire scheme and segregated cycle superhighways\n• brownfield regeneration, e.g. Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park in Stratford on former industrial land; BedZED eco-village in Sutton\n• green spaces and green roofs; recycling targets; Thames Tideway "super sewer" to stop sewage overflowing into the Thames.\nCredit other valid HIC cities.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nIn 1950 only two cities in the world, New York and Tokyo, had populations of over 10 million. By 2018 there were 33 megacities, and most of them were in Asia, Africa and South America.\n\nExplain why the number of megacities is growing fastest in Asia and Africa.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (2 marks), AO2 (2 marks).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of two or more reasons, with linked development.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; reasons listed.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content:\n• many countries in Asia and Africa are still mostly rural, so large numbers of people can still migrate to cities\n• high rates of natural increase — young populations with high birth rates\n• industrialisation and economic growth in NICs such as India and China create jobs in cities, attracting migrants\n• transnational corporations locate factories and offices in large cities, e.g. Shanghai, Mumbai\n• rural push factors — poverty, drought, conflict, lack of services\n• in HICs the population is already mostly urban and birth rates are low, so few new megacities form there.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.10: a flow diagram showing how cities in LICs and NICs grow. The first box reads "Few jobs and poor services in rural areas". An arrow leads to box A, then to box B, then to box C, then to box D. The final box reads "City authorities struggle to provide water, sewers and schools".\n\nMatch each statement to the correct letter (A, B, C or D) in Figure 1.10:\n• Demand for housing is greater than the supply\n• Young adults migrate to the city\n• Informal settlements grow on unused land\n• Many children are born to young migrant families in the city`, marks: 4, command: 'Match', markScheme: `A — Young adults migrate to the city (1)\nB — Many children are born to young migrant families in the city (1)\nC — Demand for housing is greater than the supply (1)\nD — Informal settlements grow on unused land (1)\n(AO2)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nThe Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park in Stratford, East London, was built on former industrial land for the 2012 Olympic Games. Since then, thousands of new homes, offices and a large shopping centre have been built nearby, and house prices in the area have risen sharply.\n\nGive two reasons why the regeneration of areas such as Stratford can cause conflict between different groups of people.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Award up to 2 marks for each reason: 1 mark for the reason and 1 mark for development (AO2).\n• rising house prices and rents / gentrification (1) mean that long-standing local residents can no longer afford to live there (1)\n• not enough affordable / social housing in new developments (1) so benefits go to wealthier newcomers rather than local families (1)\n• new jobs may need skills that local people do not have (1) so unemployment stays high among existing residents (1)\n• businesses and residents were moved / compulsorily purchased to clear the site (1) causing resentment (1)\n• developers and the council want profit / investment (1) while residents want community facilities and green space (1)\n• increased traffic / construction noise and disruption (1) during and after building (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nMumbai's population more than doubled between 1971 and 2011, from about 6 million to over 12 million.\n\nExplain the consequences of rapid urbanisation for people living in a city in an LIC/NIC you have studied.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (2 marks), AO2 (4 marks).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed explanation of a range of consequences, which may be positive and negative, with clear chains of reasoning and accurate place-specific detail.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of some consequences, with some development and some place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; consequences listed; generic.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content (Mumbai):\n• growth of informal settlements, e.g. Dharavi — overcrowding, poor-quality homes, risk of fire and flooding in the monsoon\n• shortage of clean water and toilets leads to diseases such as cholera, typhoid and diarrhoea\n• many people work in the informal economy — low, unreliable pay with no sick pay or safety rules\n• traffic congestion and overcrowded trains; air pollution causing respiratory illness\n• waste disposal problems, e.g. the Deonar landfill; pollution of creeks such as the Mithi River\n• positive: more jobs and higher incomes than in rural areas; access to schools and hospitals; money sent home to rural families (remittances); growth of industries such as recycling, film (Bollywood) and finance.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nIn self-help schemes, local people are given materials, training or low-cost loans to improve their own homes, while the government provides basic services such as water pipes, paved paths and electricity.\n\nExplain how self-help schemes can improve the quality of life of people living in informal settlements. Use examples you have studied.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (2 marks), AO2 (4 marks).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed explanation of how schemes improve quality of life, with clear chains of reasoning and accurate use of examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation with some development; some use of examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic explanation; generic; few or no examples.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content:\n• homes rebuilt with brick and concrete are safer from fire, floods and collapse\n• piped water and toilets reduce water-borne disease, improving health and life expectancy\n• paved paths and drains reduce flooding and mud\n• residents stay in their community and close to their jobs, so social networks and businesses survive\n• cheap for the government — residents provide the labour, so money can help more people\n• people gain building skills and pride; may gain legal rights to their land, encouraging further investment\n• examples: Favela-Bairro project in Rio de Janeiro; Mahila Milan / SPARC and community toilet blocks in Mumbai; Orangi Pilot Project in Karachi.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.11: two ways of improving Dharavi, Mumbai.\nOption 1 — Redevelopment: clear large areas of shacks and workshops and replace them with high-rise blocks of flats, offices and shopping centres built by a private developer. Eligible residents receive a free flat; the developer sells the rest of the space to make a profit.\nOption 2 — Upgrading: keep the existing homes and businesses and improve them gradually, by adding piped water, toilets, drains and paved lanes, and helping residents to rebuild their own homes.\n\nWhich option is the better way to improve the quality of life for people living in Dharavi? Justify your view. You may also use examples you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks), AO3 (4 marks).\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): A well-developed and balanced evaluation of both options, with a clear, well-justified conclusion. Applies understanding of social, economic and environmental impacts. Uses Figure 1.11 and examples effectively.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): A partial evaluation; may focus mainly on one option. A conclusion is given with some justification. Some use of Figure 1.11 and examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A basic answer with little evaluation; a view may be given without justification.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content:\nOption 1 — for: modern, safe homes with water, toilets and electricity; better health; makes efficient use of very valuable central land; brings investment and jobs; paid for by the developer, not the government.\nOption 1 — against: workshops and the informal economy (recycling, pottery, leather) may be lost; ineligible residents may be moved far away from jobs; communities broken up; flats have maintenance costs; profit for the developer may come first; little consultation.\nOption 2 — for: keeps communities and businesses together; residents involved in decisions; cheaper so more people can be helped; examples such as the Favela-Bairro project in Rio de Janeiro improved services while keeping homes.\nOption 2 — against: slow; density and overcrowding remain; buildings may still be unsafe; government must find the money; does not solve land shortage.\nCredit either view, or a view that a combination of the two is best, if justified.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.12: two views about the growth of cities in LICs and NICs.\nView A — "Rapid urban growth brings jobs, investment and better services. Cities are the engines of economic development."\nView B — "Rapid urban growth leads to slums, pollution and poverty. Cities cannot cope with the number of people arriving."\n\nTo what extent do you agree that rapid urban growth in cities in LICs and NICs brings more opportunities than problems? Use examples you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks), AO3 (4 marks).\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): A well-developed and balanced evaluation of opportunities and problems, with a clear, well-justified conclusion about the extent of agreement. Accurate place-specific detail from one or more cities.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): A partial evaluation; may be unbalanced. A conclusion is given with some justification. Some place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A basic answer; mainly descriptive; little evaluation or justification; generic.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content:\nOpportunities: more and better-paid jobs than in rural areas (e.g. Mumbai's finance, port, film and IT industries); informal economy provides income for migrants (Dharavi's recycling and leather trades); better access to schools, hospitals and electricity; remittances to rural areas; attracts TNC investment; some LIC/NIC cities improving services, e.g. Mumbai Metro.\nProblems: informal settlements with poor sanitation and disease; insecure informal jobs; congestion and air pollution; waste and water pollution; risk from flooding in the monsoon; inequality between rich and poor areas.\nEvaluation may consider: who benefits (migrants often better off than in rural areas, even in slums); short-term problems vs long-term development; whether governments can manage growth; differences between cities.\nCredit any well-justified conclusion.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\n\nFigure 1.13: a fact box comparing two global cities.\nLondon (HIC) — population about 8.8 million (2021); ULEZ covers all London boroughs; Elizabeth line opened in 2022; very high house prices; large gaps in income between boroughs.\nMumbai (NIC) — population of Greater Mumbai over 12 million (2011); over 40% of people live in informal settlements; new metro lines and a coastal road being built; frequent monsoon flooding; serious air and water pollution.\n\n"It is easier to make a city in an HIC sustainable than a city in an LIC/NIC."\nHow far do you agree with this statement? Justify your view, using examples of cities you have studied.\n\n(8 marks + 3 marks for SPaG)`, marks: 8, command: 'How far', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks), AO3 (4 marks).\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): A well-developed and balanced evaluation of the challenges and strategies in both cities, linked to the social, economic and environmental aspects of sustainability. A clear, well-justified conclusion. Accurate place-specific detail.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): A partial evaluation; may focus mainly on one city. A conclusion with some justification. Some place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A basic answer; mainly descriptive; little evaluation; generic.\n0 marks: No creditworthy response.\nIndicative content:\nAgree: HICs have more money to invest, e.g. London's Elizabeth line (2022), ULEZ expansion (2023), Thames Tideway sewer; slower population growth makes planning easier; strong governance and planning laws; most homes already have water, sanitation and electricity.\nDisagree / HIC challenges: London's very high house prices and inequality make it socially unsustainable for low-paid workers; high consumption and carbon footprint per person; ageing infrastructure is expensive to replace.\nLIC/NIC challenges: Mumbai's rapid growth; informal settlements lacking services; monsoon flooding; pollution of creeks and air; limited government money; large informal economy pays little tax.\nLIC/NIC opportunities: low consumption per person; high recycling rates in Dharavi; community-led schemes are cheap and effective; new infrastructure such as metro lines can be built sustainably from the start.\nCredit any well-justified conclusion.\n\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance (3 marks): Spelling and punctuation are used with consistent accuracy; meaning is controlled effectively by grammar; a wide range of specialist terms is used precisely.\nIntermediate performance (2 marks): Spelling and punctuation are used with considerable accuracy; meaning is generally controlled by grammar; a good range of specialist terms is used appropriately.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): Spelling and punctuation are used with reasonable accuracy; meaning is mostly controlled by grammar; any errors do not significantly hinder meaning; a limited range of specialist terms is used.\n0 marks: The response does not reach threshold performance, or is unrelated to the question.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.2': {
    name: 'Urban and Rural Processes and Change in the UK',
    questions: [
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nSince the 1970s many people in the UK have moved out of large cities such as London and Birmingham to live in small towns and villages.\n\nChoose the correct definition of the term 'counter-urbanisation'.\nA  The movement of people from rural areas into towns and cities\nB  The movement of people from towns and cities out to rural areas\nC  The movement of people back into the centre of a city\nD  The growth of a city outwards into its suburbs`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B  The movement of people from towns and cities out to rural areas (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nOld warehouses and mills in Ancoats, close to Manchester city centre, have been converted into apartments. Many of the people moving into them are young professionals and students.\n\nWhich term describes people moving back to live in or near a city centre?\nA  Suburbanisation\nB  Counter-urbanisation\nC  Re-urbanisation\nD  Rural depopulation`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `C  Re-urbanisation (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 1 shows the one-way distance travelled to work by seven commuters living in a village in the Vale of Glamorgan (data written for this practice question):\n12 km, 35 km, 8 km, 22 km, 41 km, 18 km, 27 km\n\nCalculate the median distance travelled to work.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `22 km (1)\n(Values in order: 8, 12, 18, 22, 27, 35, 41 — the 4th value is the median.)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nStudents counted pedestrians for five minutes at ten sites, at different distances from the centre of the CBD in Swansea, to see whether the number of shoppers falls with distance from the centre.\n\nWhich graph would be most suitable to present this data?\nA  Pie chart\nB  Scatter graph\nC  Population pyramid`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B  Scatter graph (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 2 is a table showing the services in the village of Llanfair (a village in mid Wales) in 1990 and 2020 (data written for this practice question):\nService | 1990 | 2020\nPrimary school | yes | yes\nPost office | yes | no\nBank | yes | no\nGeneral store | yes | no\nButcher | yes | no\nPub | yes | yes\nChapel | yes | yes\nPetrol station | yes | no\nBus service | 10 per day | 2 per day\n\nName one service which was still open in Llanfair in 2020.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Any one (1): primary school; pub; chapel; (a reduced) bus service.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 3 is a map of the UK showing urban areas with a population of more than 500,000. The largest is Greater London in the south-east. Others are the West Midlands (Birmingham), Greater Manchester, West Yorkshire (Leeds and Bradford), Merseyside (Liverpool), South Yorkshire (Sheffield), Tyneside (Newcastle), Nottingham, Bristol and Glasgow. Cardiff, Edinburgh and Belfast are smaller capital cities. Very few large urban areas are found in northern Scotland, central Wales or the south-west peninsula.\n\nDescribe the distribution of the UK's large urban areas shown in Figure 3.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Credit any two valid points (1 each), or one developed point (2):\n• The largest urban area (London) is in the south-east (1)\n• Many large urban areas are found in central and northern England / a belt from the Midlands to Lancashire and Yorkshire (1)\n• Several are on the coast or estuaries, e.g. Liverpool, Tyneside, Bristol, Glasgow (1)\n• Few or none in upland/remote areas such as northern Scotland, central Wales and the south-west (1)\nUse of named places from Figure 3 as evidence is expected for 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nThe village of St Dogmaels in Pembrokeshire has grown as people have moved out of nearby towns. Figure 4 shows its population (data written for this practice question):\n2001: 1,240\n2021: 1,612\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the population of the village between 2001 and 2021. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 1,612 − 1,240 = 372; 372 ÷ 1,240 × 100 (1)\nAnswer: 30% (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nA survey of new residents in villages in the Cotswolds found that most had moved from London, Birmingham or Bristol in the last ten years.\n\nGive two reasons why people move from large cities to live in rural areas.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two (1 each):\n• Quieter / less crime / less pollution and congestion than the city (1)\n• Larger houses or gardens, more space for the same price as a small city home (1)\n• Attractive countryside / scenery / better quality of life (1)\n• Improved roads and rail mean they can still commute to city jobs (1)\n• Working from home / broadband means they no longer need to live near work (1)\n• Retirement to the countryside (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 5 is a photograph of Cardiff city centre. It shows new high-rise blocks of student accommodation and city-centre apartments beside Cardiff Central railway station, with shops, cafés and offices at ground level.\n\nSuggest two reasons why the number of people living in Cardiff city centre has increased.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two (1 each):\n• Growth of universities brings students who want to live close to campus (1)\n• New apartments built on brownfield sites / old offices converted to flats (1)\n• Young professionals want to live near jobs in the city centre and avoid commuting (1)\n• Close to leisure, nightlife, shops and public transport such as the railway station (1)\n• Regeneration has made the centre more attractive and safer (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nIn Abersoch, on the Llŷn Peninsula in Gwynedd, a large share of houses are second homes or holiday lets that are empty for much of the year.\n\nDescribe two impacts of second homes on the local community.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two (1 each):\n• House prices rise so local young people cannot afford to buy (1)\n• Villages are empty in winter / loss of community spirit (1)\n• Shops, schools and bus services close as there are too few year-round residents (1)\n• Loss of Welsh-speaking families / local culture and language weakened (1)\n• Positive: visitors spend money in local shops, cafés and on building work (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 6 shows the selling prices of six houses sold in 2023 in a commuter village near Cambridge (data written for this practice question):\n£185,000  £240,000  £312,000  £425,000  £198,000  £560,000\n\nCalculate the range of house prices. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: £560,000 − £185,000 (1)\nAnswer: £375,000 (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 7 shows the percentage of all UK retail sales made online (data written for this practice question):\nYear | 2008 | 2012 | 2016 | 2019 | 2021 | 2024\nOnline sales (%) | 6 | 10 | 15 | 19 | 30 | 27\n\nDescribe the trend shown in Figure 7.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `• Overall increase in the share of online sales, from 6% in 2008 to 27% in 2024 (1)\n• Steady rise up to 2019, then a sharp jump to a peak of 30% in 2021 (1), followed by a small fall to 27% in 2024 (1)\nMax 2. Data must be used for full marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nCribbs Causeway, an out-of-town shopping centre on the edge of Bristol, opened in 1998 next to junction 17 of the M5 motorway. It has large free car parks, a covered mall and big retail warehouses.\n\nSuggest two reasons why out-of-town shopping centres like Cribbs Causeway attract shoppers.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two (1 each):\n• Large free car parks / no parking charges (1)\n• Easy access by car from the motorway / less congestion than the city centre (1)\n• Many shops under one roof / covered so shopping is not affected by weather (1)\n• Large stores with a wide range of goods and lower prices (1)\n• Leisure facilities such as food courts and cinemas make it a day out (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nStudents investigated whether Swansea city centre is in decline. They counted pedestrians for five minutes at each site on one Tuesday morning in February.\n\nSuggest two reasons why their pedestrian counts might not be reliable.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two (1 each):\n• Only one day / one time was sampled, so counts may not be typical (1)\n• Weather in February may have kept shoppers away (1)\n• Five minutes is a short period, so one group of people can distort the count (1)\n• Students at different sites may have counted differently / double-counted people (1)\n• Weekday morning would miss weekend or evening shoppers (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 8 describes two population pyramids from the 2021 Census (data written for this practice question).\nWard A, central Cardiff: very wide bars for ages 20–29 (over 30% of the population), narrow bars for children under 10 and for people over 65.\nWard B, a coastal village in Ceredigion: narrow bars for ages 20–34, widest bars for ages 60–79, and more people over 80 than Ward A.\n\nDescribe two differences between the population structures of Ward A and Ward B.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two (1 each):\n• Ward A has a much larger proportion of young adults aged 20–29 (over 30%) than Ward B (1)\n• Ward B has a larger proportion of older people aged 60–79 / an ageing population (1)\n• Ward B has more people over 80 than Ward A (1)\n• Ward A has few people over 65, Ward B many (1)\nComparative language needed for each mark.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 9 describes a proposed housing site in Cardiff: a disused railway goods yard beside the River Taff, 1 km from the city centre, with old sidings, empty sheds and a bus route along its edge.\n\nGive two advantages of building new homes on brownfield sites like this one.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two (1 each):\n• Protects greenfield land / countryside from development (1)\n• Services such as water, electricity and roads may already be nearby (1)\n• Close to jobs and services in the city centre so less commuting (1)\n• Improves the appearance of a derelict area / regenerates the area (1)\n• Close to public transport (the bus route) so fewer car journeys (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nA survey of the village of Llanfair in mid Wales (data written for this practice question) found that in 1990 the village had 9 services (primary school, post office, bank, general store, butcher, pub, chapel, petrol station and a bus service). In 2020 it had 4 of these services.\n\nCalculate the percentage of services that were lost between 1990 and 2020. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 9 − 4 = 5 services lost; 5 ÷ 9 × 100 (1)\nAnswer: 55.6% (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 10 describes BedZED (Beddington Zero Energy Development) in Hackbridge, south London, completed in 2002. It has about 100 homes built of reclaimed and local materials, thick insulation, south-facing glass, roof wind cowls for ventilation, rainwater collection, a car club and is close to Hackbridge railway station. Some flats share gardens and there is workspace on site.\n\nIdentify two features shown in Figure 10 that make BedZED a sustainable community.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Any two features with a brief link to sustainability (1 each):\n• Insulation / south-facing glass reduces energy needed for heating (1)\n• Rainwater collection reduces water use (1)\n• Reclaimed / local materials reduce resource use and transport emissions (1)\n• Car club / close to railway station reduces car use (1)\n• Workspace on site means people can work locally (1)\n• Shared gardens build community (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 11 shows where 400 working residents of a village near the M4 in south-east Wales work (data written for this practice question):\nPlace of work | Number of residents\nIn the village | 45\nCardiff | 210\nNewport | 60\nBristol | 35\nOther | 50\n\nComplete the sentences:\n"The percentage of working residents who work in Cardiff is ___ %."\n"The number of working residents who work outside the village is ___."\n"The second most common place of work is ___."`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `52.5 (%) (1)\n355 (1)\nNewport (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nThe last shop and post office in a village in the Brecon Beacons closed in 2019. A local councillor said: "Once the shop goes, the village starts to die."\n\nExplain why the closure of a village shop can lead to further decline in a rural community.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `A developed explanation, 1 mark per linked point, max 3:\n• Elderly people and those without a car can no longer buy everyday goods locally (1), so they may have to move away to a town (1)\n• The shop was a meeting place, so the community becomes less close / more isolated (1)\n• The village is less attractive to young families, so fewer move in (1), the school loses pupils and may also close (1)\n• Fewer local jobs, leading to more out-migration of young people (1)\nCredit the idea of a downward spiral / cycle of decline.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nStudents counted pedestrians for five minutes at five sites inside the CBD of Newport and at five sites at an out-of-centre retail park (data written for this practice question):\nCBD sites: 64, 82, 57, 91, 76\nRetail park sites: 12, 20, 9, 15, 19\n\nCalculate the mean pedestrian count for the CBD sites and state how much larger it is than the mean for the retail park sites. Show your working.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `CBD mean: 370 ÷ 5 = 74 (1)\nRetail park mean: 75 ÷ 5 = 15 (1)\nDifference: 74 − 15 = 59 (pedestrians per five minutes) (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nYou have carried out fieldwork to compare the quality of the environment in a regenerated area and in an area that has not been regenerated in a UK town or city.\n\nDescribe how you could use an environmental quality survey to collect primary data for this investigation.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per point, max 3:\n• Choose criteria such as litter, graffiti, noise, building condition, green space (1)\n• Use a bipolar scale, e.g. −3 to +3 or 1 to 5, for each criterion (1)\n• Score the same criteria at a number of sites in each area / systematic sampling along a transect (1)\n• Several students score each site and take an average to reduce bias (1)\n• Add up the scores to give a total for each site to compare the two areas (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 12 is a map of Wales showing population change in each local authority between 2011 and 2021 (data written for this practice question). The highest growth is in the south-east: Newport +9%, Cardiff +5% and the Vale of Glamorgan +4%. Wrexham and Flintshire in the north-east, close to Chester and Liverpool, grew by about +3%. Population fell in much of rural west and central Wales: Ceredigion −6%, Powys −1% and Blaenau Gwent in the South Wales Valleys −4%.\n\nDescribe the pattern of population change shown in Figure 12.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear description of the overall pattern with use of data from Figure 12 and named places; recognises both growth and decline and an exception/anomaly.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements, little or no use of data.\nIndicative content:\n• Growth is greatest in the south-east around Cardiff and Newport (Newport +9%)\n• Growth also in the north-east near the English border (Wrexham, Flintshire about +3%)\n• Decline in rural west and central Wales, greatest in Ceredigion (−6%)\n• Some urban areas also declined, e.g. Blaenau Gwent (−4%) in the old industrial valleys\n• Areas close to large cities / main transport routes grew; remote areas declined` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nBetween 1981 and 2021 the population of many small market towns and villages in Norfolk and Suffolk grew, while the population of inner London fell and then recovered.\n\nExplain why counter-urbanisation has taken place in the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): explains at least two reasons, with push and/or pull factors clearly linked to people's decision to move.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): lists reasons with little explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Push factors: congestion, pollution, crime and high house prices in cities\n• Pull factors: cheaper or larger houses, attractive countryside, perceived better schools and quality of life\n• Improved transport (motorways, rail links) and car ownership allow people to commute to city jobs\n• Broadband / working from home (increased after 2020) means people need to travel to work less often\n• Retired people move to rural and coastal areas\n• Firms relocating to edge-of-city business parks bring jobs closer to rural areas` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nThe population of central Manchester has grown rapidly since the 1990s, as warehouses and mills in areas such as Ancoats and Castlefield have been converted into apartments and new tower blocks have been built.\n\nExplain how re-urbanisation can change the population structure of a city centre.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of how re-urbanisation changes age structure and/or other characteristics, with reasons.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple description of change with limited explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Large increase in young adults (18–35) as students and young professionals are attracted by jobs, universities and nightlife\n• Few children because most new homes are small flats with no gardens, so families stay in the suburbs\n• Few elderly people\n• More single-person households and renters\n• Can be more ethnically diverse as international students and migrants move in\n• Leads to 'studentification' in some areas` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nIn 2023, councils in Wales were allowed to charge much higher council tax on second homes. In Gwynedd, which includes Abersoch and the Llŷn Peninsula, the council chose to charge second-home owners a large premium.\n\nGive two reasons why second homes cause tension in rural communities such as those in Gwynedd.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Two reasons, each 1 mark for the reason + 1 mark for development (2 × 2):\n• Second-home owners can pay more than local people (1), so house prices rise and young local people are priced out of the area (1)\n• Homes are empty for much of the year (1), so village shops, pubs, schools and buses lose customers and close (1)\n• Local families move away (1), weakening the Welsh language and culture in communities where it is strong (1)\n• Holiday visitors bring traffic and noise in summer (1), while the village is quiet / 'dead' in winter (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nCottenham, a village about 10 km north of Cambridge, has grown as new housing estates have been built for people who work in Cambridge.\n\nExplain the impacts of commuters on a village like Cottenham.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): explains both positive and negative impacts, or explains two impacts in detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements of impact.\nIndicative content:\nPositive\n• Population growth keeps the primary school and some services open\n• Higher-income residents spend money in local businesses and improve houses\nNegative\n• House prices rise, so local young people cannot afford homes\n• Commuters shop and socialise in Cambridge, so village shops may still lose trade\n• Village is quiet during the day ('dormitory village')\n• Rush-hour traffic congestion and parking problems on narrow roads\n• Loss of greenfield land to new estates; tension between newcomers and long-term residents` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nDescribe how shopping in the city centre of a UK city you have studied has changed. Name the city.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): a clear, detailed description of changes, with accurate place-specific detail for the named city.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): generic description with little place detail.\nIndicative content (e.g. Cardiff):\n• Building of the St David's shopping centre (extended in 2009) with large chain stores under one roof\n• Pedestrianisation of Queen Street\n• Closure of department stores (e.g. Debenhams in 2021) and some chain stores; more empty units\n• Rise of cafés, restaurants, bars and leisure — the 'evening economy'\n• Independent shops in the Victorian arcades\n• Competition from out-of-town retail parks and online shopping\nCredit other named UK cities (e.g. Sheffield and Meadowhall, Manchester and the Trafford Centre). No named city: max Level 1.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nOn many UK high streets there are now more empty shops, charity shops and cafés than there were twenty years ago, while parcel delivery vans are a common sight in residential streets.\n\nExplain why the growth of online shopping has affected high streets in UK towns.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation linking online shopping to at least two effects on high streets.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements.\nIndicative content:\n• Online retailers have lower costs (no shop rents or business rates), so they can offer lower prices\n• Shoppers find it convenient — 24 hours, home delivery, easy price comparison\n• Fewer customers in town centres, so sales fall and shops close (e.g. chain stores going into administration)\n• Empty units make the high street less attractive, causing a spiral of decline\n• Shops that remain change use — cafés, services, leisure, click-and-collect points\n• The pandemic lockdowns in 2020–21 sped up the change` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 13 describes Cardiff Bay before and after regeneration. Before (1980s): derelict docks and warehouses left after the coal trade ended, mudflats exposed at low tide, high unemployment in nearby Butetown. After (2020s): a barrage (completed in 1999) holds back a freshwater lake; the Senedd (Welsh Parliament), the Wales Millennium Centre, Mermaid Quay with restaurants and shops, waterfront apartments, offices and a new road link to the city centre.\n\nDescribe how Cardiff Bay has been regenerated. Use evidence from Figure 13.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear description of several changes with evidence from Figure 13; may note the role of the development corporation.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple list of features.\nIndicative content:\n• Barrage built to create a freshwater lake, covering the mudflats and making the waterfront attractive\n• New government building (Senedd) and arts/culture centre (Wales Millennium Centre) bring visitors and jobs\n• Leisure and retail at Mermaid Quay — tourism-led regeneration\n• Derelict docks replaced by apartments and offices\n• Improved road link to the city centre\n• Led by the Cardiff Bay Development Corporation (1987–2000) using public money to attract private investment` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nThe London Docklands Development Corporation regenerated the old docks of east London between 1981 and 1998. Canary Wharf became a major financial district, and the Docklands Light Railway opened in 1987.\n\nExplain why not all local people benefited from the regeneration of the London Docklands.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of at least two reasons with linked consequences.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements.\nIndicative content:\n• Many new jobs were in finance and offices; former dock workers lacked the skills and qualifications needed\n• Many jobs went to commuters from outside the area\n• Expensive new housing and rising house prices priced out local families; little affordable housing at first\n• Traditional community and corner shops were lost; locals felt the scheme was planned without them\n• Some benefited later from improved transport (DLR, Jubilee Line extension 1999) and some new services` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 14 shows changes at Salford Quays, Greater Manchester, after the docks closed in 1982 (data written for this practice question):\nIndicator | 1985 | 2021\nPeople employed in the area | 3,000 | 26,000\nResidents | 1,200 | 13,500\nAverage house price | £28,000 | £210,000\nArea of derelict land (hectares) | 90 | 5\nThe BBC moved to MediaCityUK at Salford Quays in 2011.\n\nAnalyse the changes at Salford Quays shown in Figure 14.`, marks: 4, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): uses data accurately (including manipulation, e.g. the number of jobs increased more than eightfold) and makes links between the changes.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): lifts data with limited comment.\nIndicative content:\n• Jobs increased by 23,000, over eight times as many, linked to media firms at MediaCityUK and offices\n• Residents rose by 12,300, over eleven times as many, as apartments were built\n• House prices rose by £182,000, about 7.5 times, suggesting the area became desirable — possibly unaffordable for local people\n• Derelict land fell from 90 ha to 5 ha, showing most land has been redeveloped\n• Links: more jobs attracted more residents, which increased demand for housing and pushed up prices` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nThe UK government wants new housing developments to be 'sustainable communities' where people can live, work and travel without damaging the environment for future generations.\n\nExplain how one strategy can make an urban community more sustainable.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): one strategy explained in detail with social, economic and/or environmental benefits clearly linked; may use a named example.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): strategy identified with limited explanation.\nIndicative content (one strategy):\n• Energy-efficient design (insulation, solar panels, heat pumps) — lower bills and lower carbon emissions (e.g. BedZED)\n• Integrated public transport, cycle lanes and car clubs — fewer car journeys, less congestion and pollution\n• Mixed-use development with homes, shops and workspaces close together — less travel, local jobs\n• Affordable housing in the mix — keeps a balanced community\n• Green spaces and sustainable drainage — wellbeing, biodiversity, reduced flood risk\n• Building on brownfield land — protects the countryside` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nThis question is about your own fieldwork investigating urban or rural change in the UK.\n\nEvaluate one strength and one weakness of the method you used to collect your primary data.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): a strength and a weakness, each explained with clear reference to the student's own method and its effect on the reliability or accuracy of the data.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): a generic strength and/or weakness with little explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Strength: large sample of questionnaires / several sites gave representative results; group averages reduced bias in an environmental quality survey; same method at each site allowed fair comparison\n• Weakness: only one day/time sampled; subjective scoring; small sample; people refused to answer; counts affected by weather or events\n• Must be linked to the effect on results/conclusions` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 15 compares two wards from the 2021 Census (data written for this practice question):\nIndicator | Ward A: central Cardiff | Ward B: village in Ceredigion\nAged 20–29 (%) | 31 | 8\nAged 65 and over (%) | 6 | 29\nHouseholds with no car (%) | 54 | 9\nHomes that are second homes (%) | 1 | 14\nResidents who speak Welsh (%) | 11 | 47\n\nChoose the four correct statements.\nA  Ward A has almost four times the percentage of people aged 20–29 as Ward B.\nB  Ward B has a younger population than Ward A.\nC  More than half of households in Ward A have no car.\nD  Ward B has fourteen times the percentage of second homes of Ward A.\nE  Ward A has a higher percentage of Welsh speakers than Ward B.\nF  The percentage of people aged 65 and over in Ward B is 23 percentage points higher than in Ward A.`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `A (31 ÷ 8 ≈ 3.9) (1)\nC (54%) (1)\nD (14 ÷ 1 = 14) (1)\nF (29 − 6 = 23) (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFarms in the Yorkshire Dales have opened campsites, holiday cottages, farm shops and wedding venues. Some barns have been converted into homes.\n\nExplain why many UK farms have diversified into tourism and leisure.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of at least two reasons linked to changes in the rural economy.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements.\nIndicative content:\n• Low and unreliable incomes from farming (low prices for milk and lamb, rising costs)\n• Changes to farm subsidies mean farmers need other income\n• Fewer farm workers needed because of mechanisation, so buildings and land are unused\n• Growing demand from city dwellers for countryside leisure, short breaks and 'staycations'\n• Attractive landscape of a National Park draws visitors; diversification creates local jobs and keeps families on the land` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nBetween 2011 and 2021, villages within commuting distance of Cardiff and Bristol grew, while many remote villages in mid Wales and the Scottish Highlands lost population.\n\nExplain why some rural areas in the UK are growing while others are declining.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 2, AO2 4\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): detailed explanation of reasons for both growth and decline, with clear links between accessibility, jobs, housing and services; uses named examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): explanation of reasons for growth and/or decline, some links made; may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple reasons, little explanation.\nIndicative content:\nGrowing rural areas\n• Accessible by motorway and rail (e.g. the M4 corridor) so commuters can reach city jobs — counter-urbanisation\n• Attractive to retirees and families; new housing estates built\n• Home working since 2020 widens the area people can live in\nDeclining rural areas\n• Remote, poor transport and broadband; few jobs beyond farming and seasonal tourism\n• Young people leave for university and jobs in cities (rural depopulation)\n• Services close, which makes the area less attractive (cycle of decline)\n• Second homes push up prices so young locals cannot stay` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nMany UK cities lost industries such as docks, steelworks and textile mills in the late twentieth century, leaving derelict land and high unemployment.\n\nExplain how an urban area in the UK you have studied has been regenerated. Name the urban area.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 3, AO2 3\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): detailed, accurate, place-specific explanation of how the area was regenerated and why the strategies were needed, with economic, social and environmental strands.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): some explanation with some place detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): generic description; little explanation; may not name an area.\nIndicative content (e.g. London Docklands):\n• Docks closed between the 1960s and 1981 as larger container ships used Tilbury, leaving derelict land\n• LDDC (1981–98) bought land, gave tax breaks in the Isle of Dogs Enterprise Zone to attract firms, e.g. Canary Wharf financial district\n• Transport: DLR (1987), London City Airport (1987), Jubilee Line extension (1999)\n• Housing: converted warehouses and new homes, some affordable housing later\n• Environment: cleaned-up docks, water sports, parks\nCredit Cardiff Bay, Salford Quays, Liverpool Docks, Glasgow Clyde or other named UK examples. No named area: max Level 1.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 16 gives two views on out-of-town shopping centres such as Meadowhall, which opened in 1990 on the site of a former steelworks beside the M1 near Sheffield.\nView 1 (shop owner in Sheffield city centre): "Meadowhall took our customers. Many shops on our high street closed in the 1990s."\nView 2 (Meadowhall manager): "We created thousands of jobs on land that had been derelict, and shoppers have free parking and everything under one roof."\n\n"Out-of-town shopping centres do more harm than good." To what extent do you agree? Justify your views.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4, AO3 4\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): a well-balanced evaluation of harm and benefits — economic, social and environmental — using the views in Figure 16 and wider knowledge; reaches a clear, justified conclusion about the extent of agreement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): some evaluation of both sides; conclusion stated but only partly justified.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): one-sided or simple points; no clear conclusion.\nIndicative content:\nHarm\n• Trade drawn away from city centres; shop closures and empty units in the CBD (Sheffield's 'Meadowhell' effect in the 1990s)\n• Encourages car use — congestion near motorway junctions, air pollution, carbon emissions\n• Disadvantages people without cars (elderly, low-income)\n• Greenfield sites lost at some centres\nGood\n• Jobs and regeneration of brownfield land (former steelworks)\n• Convenience, free parking, covered, leisure facilities\n• Some served by tram/rail (Meadowhall Interchange)\n• Pressure on city centres to improve, e.g. Sheffield's later city-centre regeneration\n• Online shopping now harms both, so out-of-town centres may not be the main cause of decline` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nThe UK needs many more homes. Figure 17 describes a proposal to build 3,000 new homes on farmland in the green belt on the edge of a UK city. The site is next to a motorway junction and 8 km from the city centre. Supporters say homes are needed for young families; opponents want the homes built on brownfield sites in the inner city instead.\n\nShould new housing be built on greenfield sites on the edge of UK cities? Justify your views. You may use examples you have studied.\n[8 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4, AO3 4\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): a balanced evaluation of greenfield and brownfield development considering economic, social and environmental factors; uses Figure 17 and named examples; reaches a well-justified decision.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): some evaluation of both sides with a partly justified view.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple, one-sided points; decision unsupported.\nIndicative content:\nFor greenfield\n• Cheaper and quicker to build — no clearing or decontamination\n• Space for larger homes with gardens, attractive to families\n• Good road access (motorway junction)\n• Housing shortage and rising prices need large sites quickly\nAgainst greenfield / for brownfield\n• Loss of farmland, habitats and green belt; urban sprawl\n• Increases car commuting, congestion and emissions; new services needed\n• Brownfield sites regenerate inner cities, use existing services and public transport (e.g. Cardiff's former goods yards, Salford Quays)\n• Brownfield may be contaminated and more expensive\nDecision may be either way, or 'it depends', if justified.\n\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance (3 marks): spells and punctuates with consistent accuracy; uses rules of grammar with effective control of meaning overall; uses a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nIntermediate performance (2 marks): spells and punctuates with considerable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with general control of meaning overall; uses a good range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): spells and punctuates with reasonable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with some control of meaning and any errors do not significantly hinder meaning overall; uses a limited range of specialist terms as appropriate.\n0 marks: the learner writes nothing, or the response does not relate to the question, or errors severely hinder meaning.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nFigure 18 gives views of three people living in a village in the Peak District that has grown through counter-urbanisation (data written for this practice question).\nRetired newcomer: "We moved from Manchester for the peace and the views. We volunteer at the village hall."\nLocal farmer's son, aged 24: "I can't afford a house here. Prices have doubled since newcomers arrived."\nPub landlord: "The new residents keep my business going, but most of them drive to the supermarket in town."\n\n"Counter-urbanisation brings more benefits than problems to rural areas in the UK." To what extent do you agree? Justify your views.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4, AO3 4\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): balanced, well-developed evaluation of benefits and problems (economic, social, environmental), using the views in Figure 18 and wider knowledge; considers that impacts vary between places and groups; clear, justified conclusion.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): some evaluation of benefits and problems; conclusion partly justified.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple, one-sided points; no clear conclusion.\nIndicative content:\nBenefits\n• Population growth supports pubs, schools and community groups; newcomers volunteer\n• Wealthier residents spend money locally and renovate old buildings\n• Can reverse rural depopulation\nProblems\n• House prices rise — young locals priced out; second homes leave villages empty\n• Newcomers commute and shop in towns, so village shops and buses still decline\n• Ageing population if retirees move in; more pressure on health care\n• Traffic, loss of greenfield land to new estates; tension between newcomers and locals; dilution of local culture (e.g. Welsh language in Gwynedd)\n• Benefits depend on the place: accessible commuter villages gain more than remote areas` }
    ]
  },
  '1.3': {
    name: 'A Global Perspective on Development Issues',
    questions: [
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Geographers use a range of economic and social indicators to measure the development of a country.\n\nChoose the correct definition of the term 'Gross National Income (GNI) per capita'.\nA  The number of people living in a country divided by its area\nB  The total income earned by a country's people and businesses in a year, divided by its population\nC  The amount of money a country earns from its exports in a year\nD  The average number of years a person can expect to live`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nTable 1 (data written for this practice question): development indicators for six countries.\nCountry | GNI per capita (US$) | Life expectancy (years) | HDI\nNorway | 94,000 | 83 | 0.966\nUnited Kingdom | 49,000 | 81 | 0.940\nBrazil | 9,000 | 74 | 0.760\nIndia | 2,400 | 68 | 0.644\nMalawi | 600 | 63 | 0.508\nNiger | 600 | 62 | 0.394\n\nUsing Table 1, calculate the range in life expectancy between the six countries.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `21 years (83 − 62) (1)\nUnits not required.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Figure 1.2 is a world map showing the Brandt Line, drawn in 1980. The line runs south of the USA and Mexico border, across the North Atlantic, south of Europe and the Mediterranean, through the Middle East, north of India and China, then loops south-east around the Pacific so that Australia and New Zealand lie to its 'north'.\n\nWhich one of the following statements about the Brandt Line is correct?\nA  All countries south of the line were richer than countries north of it in 1980\nB  It divided the world into the richer 'Global North' and the poorer 'Global South'\nC  It shows the location of the world's largest cities\nD  It has been redrawn every year since 1980`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nTable 1.3 (data written for this practice question): percentage of workers employed in each sector of the economy.\nCountry | Primary (%) | Secondary (%) | Tertiary (%)\nMalawi | 76 | 7 | 17\nIndia | 44 | 25 | 31\nUnited Kingdom | 1 | 18 | 81\n\nIdentify the country in Table 1.3 which has the highest percentage of workers in secondary industry.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `India (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. India is often described as a newly industrialised country (NIC). Its GNI per capita rose from US$1,180 in 2010 to US$2,390 in 2022 (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage change in India's GNI per capita between 2010 and 2022. Show your working. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: (2,390 − 1,180) ÷ 1,180 × 100 (1)\n= 102.5% increase (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Accept 102.54%.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nTable 1 (data written for this practice question):\nCountry | GNI per capita (US$) | HDI\nMalawi | 600 | 0.508\nNiger | 600 | 0.394\n\nMalawi and Niger have the same GNI per capita but different HDI values. Suggest one reason why the Human Development Index (HDI) is a better measure of development than GNI per capita.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• HDI combines several indicators — life expectancy, education (years of schooling) and income (1) so it shows quality of life / social development, not just wealth (1).\n• GNI is an average (1) so it hides how money is shared out / does not show whether people are healthy or educated (1); in Table 1 the two countries have the same income but Niger has lower HDI, so people's lives differ (1).\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Figure 1.4 is a map of Africa. Malawi is shown as a narrow, landlocked country in south-east Africa, about 900 km long from north to south. Lake Malawi runs along most of its eastern border. It is bordered by Tanzania to the north-east, Mozambique to the east, south and south-west and Zambia to the west. The Indian Ocean coast is about 250 km to the east.\n\nDescribe the location of Malawi.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from (1 each):\n• In south-east / southern Africa\n• Landlocked / has no coastline\n• Borders Tanzania (north/north-east), Mozambique (east/south), Zambia (west) — any one or two named neighbours with direction\n• Lake Malawi along its eastern side\n• About 250 km west of the Indian Ocean\nMax 2. Simply listing countries without direction = 1 max.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. A student wants to show the HDI of every country in Africa on a map. They plan to use a choropleth map with five shading classes (0.300–0.399, 0.400–0.499, 0.500–0.599, 0.600–0.699, 0.700–0.799).\n\nGive two reasons why a choropleth map is an appropriate technique to show this information.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from (1 each):\n• HDI data is collected for areas / countries, which can be shaded\n• Shows the spatial pattern clearly / regional differences can be seen at a glance\n• Data can be grouped into classes / ranges\n• Darker-to-lighter shading makes high and low values easy to compare\n• Easy to see clusters / anomalies\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Many LICs depend on exporting one primary product. Table 1.5 shows the world price of cocoa (data written for this practice question).\nYear | 2019 | 2020 | 2021 | 2022 | 2023\nPrice (US$ per tonne) | 2,340 | 2,370 | 2,430 | 2,390 | 3,280\n\nCalculate the mean price of cocoa between 2019 and 2023. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: (2,340 + 2,370 + 2,430 + 2,390 + 3,280) = 12,810 ÷ 5 (1)\n= US$2,562 per tonne (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Transnational corporations (TNCs) such as Unilever and Coca-Cola have opened factories and offices in India.\n\nSuggest one way that a TNC can benefit the host country where it locates.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a benefit, 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• Creates jobs (1) giving people a regular wage so they can afford better housing / education / healthcare (1)\n• Brings investment / builds infrastructure such as roads or power supplies (1) which can be used by local people and other businesses (1)\n• Pays taxes to the government (1) which can be spent on schools / hospitals (1)\n• Training / transfer of skills and technology (1) so local workers can find better-paid jobs (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Some aid is 'tied aid'. This means that the receiving country must agree to conditions, for example spending the money on goods or services from the donor country.\n\nDescribe one disadvantage of tied aid for the receiving country.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a disadvantage, 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• Must buy goods/services from the donor (1) which may be more expensive than buying elsewhere, so the aid buys less (1)\n• Money is spent on projects that suit the donor (1) rather than what local people most need, e.g. a large dam instead of clean water in villages (1)\n• Local firms do not benefit (1) because contracts go to companies from the donor country (1)\n• Can increase dependency / political influence of the donor (1) as the country must follow the donor's conditions (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Kuapa Kokoo is a Fairtrade cocoa farmers' co-operative in Ghana. In one year the co-operative sold 2,500 tonnes of cocoa on Fairtrade terms and received a Fairtrade Premium of US$240 per tonne (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the total Fairtrade Premium the co-operative received that year. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 2,500 × 240 (1)\n= US$600,000 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Figure 1.6 is a photograph of a simple hand-operated water pump in a village in rural Malawi. A woman is pumping water into a plastic container; the pump is made of steel pipe set into a concrete base.\n\nGive one reason why this is an example of appropriate technology.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• Cheap to build / uses local materials (1) so the village can afford it without large loans (1)\n• Easy to repair / maintain using local skills (1) so it keeps working without outside experts or spare parts from abroad (1)\n• Does not need electricity or fuel (1) which is not available / too expensive in rural areas (1)\n• Meets a basic need — clean water (1) reducing water-borne disease such as cholera (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. In 2005 the G8 group of countries agreed to cancel the debts that some of the world's poorest countries owed to the World Bank and the IMF. Zambia had around US$4 billion of debt cancelled.\n\nSuggest one way debt cancellation can help an LIC to develop.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a way, 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• Money no longer spent on repayments / interest (1) can be spent on schools, healthcare or infrastructure (1)\n• e.g. after debt cancellation Zambia was able to abolish fees for rural health care (1) improving access for poor people (1)\n• Government is less dependent on export earnings to pay debt (1) so it can invest in new industries (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Figure 1.7 is a line graph of the world price of coffee between 2014 and 2024 (US cents per pound). The price falls from about 220 cents in 2014 to a low of about 100 cents in 2019, rises to about 250 cents in early 2022, falls to about 160 cents in 2023 and then rises sharply to about 320 cents by the end of 2024.\n\nDescribe one problem for coffee farmers in LICs caused by the changes shown in Figure 1.7.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a problem, 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• Prices fluctuate / are unpredictable (1) so farmers cannot plan or know how much they will earn (1)\n• When prices fall (e.g. to about 100 cents in 2019) farmers may earn less than it costs to grow coffee (1) so they fall into debt / cannot pay school fees (1)\n• Cannot afford to invest in new equipment or fertiliser (1) as income may drop the next year (1)\nUse of data from the figure can credit the development. Max 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nTable 1.3 (data written for this practice question): percentage of workers employed in each sector of the economy.\nCountry | Primary (%) | Secondary (%) | Tertiary (%)\nMalawi | 76 | 7 | 17\nIndia | 44 | 25 | 31\nUnited Kingdom | 1 | 18 | 81\n\nChoose the correct answer in each statement:\nThe country with the largest percentage of workers in tertiary jobs is (Malawi / India / United Kingdom).\nThe difference between the percentage of primary workers in Malawi and India is (22 / 32 / 42) %.`, marks: 2, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `United Kingdom (1)\n32 (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Figure 1.8 shows the price of a £1.20 milk chocolate bar sold in the UK and who receives each part of it (data written for this practice question): retailer (shop) 42p, chocolate manufacturer 40p, VAT and taxes 20p, cocoa traders and processors 10p, cocoa farmer 8p.\n\nCalculate the percentage of the price of the bar that is received by the cocoa farmer. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 8 ÷ 120 × 100 (1)\n= 6.7% (1)\nAccept 6.67%. Correct answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nTable 1 (data written for this practice question): development indicators for six countries.\nCountry | GNI per capita (US$) | Life expectancy (years) | HDI\nNorway | 94,000 | 83 | 0.966\nUnited Kingdom | 49,000 | 81 | 0.940\nBrazil | 9,000 | 74 | 0.760\nIndia | 2,400 | 68 | 0.644\nMalawi | 600 | 63 | 0.508\nNiger | 600 | 62 | 0.394\n\nComplete the sentences:\n"The difference in HDI between Norway and Niger is ___."\n"The country with a life expectancy of 74 years is ___."\n"The United Kingdom's GNI per capita is about ___ times larger than India's (to the nearest whole number)."`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `0.572 (1)\nBrazil (1)\n20 (49,000 ÷ 2,400 = 20.4) (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Figure 1.9 is a world map showing HDI by country. Very high HDI (0.800 and above) covers North America, Europe, Russia, Australia, New Zealand, Japan, South Korea, Saudi Arabia and parts of South America (Argentina, Chile). High HDI (0.700–0.799) covers most of South America, China, Mexico and North Africa's Mediterranean coast. Medium HDI (0.550–0.699) covers India, South-East Asia and southern Africa. Low HDI (below 0.550) is found almost entirely in Sub-Saharan Africa (for example Niger, Chad, Mali, South Sudan, Somalia), with Afghanistan and Yemen in Asia.\n\nDescribe the global pattern of HDI shown in Figure 1.9.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any three from (1 each), credit use of named places / data:\n• Highest HDI mostly in the northern hemisphere / North America and Europe\n• Exceptions in the south, e.g. Australia and New Zealand very high\n• Lowest HDI concentrated in Sub-Saharan Africa, e.g. Niger, Chad\n• Few low HDI countries outside Africa, e.g. Afghanistan, Yemen\n• Medium HDI in South Asia / India and South-East Asia\n• South America and China mainly high HDI\nMax 3. Listing countries without a pattern = max 1.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. India has one of the world's fastest-growing economies, but in 2022 more than one in ten of its people still lived in extreme poverty.\n\nExplain why using GNI per capita on its own can give a misleading picture of development in a country such as India.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Point-marked, 1 mark per valid point or development, max 3, e.g.\n• GNI per capita is an average (1) so it hides inequality between rich and poor (1), e.g. wealthy city workers in Mumbai compared with rural farmers (1)\n• Does not include the informal economy / subsistence farming (1) so real income may be under-estimated (1)\n• Measures money only (1) and says nothing about health, education or access to clean water (1)\n• Does not take account of cost of living (1) — US$ figures may not reflect what money buys locally (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Figure 1.10 is a scatter graph of GNI per capita (x-axis, US$, 0–100,000) against life expectancy (y-axis, years, 50–85) for 30 countries. Countries with GNI per capita below US$5,000 have life expectancies from about 52 to 72 years. Countries above US$20,000 are all between 76 and 84 years. A best-fit curve rises steeply at first and then levels off above about US$20,000.\n\nDescribe the relationship shown in Figure 1.10.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any three from (1 each):\n• Positive relationship / correlation — as GNI per capita increases, life expectancy increases\n• Relationship is strongest / steepest at low incomes\n• Levels off above about US$20,000 — extra income adds little extra life expectancy\n• Wide spread at low incomes (about 52–72 years) — some poor countries have quite high life expectancy\n• Little spread among rich countries (76–84 years)\nUse of data credited. Max 3.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Figure 1.11 is a world map of GNI per capita (US$). Over US$40,000: USA, Canada, Western and Northern Europe, Australia, Japan, the Gulf states (Qatar, UAE). US$12,000–40,000: Russia, Eastern Europe, Saudi Arabia, Chile, Argentina, South Korea. US$4,000–12,000: China, Brazil, Mexico, South Africa, Turkey. US$1,100–4,000: India, Pakistan, Nigeria, Egypt, Bolivia. Below US$1,100: most of central and eastern Africa (for example Ethiopia, Malawi, Niger, DR Congo), plus Afghanistan.\n\nDescribe the global pattern of GNI per capita shown in Figure 1.11.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear description of the overall pattern with named examples and/or data; recognises anomalies.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic statements, may list countries with little sense of pattern.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Richest countries mainly in North America, Western Europe, plus Australia and Japan\n• Poorest concentrated in Sub-Saharan Africa, e.g. Ethiopia, Malawi below US$1,100\n• Middle-income band across Asia and South America, e.g. China and Brazil US$4,000–12,000\n• Anomalies: Gulf states very rich due to oil; Afghanistan in the poorest group outside Africa\n• Broadly a north–south divide but with exceptions` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. More than half of Malawi's export earnings come from just one crop, tobacco, which is sold unprocessed.\n\nGive two reasons why relying on exporting primary products can slow a country's development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a reason + 1 mark for development):\n• Primary products have low value (1) — the profit from processing them (e.g. into cigarettes or chocolate) is made in HICs (1)\n• Prices on the world market fluctuate (1) so income is unpredictable and governments cannot plan spending (1)\n• Relying on one crop is risky (1) — drought, disease or falling demand (e.g. fewer people smoking) can cut export earnings sharply (1)\n• Trade barriers / tariffs in HICs on processed goods (1) discourage LICs from setting up their own factories (1)\n• Little secondary industry develops (1) so few skilled or better-paid jobs are created (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Global trade is dominated by HICs and a small number of NICs. LICs in Sub-Saharan Africa account for less than 3% of world trade.\n\nExplain why patterns of world trade can widen the development gap.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation with linked reasons showing how trade causes the gap to grow.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements with limited explanation.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• HICs export high-value manufactured goods / services, LICs export low-value raw materials\n• LICs import expensive manufactured goods, creating a trade deficit and possible debt\n• Tariffs and quotas protect HIC markets; subsidies to HIC farmers make LIC crops less competitive\n• Trade blocs (e.g. the EU) favour members\n• Profits from processing stay in HICs so HICs grow richer faster` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. TNCs from many countries have invested in India, especially in cities such as Bengaluru (Bangalore), Mumbai and Pune.\n\nExplain why TNCs choose to locate factories and offices in an NIC such as India.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): two or more reasons clearly explained, with reference to India.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): reasons listed with little explanation.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Lower labour costs — wages much lower than in the UK or USA, so higher profits\n• Large, young, well-educated, English-speaking workforce, e.g. IT graduates in Bengaluru, suits call centres and software work\n• Huge and growing domestic market of over 1.4 billion people / growing middle class\n• Government incentives — tax breaks, special economic zones, relaxed rules on foreign investment\n• Improving infrastructure, ports and internet connections\n• Time difference allows 24-hour working with HIC offices` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Some LICs borrowed large amounts of money in the 1970s and 1980s. When interest rates rose, many could not pay back their loans.\n\nExplain why debt can slow development in an LIC.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, linked explanation of the effects of debt on development.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Large share of government income spent on repayments and interest rather than health, education or infrastructure\n• Interest builds up so the debt grows — 'debt trap' / cycle of borrowing to repay\n• Countries may be pushed to grow cash crops for export instead of food, risking food shortages\n• Loan conditions (e.g. structural adjustment) may require cuts to public spending or selling state industries\n• Less money to invest in new industry, so the economy stays dependent on primary exports` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Globalisation has led many manufacturing companies to move production from the UK to countries in Asia. For example, steelmaking and textile jobs in parts of South Wales and northern England have declined since the 1980s.\n\nGive two reasons why globalisation can create 'losers' in HICs such as the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a reason + 1 mark for development):\n• Factories close as TNCs move production to places with cheaper labour (1) causing unemployment in old industrial areas (1)\n• Cheap imports compete with UK-made goods (1) forcing UK firms to cut jobs or close (1)\n• Workers with skills in manufacturing may not have the skills needed for new service jobs (1) so long-term unemployment / low-paid work increases (1)\n• Closures have a multiplier effect (1) — local shops and services lose customers and the area declines (1)\nMax 4.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Kuapa Kokoo is a co-operative of cocoa farmers in Ghana that sells its cocoa on Fairtrade terms. Its members also own part of the UK company Divine Chocolate.\n\nExplain how fair trade can help to reduce inequality for farmers in LICs. You may use Kuapa Kokoo or another example you have studied.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of how fair trade benefits farmers, linked to reducing inequality; uses an example.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic statements about fair trade.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Fairtrade Minimum Price guarantees farmers do not get less than the cost of production, even when world prices fall\n• Fairtrade Premium paid to the co-operative is spent on community projects, e.g. wells, schools, health clinics in Kuapa Kokoo villages\n• Owning a share of Divine Chocolate means farmers receive some of the profit made from processing\n• Co-operatives give farmers more bargaining power and training\n• Rules on working conditions, no child labour, environmental standards` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. In 2023 the UK government spent about £15 billion on overseas aid. Countries that give aid are called 'donor countries'.\n\nDescribe two advantages for a donor country of giving aid to an LIC.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for an advantage + 1 mark for development):\n• Builds trade links / new markets (1) — the LIC may later buy goods from the donor (1)\n• Political influence / alliances (1) — the receiving country may support the donor internationally (1)\n• Tied aid creates work for donor companies (1), e.g. engineering firms building projects (1)\n• Access to resources such as oil or minerals (1) through good relations (1)\n• Improves global security / reduces migration pressure (1) by reducing poverty and conflict (1)\n• Improves the donor's international reputation (1)\nMax 4.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. WaterAid works with communities in Malawi to build wells and toilets and trains local people to maintain them. After a disaster such as Cyclone Freddy (2023), countries also send short-term emergency aid such as food, tents and medicine.\n\nExplain why long-term development aid can be more sustainable than short-term emergency aid.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear comparison explaining why long-term aid brings lasting benefits.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements about one type of aid.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Long-term aid tackles causes of poverty (lack of clean water, education, skills), emergency aid only meets immediate needs\n• Communities are involved and trained so projects keep working after the donor leaves\n• Reduces dependency — people can support themselves\n• Benefits future generations, e.g. healthier children who attend school\n• Emergency aid is still vital to save lives but goods are used up quickly` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Practical Action, a UK charity, helps rural communities in countries such as Nepal, Kenya and Bangladesh to build small-scale micro-hydro schemes, solar lights and floating vegetable gardens.\n\nExplain the advantages of using appropriate technology to reduce poverty in rural areas of LICs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of two or more advantages linked to local needs.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Low cost, so affordable for communities without large loans or debt\n• Uses local materials and skills; easy to repair so it keeps working\n• Meets basic needs: electricity for lighting, clean water, more food\n• Benefits go directly to local people (bottom-up) rather than to cities or businesses\n• Usually environmentally sustainable, e.g. micro-hydro and solar produce no emissions\n• Creates local jobs / builds skills` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nTable 1.12 (data written for this practice question): development indicators.\nIndicator | Malawi | India | United Kingdom\nInfant mortality (per 1,000 live births) | 29 | 26 | 4\nAdult literacy (%) | 68 | 76 | 99\nAccess to electricity (% of people) | 15 | 99 | 100\nDoctors per 10,000 people | 0.5 | 7 | 32\n\nChoose the correct answer in each statement:\nThe country with the highest infant mortality is (Malawi / India / United Kingdom).\nThe difference in adult literacy between Malawi and the United Kingdom is (21 / 31 / 41) %.\nThe indicator with the greatest difference between Malawi and India is (infant mortality / adult literacy / access to electricity).\nThe UK has about (4 / 64 / 320) times as many doctors per 10,000 people as Malawi.`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `Malawi (1)\n31 (1)\naccess to electricity (1)\n64 (32 ÷ 0.5 = 64) (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. India's economy has grown rapidly since the government opened it to foreign investment in 1991. Many people have moved from rural villages to cities such as Mumbai and Bengaluru.\n\nDescribe how economic development has affected the quality of life of people in an LIC or NIC you have studied. Name the country.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): accurate, located description of positive and/or negative effects, with specific detail of the named country.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): generic statements that could apply anywhere; country may not be named.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content (India):\n• Rising incomes and a growing middle class; more people can afford cars, phones, better housing\n• More jobs in manufacturing and services (IT, call centres), better-paid than farming\n• Improved access to electricity, education and healthcare; falling infant mortality\n• Hundreds of millions lifted out of extreme poverty\n• But inequality has grown between urban and rural areas and between states\n• Rapid urban growth has led to slums, e.g. Dharavi in Mumbai, and air pollution in Delhi\nCredit other valid LIC/NIC case studies.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies.\nTable 1 (data written for this practice question): development indicators.\nCountry | GNI per capita (US$) | Life expectancy (years) | HDI\nUnited Kingdom | 49,000 | 81 | 0.940\nIndia | 2,400 | 68 | 0.644\nMalawi | 600 | 63 | 0.508\n\nCompare the level of development of India with the United Kingdom and Malawi. Use data from the table in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear comparative statements using data for both comparisons; may manipulate data.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): lifts data with little comparison, or compares with only one country.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• India is between Malawi and the UK on all three indicators / in the middle of the development continuum\n• GNI per capita: India's US$2,400 is four times Malawi's (US$600) but about one-twentieth of the UK's (US$49,000)\n• Life expectancy: India 5 years higher than Malawi but 13 years lower than the UK\n• HDI: India 0.136 higher than Malawi, 0.296 lower than the UK — closer to Malawi than to the UK\n• Development gap with the UK is larger than with Malawi` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Globalisation is the process by which the world's countries are becoming more connected through trade, investment, communications and the movement of people.\n\nExplain how improvements in transport and communications have increased globalisation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of how two or more developments increase global connections.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Container ships and large ports make it cheap to move goods around the world, so products can be made where labour is cheapest\n• Cheaper air travel lets business people and tourists travel; perishable goods (flowers from Kenya) reach HIC markets quickly\n• The internet, mobile phones and video calls let TNCs manage factories and offices in many countries\n• Services (call centres, software) can be supplied from India to customers in the UK in real time\n• Online shopping / social media spread brands and culture globally` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Figure 1.13 is a fact box about the global clothing industry: many clothes sold in UK high-street shops are made in Bangladesh, where about 4 million people work in garment factories, most of them women. Factory workers earn far less than UK workers. In 2013 the Rana Plaza factory building near Dhaka collapsed, killing more than 1,100 workers. Clothing makes up over 80% of Bangladesh's exports. Some UK textile towns have lost most of their factories since the 1980s.\n\nExplain why globalisation has produced both winners and losers. Use Figure 1.13 and examples you have studied.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed, well-linked explanation of both winners and losers at different scales, using evidence from the figure and other examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): explanation of winners and losers but not balanced, or limited use of evidence.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements, mostly description; may refer to only winners or losers.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\nWinners\n• TNCs and shareholders — lower costs and higher profits\n• UK consumers — cheaper clothes and wider choice\n• Bangladesh — millions of jobs, especially for women, increasing incomes and independence; export earnings for the government\n• NICs such as India and China — rapid economic growth\nLosers\n• Factory workers in Bangladesh — low wages, long hours, unsafe buildings (Rana Plaza)\n• Bangladesh depends heavily on one industry — vulnerable if orders move elsewhere\n• Former textile workers in UK towns — unemployment, decline of communities\n• Environment — pollution of rivers by dyes, emissions from shipping\n• Some LICs left out of globalisation altogether, widening the development gap` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. In the village of Plachimada in Kerala, India, a Coca-Cola bottling plant opened in 2000. Local people complained that wells had dried up and that waste from the plant was polluting farmland. The plant closed in 2004.\n\nExplain the environmental impacts of rapid economic development in an LIC or NIC you have studied. Name the country.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): detailed, well-explained impacts with clear links to economic development and specific, located detail of the named country.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): some explanation of impacts with some place detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple, generic statements; country may not be named.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content (India):\n• Water — over-abstraction by industry and farming, falling water tables (e.g. Plachimada); river pollution, e.g. the Ganges and Yamuna polluted by industrial waste and sewage\n• Air pollution — coal power stations, vehicles and factories; Delhi among the world's most polluted cities, causing breathing problems\n• Greenhouse gas emissions — India now one of the world's largest emitters, adding to climate change\n• Deforestation / loss of habitats for mining, dams and urban growth\n• Waste — more consumer goods and plastics, e-waste processing\n• Some positives: investment in solar power (large solar parks in Rajasthan / Gujarat)\nCredit other valid LIC/NIC case studies.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Figure 1.14 shows two views.\nView A — trade: "Fairer trade lets countries earn their own money. Ghana earns over US$2 billion a year from cocoa exports, and Fairtrade co-operatives such as Kuapa Kokoo use their premium to build schools and wells."\nView B — aid: "Aid can reach the poorest people quickly. In Malawi, aid-funded projects have provided clean water to thousands of villages and helped after Cyclone Freddy in 2023."\n\nIs trade or aid the better way to reduce global inequality? Justify your views. You may also use examples you have studied. [8 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): thorough evaluation of both trade and aid, using the figure and own examples; reaches a well-supported, justified decision.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): some evaluation of both, or detailed on one; a decision with some justification.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements; little or no justification.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\nTrade\n• Creates jobs and income; countries earn rather than depend on donors\n• Fair trade guarantees minimum prices and premium for community projects\n• But world trade rules, tariffs and price fluctuations favour HICs; most cocoa profit made in processing in HICs\n• Fairtrade covers only a small share of world trade\nAid\n• Emergency aid saves lives after disasters\n• Long-term / NGO aid (e.g. WaterAid) meets basic needs such as clean water and education\n• But can create dependency; tied aid benefits donors; corruption may stop aid reaching the poor; large top-down projects may not help the poorest\nJudgement may favour either, or argue both are needed (e.g. aid for basic needs, trade for long-term growth).\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh (3): spelling and punctuation consistently accurate; meaning always clear; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate (2): spelling and punctuation considerable accuracy; meaning clear overall; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold (1): reasonable accuracy; does not hinder meaning; limited specialist terms.\n0: errors severely hinder meaning, or answer not related to the question.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Tata is an Indian TNC; foreign TNCs such as Unilever (Hindustan Unilever), Coca-Cola and Microsoft also operate in India. TNCs employ millions of people in India's cities, but critics point to low wages in some factories and to profits being sent back to headquarters overseas.\n\n'TNCs bring more advantages than disadvantages to LICs and NICs.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use an LIC or NIC you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): balanced, well-supported evaluation of advantages and disadvantages, using detailed, located case-study evidence; clear, justified conclusion about the extent of agreement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): advantages and disadvantages considered, with some case-study evidence; conclusion partly justified.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements, mostly generic; one-sided; little or no conclusion.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content (India):\nAdvantages\n• Jobs in factories, offices and call centres, often better paid than farming\n• Investment in infrastructure; transfer of skills and technology\n• Tax revenue for government services; multiplier effect on local suppliers\n• Hindustan Unilever's rural sales network gives work to women in villages\nDisadvantages\n• Some low wages, long hours, poor conditions\n• Profits leave the country to HIC headquarters\n• Environmental damage, e.g. groundwater use at Plachimada\n• TNCs can close plants and move elsewhere; local businesses may be out-competed\n• Benefits concentrated in cities, widening regional inequality\nConclusion may depend on the government's regulation, the type of TNC or whether benefits reach the poor.\nCredit other valid LIC/NIC case studies.` },
      { q: `Theme 1: Changing Places — Changing Economies. Figure 1.15 compares two approaches to development.\nTop-down: the Kariba Dam on the Zambezi River (Zambia/Zimbabwe), funded by large international loans, produces hydro-electric power mainly for cities and copper mines; about 57,000 people were moved from their land when it was built.\nBottom-up: local NGO projects in rural Zambia train villagers to build small wells, use solar panels and set up savings groups so families can borrow small sums to start businesses.\n\nShould bottom-up projects be used instead of top-down projects to reduce inequality in LICs? Justify your views. You may also use examples you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): thorough evaluation of both approaches, using evidence from the figure and own examples; well-justified decision.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): some evaluation of both, or detailed on one; decision partly justified.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements; little or no justification.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\nBottom-up\n• Local people decide what they need; appropriate technology is cheap and easy to maintain\n• Benefits go directly to the poorest rural people; little debt\n• Microfinance / savings groups help people start businesses, especially women\n• But small scale — may not transform a whole country's economy\nTop-down\n• Large-scale power supply can support industry and national economic growth / export earnings\n• But expensive, often funded by loans adding to debt; people displaced; benefits mainly to cities and businesses\n• Environmental impacts of large dams\nJudgement may favour either, or argue that both are needed at different scales.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.1': {
    name: 'Coasts and Coastal Management',
    questions: [
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 1: a photograph of Old Harry Rocks at Handfast Point, Dorset. A tall column of white chalk stands in the sea, separated from the end of the chalk headland. To its right is a much smaller, low stump of chalk just above the water.\n\nIdentify the landform shown by the tall column of chalk in Figure 1.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark for: stack.\nDo not accept arch, stump or headland.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nOS map extract (1:50,000, described for this practice question): a stretch of coast with the sea to the east. A chalk headland, Ness Point, occupies grid squares 2272 and 2372 and rises to 45 m, with cliff symbols along its seaward edge and a lighthouse at 237726. South of the headland a sandy bay curves westwards between northings 70 and 71, with a caravan site at 226705, a car park at 229707 and five groynes drawn across the beach between 230710 and 234704. From the southern end of the bay a narrow ridge of sand and shingle extends south-east across the mouth of the River Wend estuary. Its tip curves inland at 255684. Behind the ridge, in grid squares 2468 and 2469, are mudflat and saltmarsh symbols.\n\nGive the four-figure grid reference of the square that contains the tip of the sand and shingle ridge.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for: 2568.\nDo not accept 6825 (northing given first).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nOS map extract (1:50,000, described for this practice question): a stretch of coast with the sea to the east. A chalk headland, Ness Point, occupies grid squares 2272 and 2372 and rises to 45 m. The only building on the headland is a lighthouse, shown by its symbol in the eastern half of grid square 2372, seven-tenths of the way east and six-tenths of the way north across the square. South of the headland is a sandy bay with a caravan site and a car park.\n\nGive the six-figure grid reference of the lighthouse.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for: 237726.\nAllow 237725 or 237727 (one-tenth tolerance on the northing).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nSediment is moved along the Holderness coast in East Yorkshire from north to south.\n\nWhich of the following is the correct definition of longshore drift?\nA  The movement of sediment up and down a beach by waves arriving at right angles to the coast\nB  The zigzag movement of sediment along a coast by swash at an angle and backwash straight down the beach\nC  The movement of sediment out to sea by strong backwash during storms\nD  The movement of dissolved minerals in sea water`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `1 mark for: B.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 2: a photograph of the base of a chalk cliff at Flamborough Head, East Yorkshire. Waves are breaking against the cliff foot. The chalk is crossed by many vertical joints and horizontal bedding planes, and a small cave has formed where two joints meet.\n\nExplain how hydraulic action could erode the cliff shown in Figure 2.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point and 1 further mark for development, e.g.\n• Waves crash against the cliff and force water and air into the joints/bedding planes (1)\n• the air is compressed and, as the wave retreats, it expands suddenly; repeated pressure widens the cracks until blocks of chalk break off (1)\nAllow: the sheer force of the water against the cliff loosens rock (1). Credit reference to the joints in Figure 2 as lines of weakness.\nNo credit for describing abrasion or solution.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nYou have carried out fieldwork to investigate whether pebbles become smaller along a beach in the direction of longshore drift.\nTable 1 (data written for this practice question): long-axis length (mm) of ten pebbles sampled at Site 1.\n52, 47, 61, 38, 55, 49, 44, 58, 40, 56\n\nCalculate the mean long-axis length of the pebbles at Site 1. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: 52 + 47 + 61 + 38 + 55 + 49 + 44 + 58 + 40 + 56 = 500, then 500 ÷ 10 (1)\n1 mark for the answer: 50 mm (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nOS map extract (1:25,000, described for this practice question): a straight sandy coastline with a long shingle spit at its southern end. On the map, the straight-line distance from the car park at the root of the spit to the coastguard station at its tip measures 7.4 cm.\n\nCalculate the actual distance from the car park to the coastguard station. Give your answer in kilometres.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for method: 7.4 × 25,000 = 185,000 cm, or 1 cm = 0.25 km so 7.4 × 0.25 (1)\n1 mark for the answer: 1.85 km (1)\nAllow 1.8–1.9 km. Correct answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 3: a cross-section diagram of a wave breaking on a beach. The wave is tall and steep, and it plunges downwards as it breaks. A short arrow labelled 'swash' points up the beach; a much longer, thicker arrow labelled 'backwash' points down the beach. The label reads '13–15 waves per minute'. The beach is steep and narrow.\n\nGive two characteristics of the wave shown in Figure 3 that show it is a destructive wave.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark each for any two of:\n• Backwash is stronger than the swash (1)\n• High frequency — 13–15 waves per minute (1)\n• Tall/steep wave that plunges as it breaks (1)\n• It produces a steep, narrow beach / removes material from the beach (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nTable 2 (data written for this practice question): average annual rate of cliff retreat at five undefended sites on the Holderness coast.\nSite A 1.6 m/year\nSite B 2.1 m/year\nSite C 0.4 m/year\nSite D 1.9 m/year\nSite E 2.5 m/year\n\nUsing Table 2, calculate the mean annual rate of cliff retreat for the five sites. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for working: (1.6 + 2.1 + 0.4 + 1.9 + 2.5) = 8.5, then 8.5 ÷ 5 (1)\n1 mark for the answer: 1.7 m/year (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 4: a photograph of a seaside town where a new line of large granite boulders (rock armour) has been placed at the foot of an old, cracked concrete sea wall. A sign reads 'Coastal defence improvement scheme — Cost: £3.2 million'.\n\nSuggest one reason why rock armour has been placed in front of the sea wall shown in Figure 4.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason and 1 further mark for development, e.g.\n• The boulders absorb/break up the energy of the waves (1) so less force hits the old sea wall and it is not undermined further (1)\n• The cracked wall is being eroded at its base (1); rock armour is cheaper than building a completely new sea wall (1)\n• Gaps between the boulders let water drain through and dissipate energy (1), reducing wave overtopping into the town (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nBournemouth's beaches in Dorset are a major tourist attraction. Every few years, sand dredged from the seabed offshore is pumped onto the beaches to replace sand lost to erosion and longshore drift.\n\nDescribe how beach nourishment can help to protect a coastline.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point and 1 further mark for development, e.g.\n• Sand or shingle is added to make the beach wider/higher (1); a wider beach absorbs wave energy before waves reach the cliffs or promenade (1)\n• It replaces sediment lost by longshore drift (1), so the beach continues to act as a natural barrier and keeps attracting tourists (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 5: a photograph of a cliff of boulder clay on the Holderness coast after a week of heavy rain. A large section of the cliff has slipped down in a curved movement, leaving a scar at the top and a bulge of clay at the foot, which is being washed away by the sea.\n\nExplain why slumping happens on boulder clay cliffs like the one in Figure 5.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point and 1 further mark for development, e.g.\n• Heavy rain soaks into the boulder clay, making it saturated and heavier (1); the clay becomes slippery/weak and slides down along a curved surface (1)\n• Waves erode the base of the cliff (1), removing support so the cliff above collapses (1)\nMax 1 for a description of slumping without a reason.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 6: a diagram showing a chalk headland with a cave on one side, a natural arch through the headland, a stack standing in the sea and a stump just above the water line.\n\nComplete the sentences using words from the list.\nweathering  hydraulic action  deposition  stack  stump  bay\n\n"The roof of an arch is weakened by ________ and the sea, and eventually it collapses. This leaves a ________ separated from the headland."`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `1 mark for: weathering (1)\n1 mark for: stack (1)\nAllow hydraulic action for the first space.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nA group of students measured pebble size every 20 m along a 200 m transect on a beach, starting at the cliff foot and finishing at the low-water mark.\n\nGive one advantage of using systematic sampling in this investigation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for an advantage and 1 further mark for development, e.g.\n• It covers the whole beach evenly (1) so changes in pebble size across the beach can be seen/patterns are not missed (1)\n• It is quick and easy to set up (1) because the sites are at regular, measured intervals (1)\n• It reduces bias (1) as the students do not choose which pebbles to measure (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 7: a map of the Holderness coast, East Yorkshire. The coast runs from Flamborough Head in the north to Spurn Head in the south. Spurn Head is a long, narrow spit that extends about 5 km south-westwards from the village of Kilnsea, across the mouth of the Humber Estuary. Hull lies about 30 km to the west of Spurn Head, upstream on the north bank of the estuary.\n\nUsing Figure 7, describe the location of Spurn Head.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark each for any two accurate locational points, e.g.\n• At the southern end of the Holderness coast (1)\n• Across the mouth of the Humber Estuary (1)\n• Extends south-west from Kilnsea (1)\n• About 30 km east/south-east of Hull (1)\nUse of a named place with direction or distance is needed for each mark.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nThe Maldives is a country of about 1,190 small coral islands in the Indian Ocean. About 80% of its land lies less than 1 m above sea level.\n\nGive two reasons why rising sea levels are a threat to the people of the Maldives.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark each for any two of:\n• Islands/homes could be flooded or lost completely (1)\n• Salt water contaminates fresh groundwater supplies (1)\n• Salt damages crops/farmland (1)\n• Tourism (the main industry) is threatened as beaches and resorts are lost (1)\n• Storm surges reach further inland / flooding becomes more frequent (1)\n• People may have to migrate to other islands or countries (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nTable 3 (data written for this practice question): beach profile at Site A, measured from the low-water mark up to the foot of the dunes.\nDistance from low-water mark (m): 0, 10, 20, 30, 40\nHeight above low-water mark (m): 0, 0.6, 1.4, 2.2, 3.2\n\nCalculate the average gradient of the beach at Site A between 0 m and 40 m. Give your answer as a ratio in the form 1 : n.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for method: 3.2 m rise over 40 m horizontal distance, 40 ÷ 3.2 (1)\n1 mark for the answer: 1 : 12.5 (1)\nAllow 1 : 13. Correct answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 8 (data written for this practice question): a map of the Holderness coast showing average annual cliff retreat. At Flamborough Head (chalk) in the north the rate is less than 0.1 m/year. South of Bridlington (defended by a sea wall) the rate rises to about 1.5 m/year at Barmston and 2.0 m/year at Skipsea. At Hornsea (sea wall and groynes) it is almost 0 m/year. Just south of Hornsea it is 1.2 m/year, rising to 2.5 m/year at Cowden and 2.8 m/year near Aldbrough. At Withernsea (sea wall) it is almost 0 m/year, and between Withernsea and Kilnsea it reaches 3.0 m/year.\n\nDescribe the pattern of cliff retreat shown in Figure 8.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid descriptive point, max 3. Credit use of data.\n• Rates generally increase from north to south (1)\n• Lowest at Flamborough Head, under 0.1 m/year (1)\n• Highest in the far south, 3.0 m/year between Withernsea and Kilnsea (1)\n• Rates fall to almost 0 m/year at the defended towns of Bridlington, Hornsea and Withernsea (1)\n• Rates are high just south of the defended towns, e.g. 2.5 m/year at Cowden (1)\nNo credit for explanation.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nOn the Holderness coast the prevailing wind blows from the north-east, and large amounts of sand and clay eroded from the cliffs are carried southwards towards Spurn Head.\n\nExplain how longshore drift moves material along a coastline.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each linked point, max 3.\n• Waves approach the coast at an angle because of the direction of the prevailing wind (1)\n• The swash carries sediment up the beach at that same angle (1)\n• The backwash pulls sediment straight back down the beach at right angles to the coast, under gravity (1)\n• Repeated over and over, sediment moves along the coast in a zigzag (1), from north to south on Holderness (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nYou have carried out fieldwork on a beach to investigate how the beach profile changes on either side of a groyne.\n\nDescribe how you could measure a beach profile.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each step of a workable method, max 3.\n• Lay a tape measure in a straight line from the low-water mark (sea) up to the back of the beach (1)\n• Place two ranging poles at each break of slope or at fixed intervals, e.g. every 5 m (1)\n• Measure the angle between the poles with a clinometer, sighting from the same height on each pole (1)\n• Record the distance and angle for each section and repeat on the other side of the groyne (1)\n• Repeat the profile three times to improve reliability (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nTable 4 (data written for this practice question): beach profiles either side of a groyne. Longshore drift moves sediment from north to south.\nDistance from low-water mark (m): 0, 10, 20, 30, 40\nSite A, north side of groyne — height (m): 0, 0.6, 1.4, 2.2, 3.2\nSite B, south side of groyne — height (m): 0, 0.3, 0.7, 1.0, 1.4\n\nUsing Table 4, choose the correct answer in each statement.\nThe steeper and higher beach is at (Site A / Site B).\nThe total height gain at Site B is (1.0 / 1.4 / 3.2) m.\nThe steepest section of the beach at Site A is between (0 and 10 m / 10 and 20 m / 30 and 40 m).`, marks: 3, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `1 mark for each correct answer:\n• Site A (1)\n• 1.4 m (1)\n• 30 and 40 m (1) — height gain of 1.0 m, the largest of any section.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 9: an aerial photograph of Spurn Head, a curved spit about 5 km long at the southern end of the Holderness coast. The spit stretches across the mouth of the Humber Estuary, and its tip hooks westwards. Behind the spit, in sheltered water, there are large areas of mudflat and saltmarsh.\n\nExplain how a spit such as Spurn Head is formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A clear explanation of the sequence, with processes linked to features; uses correct terminology.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic statements about formation; limited sequence or links.\nIndicative content:\n• Longshore drift carries sand and shingle along the coast (south on Holderness)\n• Where the coastline changes direction, e.g. at an estuary mouth, the sediment continues to be deposited out to sea\n• Deposition occurs because energy falls in the deeper/calmer water and where the river meets the sea\n• The ridge of sediment grows longer over time\n• Changes in wind/wave direction curve the end of the spit, forming a hooked or recurved tip\n• Sheltered water behind the spit allows fine silt to settle, forming mudflats and saltmarsh\n• The river current stops the spit from crossing the estuary completely` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nAt the foot of the chalk cliffs at Flamborough Head, a wide, flat, rocky surface is exposed at low tide. It is covered with rock pools and extends about 200 m out to sea.\n\nDraw an annotated diagram to show how a wave-cut platform is formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): An accurate diagram (cross-section of cliff and platform) with annotations that explain the sequence of formation.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A simple diagram with labels that name features, or annotations that describe without explaining.\nIndicative content:\n• Waves erode the base of the cliff between high and low tide by hydraulic action and abrasion\n• A wave-cut notch forms\n• The cliff above is unsupported and collapses\n• The cliff retreats inland and the process repeats\n• A gently sloping, flat rocky area is left at the foot of the cliff — the wave-cut platform, exposed at low tide\nMax Level 1 for labels only.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 10 (maps written for this practice question): two maps of the same 2 km stretch of the Holderness coast near a small village.\nIn 1900 the cliff top lay about 220 m east of the village church. A road ran north–south along the cliff top and Cliff Farm stood between the road and the cliff.\nIn 2020 the cliff top lies about 20 m from the church. The cliff-top road and Cliff Farm have gone. A caravan park, shown at the cliff edge on a 1960 map, has moved 150 m inland. A 300 m section of rock armour now protects the cliff in front of the village, and the coastline sticks out slightly into the sea at this point.\n\nDescribe the changes to the coastline shown in Figure 10.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid descriptive point, max 4. At least one point must use data for full marks.\n• The cliff top has retreated about 200 m inland between 1900 and 2020 (1)\n• This is an average of about 1.7 m per year (1)\n• The cliff-top road has been lost to the sea (1)\n• Cliff Farm has been destroyed/lost (1)\n• The caravan park has been moved 150 m inland (1)\n• Rock armour has been added in front of the village (1)\n• The defended section now forms a small bump/headland as the undefended coast either side continues to retreat (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 11: a geology map of the coast around Swanage, Dorset. The rock bands run west–east, meeting the coast at right angles. In the north, Ballard Point is made of chalk. In the centre, Swanage Bay is cut into Wealden clays and sands. In the south, Durlston Head is made of Purbeck and Portland limestone.\n\nUsing Figure 11, explain how geology has affected the shape of the coastline around Swanage.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A clear explanation that links rock type and resistance to the formation of headlands and a bay, using evidence from Figure 11.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points about hard and soft rock with little use of the figure.\nIndicative content:\n• This is a discordant coastline — bands of rock of different resistance meet the coast at right angles\n• The Wealden clays and sands are less resistant and are eroded quickly, forming Swanage Bay\n• Chalk (Ballard Point) and limestone (Durlston Head) are more resistant and erode slowly, so they stick out as headlands\n• Once the bay forms, the headlands receive more wave energy through wave refraction, while the bay is sheltered and sand is deposited to form a beach` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nOS map extract (1:50,000, described for this practice question): a stretch of coast with the sea to the east. A chalk headland, Ness Point, occupies grid squares 2272 and 2372 and rises to 45 m, with cliff symbols along its seaward edge and a lighthouse at 237726. South of the headland a sandy bay curves westwards between northings 70 and 71, with a caravan site at 226705, a car park at 229707 and five groynes drawn across the beach between 230710 and 234704. From the southern end of the bay a narrow ridge of sand and shingle extends south-east across the mouth of the River Wend estuary. Its tip curves inland at 255684. Behind the ridge, in grid squares 2468 and 2469, are mudflat and saltmarsh symbols.\n\nDescribe the physical features of the coastline shown on the map extract. Use map evidence in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each physical feature correctly identified with map evidence (grid reference, height or direction), max 4. Human features (caravan site, car park, lighthouse, groynes) gain no credit unless used to locate a physical feature.\n• Headland at Ness Point, squares 2272/2372 (1)\n• High chalk cliffs, up to 45 m (1)\n• A sandy bay south of the headland, between northings 70 and 71 (1)\n• A spit of sand and shingle extending south-east across the estuary (1)\n• The spit has a curved/hooked tip at 255684 (1)\n• Mudflats and saltmarsh behind the spit in 2468/2469 (1)\n• An estuary of the River Wend (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFairbourne is a village of about 400 homes on low-lying land on the coast of Gwynedd, North Wales. The Shoreline Management Plan for the area states that 'hold the line' will continue only until about 2054. After that, the sea defences will not be maintained and the village is expected to be decommissioned.\n\nExplain why groups of people have different views about the plan for Fairbourne.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of at least two contrasting views, linked to the reasons different groups hold them; specific to Fairbourne.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements of views, little explanation or not linked to groups.\nIndicative content:\n• Residents oppose it: they may lose their homes and community; house values have fallen and homes are hard to sell or insure\n• Local businesses (e.g. caravan parks, the railway) fear loss of income\n• The council/Natural Resources Wales support it: rising sea levels and the cost of maintaining defences make holding the line unsustainable; money can be spent where more people benefit\n• Environmental groups may welcome a return to natural salt marsh and intertidal habitats\n• Taxpayers may think it unfair to spend large sums protecting a small number of properties` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nIn 1991, two rock groynes and a rock revetment were built at Mappleton on the Holderness coast at a cost of about £2 million to protect the village and the main coast road. Since then, the beach at Mappleton has grown wider, but the cliffs at Cowden, about 3 km to the south, have retreated faster than before.\n\nExplain why hard engineering at one place can cause problems further along the coast.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation linking the defence to longshore drift and the effects downdrift, with reference to Mappleton/Cowden.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic statements, e.g. 'erosion moves along the coast', with little explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Groynes trap sediment moved south by longshore drift, so the beach builds up at Mappleton\n• Less sediment reaches the coast to the south (Cowden), so beaches there become narrower\n• Without a beach to absorb wave energy, waves attack the base of the boulder clay cliffs\n• Erosion rates increase downdrift — the 'terminal groyne effect'\n• Farmland and properties at Cowden are lost faster, causing conflict` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nOn 5–6 December 2013, a storm surge struck the east coast of England. In some places, sea levels were the highest since the 1953 North Sea flood. At Hemsby in Norfolk, several houses fell into the sea when the dunes they stood on were washed away.\n\nExplain how storms can cause coastal flooding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of the processes, with linked points, e.g. low pressure plus wind plus high tide.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic statements, e.g. 'big waves flood the land'.\nIndicative content:\n• Low air pressure in a deep depression allows the sea surface to rise (about 1 cm for every 1 mb fall in pressure)\n• Strong winds push water towards the coast and build up large, powerful waves\n• In a narrowing sea such as the southern North Sea, water piles up into a storm surge\n• If the surge coincides with a high (spring) tide, water levels rise above sea defences\n• Large waves overtop or breach walls and erode dunes, letting the sea flood low-lying land` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nMany low-lying coastal areas in countries outside the UK, such as Bangladesh, the Netherlands and the Maldives, face an increasing risk of coastal flooding.\n\nDescribe how people in a country you have studied are reducing the risk of coastal flooding. Name the country.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Detailed, place-specific description of at least two methods.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Generic description of methods, which could apply anywhere.\nMax Level 1 if no country is named.\nIndicative content, e.g. Bangladesh:\n• Raised embankments around 'polders' in the coastal zone\n• Thousands of raised concrete cyclone shelters\n• Early warnings through the Cyclone Preparedness Programme, spread by trained volunteers\n• Planting mangroves along the coast to absorb wave energy and storm surges\nOr the Maldives:\n• A sea wall built around the capital Malé, funded by Japan after the 1987 floods\n• Hulhumalé, an artificial island built higher above sea level, for new housing\n• Protecting coral reefs, which absorb wave energy\nCredit any valid country, e.g. the Netherlands (Delta Works, storm surge barriers, dykes).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nTable 5: approximate costs and lifespans of coastal management methods (figures rounded, from published UK coastal management guides).\nSea wall: up to £10,000 per metre, lasts about 50 years\nRock armour: up to £3,000 per metre, lasts about 30 years\nBeach nourishment: about £2,000 per metre each time, needs repeating every 5–10 years\nSand dune planting and fencing: under £100 per metre, lasts as long as it is maintained\n\nSuggest why soft engineering may be more sustainable than hard engineering. Use evidence from Table 5.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear reasons, linked to sustainability (economic and/or environmental and/or social), with evidence from Table 5.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic reasons with little use of the table.\nIndicative content:\n• Dune planting costs under £100 per metre compared with up to £10,000 per metre for a sea wall, so more coast can be protected for the same money\n• Soft engineering works with natural processes, so it does not stop longshore drift or cause erosion further along the coast\n• It keeps natural habitats (beaches, dunes) and looks natural, which supports tourism\n• Hard defences are expensive to replace when they reach the end of their life (about 30–50 years)\n• Allow the counter-argument that beach nourishment must be repeated every 5–10 years, so its long-term costs build up` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nTable 6 (data written for this practice question): distance of the cliff top from fixed markers on the Holderness coast.\nBarmston: 62 m in 2000, 30 m in 2020\nSkipsea: 85 m in 2000, 43 m in 2020\nAldbrough: 70 m in 2000, 34 m in 2020\nWithernsea (sea wall): 15 m in 2000, 15 m in 2020\n\nUsing Table 6, choose the correct answer in each statement.\nThe greatest loss of cliff between 2000 and 2020 was at (Barmston / Skipsea / Aldbrough).\nThe total loss of cliff at Barmston was (22 / 32 / 42) m.\nThe average rate of cliff loss at Aldbrough was (1.2 / 1.8 / 3.6) m per year.\nThe site with the least erosion was (Barmston / Aldbrough / Withernsea).`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `1 mark for each correct answer:\n• Skipsea (42 m) (1)\n• 32 m (1)\n• 1.8 m per year (36 m ÷ 20 years) (1)\n• Withernsea (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nStudents investigated the rate of longshore drift on a beach. They placed a brightly painted float in the sea at a marker and recorded how far it travelled along the beach in 5 minutes. They did this once at 2 pm on a single day.\n\nEvaluate the reliability of the students' method.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A balanced evaluation that recognises strengths and weaknesses and suggests improvements that would increase reliability.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements of strengths or weaknesses.\nIndicative content:\n• Strength: simple, cheap and quick; the float clearly shows the direction of drift; the timing is fixed at 5 minutes\n• Weakness: only one trial, so an anomalous result cannot be identified\n• A float shows water movement, not the movement of sediment\n• Only one day and one time — the result depends on the wind and tide at that time\n• Improvements: repeat at least three times and calculate a mean; repeat on different days and at different states of the tide; use painted pebbles to measure sediment movement` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 12: a photograph of sand dunes at Ynyslas, at the mouth of the Dyfi Estuary in Ceredigion, Wales. Marram grass is growing on the dunes. Wooden boardwalks guide visitors across them, sections of chestnut fencing have been placed on the front of the dunes, and signs ask people to keep off fenced areas.\n\nExplain how the management shown in Figure 12 can help to protect the coastline.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of how at least two methods in Figure 12 protect the dunes, linked to how dunes protect the coast.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic description of the methods with little explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Dunes are a natural barrier, absorbing wave energy and stopping the sea flooding the land behind\n• Marram grass roots bind the sand together and its leaves trap blown sand, so the dunes grow\n• Boardwalks stop trampling, which kills vegetation and causes blowouts\n• Fencing traps sand and keeps people off fragile areas so the dunes can recover\n• It is cheap, natural and sustainable compared with a sea wall` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nThe Holderness coast in East Yorkshire is one of the fastest-eroding coastlines in Europe, losing on average about 2 m of land each year.\n\nExplain the social and economic impacts of coastal erosion on a UK coastline you have studied.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of both social and economic impacts, with place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic, generic impacts with little explanation; or only social or only economic impacts.\nIndicative content (e.g. Holderness):\n• Homes are lost to the sea (e.g. at Skipsea), and owners usually receive no compensation because insurance does not cover erosion\n• Communities break up and people suffer stress and anxiety\n• Farmland is lost, so farm incomes fall (e.g. at Cowden)\n• Roads are cut or must be moved inland, so journeys take longer and repairs are costly\n• Caravan parks must relocate inland, and tourism income can be affected\n• Important sites must be protected, e.g. the Easington gas terminal\nCredit any valid UK coastline, e.g. Happisburgh (Norfolk) or Borth (Ceredigion).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 13: a photograph of Handfast Point, Dorset. The end of the chalk headland has a small cave at sea level. Offshore stands Old Harry, a chalk stack, and next to it a low stump known as Old Harry's Wife, which collapsed in 1896.\n\nExplain how a headland can be eroded to form a stack and then a stump.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 2, AO2 4.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed, well-sequenced explanation that links processes to each stage (crack → cave → arch → stack → stump), using precise terminology; may refer to Figure 13.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A sound explanation of most stages, with some processes named and linked.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic description of some stages; processes named but not linked.\nIndicative content:\n• Headlands are made of more resistant rock but contain lines of weakness such as joints and faults\n• Hydraulic action and abrasion widen a crack at the base of the headland\n• The crack is enlarged into a cave\n• Caves on opposite sides of the headland meet, or one cuts right through, forming an arch\n• Erosion at the base of the arch and weathering of the roof (freeze-thaw, salt weathering) weaken it\n• The roof collapses, leaving a stack separated from the headland, e.g. Old Harry\n• Waves erode the base of the stack, which collapses to form a stump, e.g. Old Harry's Wife` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nShoreline Management Plans (SMPs) divide the coast of England and Wales into sections. For each section, one of four policies is chosen: hold the line, advance the line, managed realignment or no active intervention. On the Holderness coast, the towns of Bridlington, Hornsea and Withernsea are protected, but most of the coastline between them is not.\n\nExplain why some sections of coastline are protected while others are not.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 2, AO2 4.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of several reasons, clearly linked to SMP decision-making (e.g. cost–benefit analysis), with specific examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A sound explanation of at least two reasons; some use of examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic reasons, e.g. 'it costs too much', with little explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Cost–benefit analysis: defences are built only where the value of the land and property protected is greater than the cost\n• Towns such as Hornsea and Withernsea have many homes, businesses and tourist facilities, so they are protected (hold the line)\n• Farmland and scattered homes have low value, so the policy is no active intervention\n• Important infrastructure is protected, e.g. the Easington gas terminal\n• Defending one area can increase erosion downdrift, so not defending keeps sediment moving to beaches and to Spurn Head\n• Some areas are left to provide natural habitats, or for managed realignment to create salt marsh\n• There is limited government money, and sea-level rise makes defending everywhere unsustainable` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 14: information about Medmerry, West Sussex. Until 2013 a shingle bank protected low-lying farmland, caravan parks and around 350 homes, but it was often breached and had to be repaired every winter. In 2013 the Environment Agency completed a managed realignment scheme. The sea was allowed through the shingle bank, and about 7 km of new clay embankments were built further inland. The flooded land became intertidal habitat and an RSPB nature reserve. Some farmland was lost.\n\nShould more of the UK coastline be managed by 'managed realignment' instead of 'hold the line'? Justify your views. You may also use examples you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4, AO3 4.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): A well-reasoned, balanced judgement that weighs the costs and benefits of both approaches; makes detailed use of Figure 14 and other examples; reaches a clear, justified conclusion.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): A reasonable judgement with some justification; uses Figure 14 and/or examples; may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): A simple view with little justification; limited use of evidence.\nIndicative content:\nFor managed realignment:\n• Works with natural processes; salt marsh absorbs wave energy and adjusts as sea levels rise\n• Cheaper in the long term than repairing defences every year, as at Medmerry before 2013\n• Creates habitats and biodiversity (RSPB reserve), and can bring in eco-tourism income\n• The shorter, inland line of defence is easier to maintain\nFor hold the line:\n• Protects towns, infrastructure and high-value land where many people live, e.g. Hornsea or Withernsea\n• Managed realignment loses farmland, and homeowners may get no compensation\n• It is socially unpopular, e.g. strong opposition at Fairbourne\nConclusion: credit any justified view, e.g. 'managed realignment where land is of low value, hold the line for towns'.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 15: fact box about the Maldives.\n• About 1,190 coral islands; around 80% of the land is less than 1 m above sea level\n• Global sea levels are rising, and the IPCC projects a further rise of roughly 0.3–1 m by 2100\n• After serious floods in 1987, a sea wall about 3 m high was built around the capital, Malé, with funding from Japan\n• Hulhumalé, an artificial island built higher above sea level next to Malé, is being developed for new housing\n• The government protects coral reefs and has spoken of buying land in other countries if islands become uninhabitable\n• Tourism is the main source of income\n\nEvaluate the success of the strategies used to manage the effects of rising sea levels in a country outside the UK that you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO2 4, AO3 4.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): A detailed, balanced evaluation of several strategies, assessing successes and limitations, with place-specific evidence; reaches a supported judgement on overall success.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): A sound evaluation of some strategies with some evidence; judgement may be implied or weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic description of strategies with little evaluation.\nIndicative content (Maldives):\n• Malé sea wall: protects the densely populated capital and reduced wave damage during the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami; but it is costly, depended on foreign aid, protects only one island, and may be overtopped as sea levels keep rising\n• Hulhumalé: a long-term solution that gives people higher ground; but it is expensive, dredging damages reefs, and it houses only part of the population\n• Coral reef protection: natural and sustainable, and supports tourism; but reefs are threatened by coral bleaching as oceans warm\n• Plans to relocate abroad: an admission that defences may fail\n• Small, poor outer islands have little protection\n• The Maldives cannot solve global emissions alone\nCredit other countries, e.g. Bangladesh (embankments, cyclone shelters, mangroves) or Kiribati.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Coasts.\nFigure 16: two coastal communities in Wales.\nBorth, Ceredigion: a village built along a shingle ridge. A coastal defence scheme completed in 2015 cost about £18 million. It included rock groynes, an offshore rock reef and beach nourishment. It protects homes, the main road and the railway, and the wider beach attracts surfers and tourists.\nFairbourne, Gwynedd: a village of about 400 homes on low-lying land behind a shingle bank. The Shoreline Management Plan states that the defences will be maintained only until about 2054, after which the village is expected to be decommissioned.\n\nIs 'holding the line', as at Borth, the best way to manage coastal communities at risk in Wales? Justify your views.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4, AO3 4.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): A well-reasoned, balanced argument that compares both approaches using evidence from Figure 16 and wider knowledge (sustainability, cost, sea-level rise, people's views); reaches a clear, justified conclusion.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): A reasonable argument with some justification and use of Figure 16; may be one-sided.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): A simple view with little justification or use of evidence.\nIndicative content:\nFor holding the line:\n• Protects homes, businesses, the road and the railway; communities stay together\n• At Borth, the scheme combines hard and soft methods and has boosted tourism (surfing reef, wider beach)\nAgainst holding the line:\n• It is very expensive (about £18 million at Borth) and defences need maintaining and upgrading as sea levels rise\n• Groynes can starve beaches downdrift\n• Funding is limited, so not every community can be protected; a cost–benefit analysis may not justify it\n• At Fairbourne, rising sea levels make long-term defence unsustainable; planned retreat may be safer and more realistic\nAgainst Fairbourne's approach: falling property values, stress and loss of community; residents may get no compensation\nConclusion: credit any justified view, e.g. 'it depends on the number of people and the value of what is protected, and on long-term sustainability'.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.2': {
    name: 'Rivers and River Management',
    questions: [
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 1: a map of the drainage basin of the River Severn. The river rises on Plynlimon in mid-Wales and flows north-east to Shrewsbury, then south through Worcester and Gloucester to the Severn Estuary. The River Vyrnwy joins it above Shrewsbury, the River Teme joins it at Worcester and the River Avon joins it at Tewkesbury. A thick dashed line marks the edge of the basin.\n\nChoose the correct definition of the term 'confluence'.\nA  The point where a river meets the sea\nB  The point where two rivers join\nC  The boundary between two drainage basins\nD  The place where a river begins`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B — the point where two rivers join (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nOS map extract (1:25,000), described: a river flows from west to east across the extract. In grid square 4527 the river forms a very tight meander loop; the narrow neck of land between the two sides of the loop is only about 40 m wide. In grid square 4627 there is a curved, crescent-shaped lake (blue) beside the river, not joined to it. A footbridge crosses the river in grid square 4427.\n\nGive the 4-figure grid reference of the square containing the crescent-shaped lake.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `4627 (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 2: a photograph of the lower course of the River Severn near Tewkesbury. The river is wide and slow-flowing, with a broad, flat area of fields on either side that are covered in shallow water after heavy rain. A low grassy ridge runs along each bank.\n\nName one landform in the lower course of a river that is formed by deposition.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `One from (1):\n• floodplain\n• levée\n• delta / estuary mudflats\n• slip-off slope / point bar (on the inside of a meander)\nAccept other valid depositional landforms.` },
      { q: `Applied Fieldwork Enquiry — investigating how a river changes downstream.\n\nStudents visited five sites along a small river in Wales. At each site they measured the width, depth and speed of the water.\n\nWhich piece of equipment would be suitable to measure the speed (velocity) of the river?\nA  Clinometer\nB  Flow meter\nC  Anemometer\nD  Quadrat`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B — flow meter (1).\n(Accept: a float timed over a measured distance, if a candidate explains this as an alternative.)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 3 (data written for this practice question): a storm hydrograph for a river in north Wales after a storm. Rainfall bars show the heaviest rainfall, 12 mm, fell in the hour starting at 09:00 on 3 November. The discharge line starts at 6 cumecs (m³/s), rises steeply and reaches its peak of 48 cumecs at 21:00 on 3 November, then falls slowly back to 8 cumecs by 12:00 on 5 November.\n\nUsing Figure 3, calculate the lag time. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: time of peak rainfall 09:00 to time of peak discharge 21:00 (1)\nAnswer: 12 hours (1)\nCorrect answer without working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 3 (data written for this practice question): a storm hydrograph for a river in north Wales. Before the storm the discharge of the river was 6 cumecs (m³/s). At the peak of the flood the discharge was 48 cumecs.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in discharge from before the storm to the peak. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 48 − 6 = 42; (42 ÷ 6) × 100 (1)\nAnswer: 700% (1)` },
      { q: `Applied Fieldwork Enquiry — investigating how a river changes downstream.\n\nTable 1 (data written for this practice question): measurements taken by students at site 3.\nChannel width: 4.2 m\nDepth measurements across the channel (m): 0.10, 0.24, 0.36, 0.30, 0.15\n\nCalculate the cross-sectional area of the channel at site 3. Use the formula: cross-sectional area = width × mean depth. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: mean depth = (0.10 + 0.24 + 0.36 + 0.30 + 0.15) ÷ 5 = 1.15 ÷ 5 = 0.23 m; 4.2 × 0.23 (1)\nAnswer: 0.966 m² (accept 0.97 m²) (1)` },
      { q: `Applied Fieldwork Enquiry — investigating how a river changes downstream.\n\nAt site 4 students found that the cross-sectional area of the channel was 1.6 m² and the mean velocity was 0.45 m/s (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the discharge at site 4. Use the formula: discharge = cross-sectional area × velocity. Give your answer in cumecs (m³/s). Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 1.6 × 0.45 (1)\nAnswer: 0.72 cumecs / m³/s (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nOS map extract (1:25,000), described: on a 1:25,000 map, 4 cm on the map represents 1 km on the ground. A student measures the river from the footbridge at 442271 downstream to the road bridge at 487262 by following its curves with a piece of string. The string measures 6.2 cm.\n\nCalculate the real distance along the river between the two bridges. Give your answer in kilometres. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 6.2 × 25,000 = 155,000 cm OR 6.2 ÷ 4 (1)\nAnswer: 1.55 km (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nOS map extract (1:50,000), described: a stream flows down the side of a valley in the Brecon Beacons. At its source the spot height is 300 m. At the point where it joins the main river the contour is 150 m. The horizontal distance between these two points is 1.5 km.\n\nCalculate the average gradient of the stream. Give your answer as a ratio (1 in …). Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: vertical drop 300 − 150 = 150 m; horizontal distance 1.5 km = 1,500 m; 1,500 ÷ 150 (1)\nAnswer: 1 in 10 (1)\nNo credit for answer left as 150 : 1.5.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 4 (data written for this practice question): an incomplete storm hydrograph for a river in Cumbria. The y-axis on the left shows rainfall (mm) from 0 to 20 and the x-axis shows time every 3 hours from 00:00 to 24:00. Rainfall bars are drawn for 00:00 (2 mm) and 03:00 (8 mm). The rainfall bar for 06:00 is missing.\n\nDescribe how you would complete Figure 4 to show 14 mm of rainfall at 06:00.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark each for:\n• draw a bar above the 06:00 position on the x-axis, the same width as the other bars (1)\n• the top of the bar at 14 mm on the rainfall axis; shaded/coloured the same as the other rainfall bars (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 5: a photograph of the centre of Carlisle, a city on the River Eden. It shows tarmac roads, car parks, rooftops and drains, with very little grass or trees.\n\nSuggest one reason why rivers flowing through urban areas like the one in Figure 5 have a short lag time.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• surfaces such as tarmac and concrete are impermeable (1) so rain cannot infiltrate and flows quickly over the surface to the river (1)\n• drains and sewers carry water (1) directly and rapidly into the river channel (1)\n• few trees / little vegetation (1) so less interception and the rain reaches the ground and the river faster (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 6: a photograph of the base of High Force waterfall on the River Tees in County Durham. Water falls about 21 m into a deep plunge pool. Large boulders and pebbles lie in the pool and on the river bed below it.\n\nDescribe how the process of abrasion erodes the river bed in Figure 6.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark each for:\n• the river picks up stones / boulders / its load (1)\n• which are thrown / swirled against the bed and banks, wearing them away like sandpaper (1)\nAccept reference to the plunge pool being deepened as rocks are swirled around.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 7 (data written for this practice question): two storm hydrographs after the same storm. River A drains a forested basin with permeable sandstone: lag time 14 hours, peak discharge 22 cumecs. River B drains an urban basin on clay: lag time 5 hours, peak discharge 60 cumecs.\n\nChoose the two correct statements.\nA  River A has a steeper rising limb than River B\nB  River B reaches its peak discharge 9 hours before River A\nC  River A has the higher peak discharge\nD  The peak discharge of River B is nearly three times that of River A\nE  The lag time of River A is shorter than that of River B`, marks: 2, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)\nD (1)\n(60 ÷ 22 = 2.7, nearly three times; 14 − 5 = 9 hours.)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nOS map extract (1:25,000), described: in the south-east of the extract (grid squares 5021 and 5121) the River Ouse is about 50 m wide and meanders across flat land. There are no contour lines within 1 km of the river. The land beside the river is marked with marsh symbols, and there are long embankments along both banks. Drainage ditches run in straight lines across the fields.\n\nGive two pieces of map evidence that the River Ouse is in its lower course in this area.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark each for any two:\n• the river is wide (about 50 m)\n• the river meanders\n• no contour lines / the land is flat / low gradient — a wide floodplain\n• marsh / wet land beside the river\n• embankments / levées along the banks\n• drainage ditches showing low-lying, easily flooded land` },
      { q: `Applied Fieldwork Enquiry — investigating how a river changes downstream.\n\nAt each site, students timed a float three times over a 10 m stretch of river and used the results to work out the velocity.\n\nGive one reason why the students repeated their measurement three times at each site, and explain why this improves their results.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for the reason, 1 mark for development. For example:\n• to calculate a mean / average (1) so one unusual result (e.g. the float catching on a rock or in an eddy) has less effect, making results more reliable/accurate (1)\n• to identify anomalies (1) which can then be removed before working out velocity (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 8: a map of China. The Yangtze River flows from the Tibetan Plateau in the west eastwards across the country to the East China Sea at Shanghai. The Three Gorges Dam is marked on the river near the city of Yichang in Hubei province, about 1,000 km west of Shanghai. The city of Chongqing lies upstream of the dam, about 600 km to the west.\n\nDescribe the location of the Three Gorges Dam.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark each for any two:\n• on the Yangtze River\n• in central China / Hubei province\n• near Yichang\n• about 1,000 km west / inland of Shanghai\n• downstream / east of Chongqing (about 600 km)\nMax 1 for a vague answer such as "in China".` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 9 (data written for this practice question): river discharge (cumecs) of the River Usk at Brecon, recorded every 4 hours after a storm. The heaviest rainfall fell at 04:00.\n04:00 — 9 | 08:00 — 18 | 12:00 — 35 | 16:00 — 51 | 20:00 — 44 | 24:00 — 30 | 04:00 next day — 19\n\nComplete the sentences.\n"The peak discharge was ___ cumecs."\n"The lag time was ___ hours."\n"Between 12:00 and 16:00 discharge increased by ___ cumecs."`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `1 mark each:\n• 51 (cumecs) (1)\n• 12 (hours) — 04:00 to 16:00 (1)\n• 16 (cumecs) — 51 − 35 (1)` },
      { q: `Applied Fieldwork Enquiry — investigating how a river changes downstream.\n\nTable 2 (data written for this practice question): channel width at five sites, from site 1 near the source to site 5 furthest downstream.\nSite 1: 1.2 m | Site 2: 2.0 m | Site 3: 4.2 m | Site 4: 3.6 m | Site 5: 5.4 m\n\nDescribe the changes in channel width downstream shown in Table 2. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark each for:\n• overall trend: the channel gets wider downstream (1)\n• use of data, e.g. width increases from 1.2 m at site 1 to 5.4 m at site 5 / a 4.2 m increase / 4.5 times wider (1)\n• anomaly: width falls between site 3 (4.2 m) and site 4 (3.6 m) (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nOS map extract (1:50,000), described: Area A covers grid squares 8432 and 8532 in the upper Wye valley. A narrow stream flows south-east. The contour lines are very close together on both sides of the stream and bend sharply upstream (V-shapes pointing uphill) where they cross it. Ridges of high land stick out alternately from the east and west sides of the valley so the stream zig-zags between them. There are no settlements, only a footpath and a few sheepfolds.\n\nDescribe the physical features of the river valley in Area A.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark each for any three:\n• steep valley sides (contours close together)\n• V-shaped valley (contours form V-shapes pointing upstream)\n• narrow valley floor / narrow stream\n• interlocking spurs (ridges from alternate sides)\n• the stream winds/zig-zags around the spurs\n• high, upland area / no floodplain` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 10: a photograph of the upper course of a stream on Snowdon. The stream is narrow and shallow and flows over large, angular boulders. Further downstream, at the mouth of the river, the bed is made of small, rounded pebbles and sand.\n\nExplain why the size and shape of the river's load changes from source to mouth.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark each for up to three linked points:\n• attrition — stones carried by the river knock against each other (1)\n• so they break into smaller pieces and their edges are chipped off, becoming more rounded (1)\n• the further the load travels downstream, the longer it has been eroded, so it is smallest/roundest at the mouth (1)\n• abrasion against the bed also smooths and wears down the load (1)\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 11: a diagram of High Force waterfall on the River Tees. A layer of hard dolerite rock (the Whin Sill) lies on top of softer layers of limestone, sandstone and shale. The soft rock below the lip has been worn back, leaving an overhang of hard rock. Below the waterfall is a deep plunge pool and a steep-sided gorge about 700 m long.\n\nExplain how the waterfall in Figure 11 was formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Credit linked explanation, 1 mark per valid point, max 4:\n• the river flows over a band of hard rock (dolerite) lying above softer rock (1)\n• the softer rock is eroded more quickly by hydraulic action and abrasion (1)\n• this undercuts the hard rock, creating an overhang (1)\n• the overhang becomes unsupported and collapses into the plunge pool (1)\n• the falling rocks are swirled around, deepening the plunge pool by abrasion (1)\n• the process repeats so the waterfall retreats upstream, leaving a gorge (1)\nCredit a sequence of annotated diagrams.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nOS map extract (1:25,000), described: in grid square 4527 a river forms a very tight meander loop with a neck of land only about 40 m wide. In the next square to the east there is a crescent-shaped lake that is no longer joined to the river.\n\nExplain how a crescent-shaped lake like this (an ox-bow lake) is formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Credit linked explanation, 1 mark per valid point, max 4:\n• the fastest flow is on the outside of the meander bend, causing lateral erosion (hydraulic action/abrasion) (1)\n• deposition happens on the inside bend where the water is slower (1)\n• erosion on the outside bends makes the neck of the meander narrower (1)\n• during a flood the river cuts through the neck, taking the shortest, straightest course (1)\n• deposition seals off the ends of the old loop from the new channel (1)\n• leaving a crescent-shaped ox-bow lake, which may later dry up (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 12: a cross-section of the lower course of the Mississippi River. The river channel is bordered on each side by a natural raised bank of silt, slightly higher than the flat floodplain behind it. The banks are made of coarser sediment and the floodplain further away is made of fine clay.\n\nExplain how the raised banks (levées) shown in Figure 12 are formed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Credit linked explanation, 1 mark per valid point, max 4:\n• when the river floods, water spreads out over the floodplain (1)\n• the water loses energy / slows down due to friction as it leaves the channel (1)\n• so it deposits its load (1)\n• the heaviest/coarsest material is deposited first, next to the channel (1)\n• finer material (clay/silt) is carried further and deposited on the floodplain (1)\n• after repeated floods the deposits build up into raised banks (levées) (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 7 (data written for this practice question): two storm hydrographs after the same storm. River A drains a forested basin with permeable sandstone: discharge rises slowly from 4 cumecs to a peak of 22 cumecs, lag time 14 hours. River B drains an urban basin on impermeable clay: discharge rises very steeply from 5 cumecs to a peak of 60 cumecs, lag time 5 hours, and returns to normal quickly.\n\nCompare the hydrographs of River A and River B. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Credit comparisons (both rivers referred to), 1 mark per valid point, max 4; max 2 without data:\n• River B has a much higher peak discharge — 60 cumecs compared with 22 cumecs (38 cumecs more / nearly three times) (1)\n• River B has a shorter lag time — 5 hours compared with 14 hours (9 hours shorter) (1)\n• River B has a steeper rising limb than River A (1)\n• River B has a steeper falling limb / returns to normal faster (1)\n• both start from a similar base flow (4–5 cumecs) (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 13: a photograph of Boscastle, north Cornwall, on 16 August 2004. Floodwater from the River Valency is pouring through the village, carrying cars and trees. The river runs through a narrow, steep-sided valley above the village. Over 180 mm of rain fell in about five hours on ground already wet from earlier rain.\n\nGive two physical reasons why the flood in Boscastle happened so quickly.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a reason + 1 mark for development):\n• intense rainfall — a very large amount fell in a short time (1) so the rain could not infiltrate fast enough and became surface runoff (1)\n• the ground was already saturated (1) so water could not soak in and flowed over the surface into the river (1)\n• steep valley sides (1) mean water flows quickly downhill to the river, giving a short lag time (1)\n• small drainage basin / narrow valley (1) so water reaches the channel and the village rapidly / it is confined and rises quickly (1)\n• several streams (e.g. Valency and Jordan) meet at the village (1) so their discharges combine (1)\nDo not credit human reasons (e.g. cars blocking the bridge).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nIn December 2015 Storm Desmond brought record rainfall to Cumbria; 341 mm fell in 24 hours at Honister Pass. Rivers such as the Eden, the Derwent and the Greta burst their banks.\n\nDescribe the effects of a flood in the UK that you have studied. Name the river.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Band 2 (3–4 marks): clear, detailed description of a range of effects (social, economic and/or environmental) that are specific to the named flood.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): basic description of effects; generic statements that could apply to any flood.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content (Storm Desmond, 2015):\n• thousands of homes flooded across Cumbria, around 2,000 properties in Carlisle (River Eden, Caldew and Petteril)\n• people evacuated to emergency shelters; some unable to return for months\n• bridges destroyed, e.g. Pooley Bridge; the A591 was closed after being washed away near Thirlmere\n• power cuts to tens of thousands of homes in Lancaster and Cumbria\n• businesses and tourism in Keswick and Cockermouth affected; high insurance costs\n• farmland covered in silt and debris; livestock lost\nCredit other named UK floods, e.g. Boscastle 2004, Somerset Levels 2014.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 14: a land-use zoning plan for the floodplain of a river. Next to the river channel the land is marked 'playing fields, parkland and nature reserve'. Further away is a zone marked 'car parks and low-value buildings'. On the highest land, furthest from the river, is a zone marked 'housing, schools and hospital'.\n\nExplain how the land-use zoning plan in Figure 14 can reduce the impact of flooding.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Credit linked explanation, 1 mark per valid point, max 4:\n• land closest to the river floods most often (1)\n• so it is used for things that are not badly damaged by floodwater, e.g. parks and playing fields (1)\n• this land can store floodwater, reducing flood levels downstream (1)\n• expensive/important buildings such as homes and hospitals are on higher land away from the river (1)\n• so fewer people are at risk and the cost of damage is lower (1)\n• it is cheaper than hard engineering and lets the river flood naturally (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFact box: the Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze River, China. It is 181 m high and over 2 km long. Its reservoir stretches over 600 km upstream. About 1.3 million people were moved from their homes, and many towns, farmland and archaeological sites were flooded by the reservoir.\n\nGive two reasons why large dams like the Three Gorges Dam are not always popular.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a reason + 1 mark for development):\n• people are forced to move (1) losing their homes, land and communities — about 1.3 million people at Three Gorges (1)\n• farmland and settlements are flooded by the reservoir (1) so people lose their livelihoods / cultural sites are lost (1)\n• sediment is trapped behind the dam (1) so farmland downstream is less fertile / the delta and coast erode (1)\n• very expensive to build (1) money could be spent on other needs (1)\n• damages ecosystems (1) e.g. fish cannot migrate; habitats flooded; species such as the river dolphin harmed (1)\n• risk of dam failure / landslides around the reservoir (1) putting people downstream at risk (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 15: an information panel from the 'Slowing the Flow' scheme at Pickering, North Yorkshire. Upstream of the town, hundreds of small leaky dams made of logs and heather bales have been built across streams on the moors, trees have been planted, and a large earth embankment (bund) creates a flood storage area that can hold around 120,000 m³ of water. On Boxing Day 2015, when nearby towns flooded, Pickering did not.\n\nExplain how a soft engineering scheme like the one in Figure 15 reduces the risk of flooding in a town.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Credit linked explanation, 1 mark per valid point, max 4:\n• leaky dams slow the flow of water down the streams (1)\n• so water reaches the town more slowly, increasing lag time and lowering peak discharge (1)\n• planting trees increases interception and evapotranspiration (1)\n• tree roots increase infiltration, so less surface runoff reaches the river (1)\n• the flood storage area holds back floodwater upstream and releases it slowly (1)\n• works with natural processes, so it is cheaper and better for wildlife than building a concrete wall in the town (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nOS map extract (1:25,000), described: Area C, between eastings 32–34 and northings 55–56, shows a town on the floodplain of the River Derwent. The river flows through the middle of the area at about 40 m above sea level. On both sides of the river within 300 m there are a hospital (329556), a fire station (331554), a school, a supermarket and car parks, a caravan site (338557) and rows of terraced housing. There are no embankments shown. To the north, the land rises steeply to 200 m with woodland and a reservoir. A main road (A66) and railway cross the river on bridges at 335552.\n\nStudy Area C. Analyse the risk to people if the River Derwent were to flood in Area C. Use map evidence in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `Band 2 (3–4 marks): analysis that links several features at risk to their consequences, using accurate map evidence (place names, grid references or heights).\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): lists features at risk with limited map evidence or limited links to consequences.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• the town is low-lying (about 40 m) on a flat floodplain beside the river with no embankments, so the risk is high\n• the hospital (329556) and fire station (331554) are close to the river — emergency services may be cut off just when they are needed\n• terraced housing and the caravan site (338557) are near the river; people may need to evacuate and caravans offer little protection\n• the A66 and railway bridges (335552) may be closed, cutting off access and making rescue harder\n• steep land to the north will send runoff quickly into the river, so floods could rise fast with little warning` },
      { q: `Applied Fieldwork Enquiry — investigating how a river changes downstream.\n\nTable 3 (data written for this practice question): results from five sites, from site 1 near the source to site 5 downstream.\nSite 1: width 1.2 m, mean depth 0.08 m, velocity 0.18 m/s, mean pebble length 14 cm\nSite 3: width 4.2 m, mean depth 0.23 m, velocity 0.31 m/s, mean pebble length 8 cm\nSite 5: width 5.4 m, mean depth 0.41 m, velocity 0.52 m/s, mean pebble length 4 cm\nThe Bradshaw model predicts that width, depth and velocity increase downstream and load particle size decreases.\n\nTo what extent do the results in Table 3 support the Bradshaw model? Use data in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Band 2 (3–4 marks): a clear judgement supported by data for several variables; may note limitations (only three sites shown, one river, one day).\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements of change with little or no data; no clear judgement.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• the results largely support the model\n• width increases from 1.2 m to 5.4 m and depth from 0.08 m to 0.41 m\n• velocity increases from 0.18 m/s to 0.52 m/s, as the model predicts\n• pebble length decreases from 14 cm to 4 cm, as the model predicts\n• however, only three sites are shown and a single visit may not be representative, so conclusions are limited` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 16: a graph of UK winter rainfall projections. Climate models suggest that by the 2070s UK winters could be wetter on average, with more days of very heavy rainfall, while summers could be drier.\n\nExplain why climate change might increase the risk of river flooding in the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Credit linked explanation, 1 mark per valid point, max 4:\n• warmer air can hold more moisture (1)\n• so storms bring more intense / heavier rainfall (1)\n• wetter winters mean the ground is more often saturated (1)\n• so more rain becomes surface runoff and reaches rivers quickly, raising peak discharge (1)\n• drier summers bake the ground hard, so sudden summer storms run off rather than infiltrating (1)\n• rising sea levels make tidal flooding in estuaries (e.g. the Thames, the Severn) more likely (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 17 (data written for this practice question): a storm hydrograph for the River Taff at Pontypridd. Rainfall began at 06:00 and was heaviest at 10:00 (15 mm). Discharge was 20 cumecs at 06:00, rose to a peak of 110 cumecs at 18:00, and fell to 45 cumecs by 06:00 the next day.\n\nChoose the correct answer in each statement.\nThe peak discharge was (45 / 110 / 15) cumecs.\nThe lag time was (8 / 12 / 18) hours.\nThe rising limb lasted (12 / 8 / 10) hours.\nBetween the peak and 06:00 the next day, discharge fell by (65 / 90 / 25) cumecs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `1 mark each:\n• 110 cumecs (1)\n• 8 hours (10:00 to 18:00) (1)\n• 12 hours (06:00 to 18:00) (1)\n• 65 cumecs (110 − 45) (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 22: a systems diagram of a drainage basin in mid-Wales. Precipitation falls onto trees and the ground. Arrows show water moving down into the soil, then deeper into the rock, and sideways through the soil and rock towards the river. Another arrow shows water flowing over the surface of the ground to the river. Arrows also show water returning to the atmosphere from plants and from the ground surface.\n\nDescribe how water is transferred from where precipitation lands to the river channel in Figure 22.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point using correct terms, max 4:\n• interception — rain is caught by leaves; some drips or flows down stems to the ground (stemflow) (1)\n• infiltration — water soaks into the soil (1)\n• throughflow — water moves sideways through the soil to the river (1)\n• percolation — water moves down into the rock (1)\n• groundwater flow — water moves slowly through the rock to the river (1)\n• surface runoff / overland flow — water flows over the surface when the ground is saturated or impermeable (1)\nDo not credit outputs (evaporation, transpiration) as transfers to the channel.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 18: a series of three diagrams showing a meander on the River Wye near Hay-on-Wye at different times. In the first, the bend is gentle. In the second, the outside of the bend has a steep river cliff and the inside has a gently sloping beach of sand and gravel. In the third, the bend has migrated across the floodplain and the neck of the meander has become narrower.\n\nExplain how the processes of erosion and deposition change the shape of a meander over time.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 3 marks, AO2 3 marks.\nBand 3 (5–6 marks): thorough explanation of erosion and deposition, clearly linked to changes in shape over time; accurate use of terms (thalweg, helicoidal flow, river cliff, slip-off slope, lateral erosion).\nBand 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of erosion and/or deposition with some links to changes in shape; some imbalance.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): basic description of meander features with little explanation of processes.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• fastest current (thalweg) swings to the outside of the bend, with more energy\n• lateral erosion by hydraulic action and abrasion undercuts the outer bank, forming a river cliff\n• slower water on the inside bend loses energy and deposits sediment, forming a slip-off slope / point bar\n• helicoidal flow carries eroded material from the outer bank to the inner bank of the next bend\n• over time bends become more pronounced and migrate sideways and downstream across the floodplain\n• the neck narrows and may be cut through in a flood, forming an ox-bow lake` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nIn 1998 about two-thirds of Bangladesh was flooded when the Ganges, Brahmaputra and Meghna rivers burst their banks; over 1,000 people died and millions were made homeless. In the same decade, floods in the UK caused far fewer deaths.\n\nExplain why the effects of river flooding are often greater in LICs than in HICs. Use examples you have studied.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 3 marks, AO2 3 marks.\nBand 3 (5–6 marks): thorough, well-linked explanation of several reasons, supported by specific examples from contrasting countries.\nBand 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of some reasons with some use of examples; may be imbalanced.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): basic reasons, mostly descriptive, with little or no exemplification.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• many people in LICs live on low-lying floodplains and deltas because it is fertile land (e.g. 80% of Bangladesh is floodplain)\n• housing is poorly built so it is easily destroyed\n• limited money for flood defences, warning systems and embankments\n• poor communications and transport make evacuation and rescue slow\n• flooding contaminates water supplies, spreading diseases such as cholera; limited healthcare\n• crops and livestock are lost, leading to food shortages for subsistence farmers\n• HICs (e.g. UK, Cumbria 2015) have Environment Agency warnings, emergency services, insurance and money to rebuild, so deaths are fewer although economic costs can be high` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 19: the Jubilee River, a flood relief channel on the River Thames. It is 11.6 km long and opened in 2002 at a cost of about £110 million. It takes excess water from the Thames around Maidenhead, Windsor and Eton, protecting about 3,000 properties. It was landscaped with reed beds, ponds and footpaths. In 2003 and 2014, some residents in villages downstream such as Datchet and Wraysbury complained that the channel had made flooding worse for them.\n\nShould flood relief channels like the Jubilee River be used to protect other towns in the UK? Justify your views. You may also use examples you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4 marks, AO3 4 marks.\nBand 3 (7–8 marks): a well-reasoned, balanced evaluation of advantages and disadvantages, with thorough use of Figure 19 and other examples, leading to a clear, well-justified conclusion.\nBand 2 (4–6 marks): a clear evaluation with some use of the resource and/or examples; a conclusion is given but may be partly justified.\nBand 1 (1–3 marks): basic points, mostly one-sided or descriptive, with limited use of evidence; little or no conclusion.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nFor:\n• protects large numbers of homes and businesses (about 3,000 properties)\n• landscaping creates wildlife habitats and leisure space, so it is more environmentally friendly than concrete walls\n• long-term protection reduces insurance costs and damage\nAgainst:\n• very expensive (£110 million) — may not be affordable or cost-effective for small towns\n• may move the problem downstream (Datchet, Wraysbury) — causes conflict between communities\n• needs maintenance; may not cope with extreme events as climate change increases rainfall\n• soft engineering (e.g. Slowing the Flow at Pickering, about £2 million) may be cheaper and more sustainable upstream\nConclusion: may depend on the size of the town, the value of property protected and whether impacts downstream can be managed.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 20: the Somerset Levels flood, winter 2013–14. After weeks of heavy rain, the Rivers Parrett and Tone overflowed and large areas of low-lying farmland were under water for over a month. Villages such as Muchelney were cut off. Many local people blamed the lack of dredging of the rivers. In 2014 about 8 km of the Parrett and Tone were dredged. Some scientists argued that dredging would not stop floods of this size, and that changing land use upstream and allowing some areas to flood would be better.\n\n"Dredging is the best way to manage flood risk on the Somerset Levels." How far do you agree with this statement? Justify your views.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4 marks, AO3 4 marks.\nBand 3 (7–8 marks): a well-reasoned, balanced evaluation of dredging and alternative strategies, using Figure 20 and own knowledge, with a clear, well-justified conclusion about how far the candidate agrees.\nBand 2 (4–6 marks): a clear evaluation with some reference to alternatives and to the resource; conclusion partly justified.\nBand 1 (1–3 marks): basic, largely one-sided points with little use of evidence; little or no conclusion.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nAgree:\n• removing silt increases the capacity of the channel so it can carry more water away\n• supported by local farmers and residents; quick, visible action\n• helps water drain off the fields faster after a flood\nDisagree:\n• the Levels are very flat and near sea level, so water drains slowly whatever the channel size; tides can block outflow\n• silt builds up again, so dredging must be repeated and is costly\n• rainfall in 2013–14 was so extreme that dredging alone could not have prevented the flood\n• soft engineering upstream (tree planting, changes to farming to reduce runoff) and washlands can store water\n• other hard engineering such as pumping stations, raised roads or a tidal barrier at Bridgwater may also be needed\nConclusion: dredging is part of the answer but an integrated approach is likely to be more sustainable.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Rivers and river management.\n\nFigure 21: fact file on the Three Gorges Dam, Yangtze River, China.\n• Height 181 m; length over 2 km; fully operational by 2012\n• Generates up to 22,500 MW of hydro-electric power, reducing the use of coal\n• Designed to protect millions of people living downstream from floods\n• Allows large ships to travel inland to Chongqing\n• About 1.3 million people relocated; towns, farmland and archaeological sites flooded\n• Traps sediment behind the dam; landslides have occurred around the reservoir\n\nDo the benefits of the Three Gorges Dam outweigh the costs? Justify your views.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4 marks, AO3 4 marks.\nBand 3 (7–8 marks): a well-reasoned, balanced evaluation of social, economic and environmental benefits and costs, making thorough use of Figure 21 and own knowledge, with a clear, well-justified conclusion.\nBand 2 (4–6 marks): a clear evaluation with some use of the resource; may be imbalanced; conclusion partly justified.\nBand 1 (1–3 marks): basic points, mostly lifted from the resource, with little evaluation; little or no conclusion.\n0: nothing worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\nBenefits:\n• flood protection for millions downstream along the middle and lower Yangtze, where past floods killed many people\n• renewable electricity (22,500 MW) supports industry and reduces coal burning and air pollution\n• navigation — larger ships reach Chongqing, helping trade and development inland\nCosts:\n• about 1.3 million people relocated, losing homes, land and communities; some new land was poorer\n• cultural and archaeological sites lost\n• sediment trapped — less fertile floodplain soils downstream; erosion of the delta\n• landslides and pollution build-up in the reservoir; harm to wildlife\nConclusion: candidates may argue benefits outweigh costs at a national scale, but costs fall heavily on local people and the environment; credit either view if justified.` }
    ]
  },
  '2.3': {
    name: 'Weather and Climate',
    questions: [
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nThe weather in the UK changes from day to day because different air masses arrive over the country.\n\nWhich of the following is the correct definition of an air mass?\nA  A band of cloud and rain where warm air meets cold air\nB  A large body of air with similar temperature and humidity throughout\nC  An area of high pressure that stays over one place for many days\nD  The average weather conditions of a place over 30 years`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (a large body of air with similar temperature and humidity throughout) (1)\nNo other answer is acceptable.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 1 is a cross-section of the global atmospheric circulation (tri-cellular model) for the northern hemisphere. Air rises at the equator, flows north high in the atmosphere and sinks at about 30°N, completing the Hadley cell. A second cell (Ferrel) lies between 30°N and 60°N and a third (Polar) cell lies between 60°N and the North Pole.\n\nName the type of surface pressure found at 30°N in Figure 1.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `High pressure / subtropical high pressure (1)\nDo not credit 'low pressure'.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 2 is a table of tropical storm wind speeds on the Saffir-Simpson scale:\nCategory 1: 119–153 km/h\nCategory 2: 154–177 km/h\nCategory 3: 178–208 km/h\nCategory 4: 209–251 km/h\nCategory 5: 252 km/h or more\nTyphoon Haiyan reached the Philippines on 8 November 2013 with sustained winds of about 315 km/h.\n\nUsing Figure 2, state the category of Typhoon Haiyan at landfall.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Category 5 (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 3 describes a synoptic chart for 1200 on a day in January (data written for this practice question). A low pressure centre of 976 mb lies off north-west Scotland. A warm front runs south-east across northern England and a cold front trails south-west across Wales and Cornwall. Weather station P in eastern England lies ahead of the warm front; weather station R in the West Midlands lies between the warm front and the cold front; weather station Q in Cornwall lies behind the cold front.\n\nIdentify which station, P, Q or R, is in the warm sector of the depression.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `R (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 4 is a climate table for Station W, a coastal weather station in western Britain (data written for this practice question).\nMonth: J F M A M J J A S O N D\nTemperature (°C): 6 6 7 9 11 14 15 15 14 12 9 7\nRainfall (mm): 140 105 100 70 70 70 75 90 105 140 140 150\n\nUsing Figure 4, calculate the annual temperature range for Station W. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: highest monthly temperature 15 °C (July/August) − lowest 6 °C (January/February) (1)\nAnswer: 9 °C (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 5 is a climate table for Station E, a lowland weather station in eastern England (data written for this practice question).\nMonth: J F M A M J J A S O N D\nTemperature (°C): 4 4 7 9 12 15 18 17 15 11 7 5\nRainfall (mm): 50 38 40 42 45 50 48 52 52 55 55 52\n\nUsing Figure 5, calculate the total annual rainfall for Station E. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 50 + 38 + 40 + 42 + 45 + 50 + 48 + 52 + 52 + 55 + 55 + 52 (1)\nAnswer: 579 mm (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 6 is a world map showing the tracks of tropical storms between 1985 and 2015. The tracks begin over the oceans between about 5° and 20° north and south of the equator: in the North Atlantic and Caribbean, the eastern Pacific off Mexico, the western Pacific east of the Philippines, the Bay of Bengal and Arabian Sea, the south-west Indian Ocean near Madagascar and the seas around northern Australia. There are almost no tracks in the South Atlantic or within 5° of the equator. Most tracks move westwards and then curve away from the equator.\n\nDescribe the distribution of tropical storms shown in Figure 6.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid descriptive point, up to 2 marks:\n• Found between about 5° and 20° north and south of the equator / in the tropics (1)\n• Form over oceans, e.g. Caribbean, western Pacific, Bay of Bengal (1)\n• None within 5° of the equator / almost none in the South Atlantic (1)\n• Tracks move westwards and then curve away from the equator / polewards (1)\nNo credit for explanation of why they form there.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nIn February 2023 an anticyclone was centred over the UK for over a week. The Met Office reported clear skies at night, with frost and freezing fog in many inland valleys.\n\nGive two characteristics of the weather brought by a winter anticyclone in the UK.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each characteristic, up to 2 marks:\n• Clear skies / little cloud (1)\n• Dry / little or no precipitation (1)\n• Cold nights / temperatures fall below freezing (1)\n• Frost / ice (1)\n• Fog / freezing fog, especially in valleys (1)\n• Light winds / calm (1)\n• Cold days with low sun (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 7 describes a synoptic chart of the British Isles (data written for this practice question). Over the Hebrides the isobars are drawn 4 mb apart and are very close together, about 50 km apart. Over south-east England the same isobars are spread out, about 250 km apart.\n\nSuggest why wind speeds are higher over the Hebrides than over south-east England.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• Isobars are closer together over the Hebrides (1)\n• This means a steeper pressure gradient / greater change in pressure over a short distance, so air moves faster from high to low pressure (1)\nAccept: 'the pressure changes more quickly over the distance' for the second mark.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 8 lists the air masses that affect the UK:\nArctic maritime — from the north\nPolar maritime — from the north-west\nPolar continental — from the east\nTropical continental — from the south-east\nTropical maritime — from the south-west\n\nChoose the two correct statements:\nA  Polar continental air brings hot, dry weather in summer\nB  Tropical maritime air is warm and moist\nC  Polar maritime air crosses the Atlantic Ocean and brings showers\nD  Tropical continental air is cold and wet\nE  Arctic maritime air brings the warmest weather in winter`, marks: 2, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)\nC (1)\nIf more than two letters are chosen, deduct 1 mark for each incorrect answer.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nThe Sahara and Arabian deserts both lie at about 20–30° north of the equator, where the descending limb of the Hadley cell reaches the ground.\n\nExplain why the sinking air at about 30° north leads to dry conditions.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `• Sinking air creates high pressure (1)\n• Air warms as it sinks so clouds do not form / no condensation / no rain (1)\nAccept: 'air is not rising, so it cannot cool to its dew point and form rain clouds'.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nStudents at a school in Cardiff used a rain gauge in the school grounds for one week in October (data written for this practice question).\nDay: Mon Tue Wed Thu Fri Sat Sun\nRainfall (mm): 0 4.2 12.6 0.4 0 0 8.8\n\nCalculate the mean daily rainfall for the week. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: total 26.0 mm ÷ 7 (1)\nAnswer: 3.7 mm (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Accept 3.71 for 1 mark only if not rounded to one decimal place.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 4 is a climate table for Station W, a coastal weather station in western Britain (data written for this practice question).\nMonth: J F M A M J J A S O N D\nRainfall (mm): 140 105 100 70 70 70 75 90 105 140 140 150\nThe total annual rainfall is 1255 mm.\n\nCalculate the percentage of the annual rainfall that falls in the winter months of December, January and February. Give your answer to the nearest whole number.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 150 + 140 + 105 = 395 mm; 395 ÷ 1255 × 100 (1)\nAnswer: 31% (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 9 is a rainfall table for Station K in West Africa, at about 12° north of the equator (data written for this practice question).\nMonth: J F M A M J J A S O N D\nRainfall (mm): 0 0 0 1 6 60 180 290 140 15 0 0\n\nSuggest why Station K receives most of its rainfall between June and September.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• The ITCZ / zone of low pressure / rising air of the Hadley cell moves north in the northern hemisphere summer (1)\n• Rising air cools and condenses, giving convectional rain / thunderstorms over Station K (1)\nAccept: 'the overhead sun moves north, so the area is heated strongly and air rises' for the first mark.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nHurricane Ian (2022) weakened from Category 4 to a tropical storm within about a day of crossing the coast of Florida.\n\nGive two reasons why tropical storms weaken after they reach land.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each reason, up to 2 marks:\n• They lose their supply of warm, moist air / energy from warm sea water (1)\n• Less evaporation over land so less latent heat released / less condensation (1)\n• Friction with the land surface (hills, buildings, trees) slows the winds (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nOn 19 July 2022 a temperature of 40.3 °C was recorded at Coningsby in Lincolnshire, the highest ever recorded in the UK. Hot air had moved north to the UK from North Africa and southern Europe.\n\nName the air mass that brought this heatwave and give one of its characteristics.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `• Tropical continental (1)\n• One characteristic: hot / dry / low humidity / clear skies / formed over land (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nYou have carried out fieldwork to compare rainfall in two places in your school grounds using rain gauges.\n\nSuggest one way the accuracy of your rainfall data could be improved, and explain why.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid improvement, 1 mark for the reason. For example:\n• Place the gauge in the open away from buildings and trees (1) so rain is not blocked or does not drip in from leaves (1)\n• Sink the gauge into the ground / raise it so the rim is about 30 cm above the surface (1) so rain does not splash in from the ground (1)\n• Read the gauge at the same time every day / at eye level (1) to avoid evaporation losses / reading errors (1)\n• Use several gauges and calculate a mean (1) to reduce the effect of anomalies (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 10 compares two weather stations in Britain (data written for this practice question).\nStation W (west coast): annual rainfall 1255 mm; January mean temperature 6 °C; July mean temperature 15 °C.\nStation E (eastern lowland): annual rainfall 579 mm; January mean temperature 4 °C; July mean temperature 18 °C.\n\nComplete the sentences using Figure 10.\n"Station W receives ___ mm more rain per year than Station E."\n"The annual temperature range at Station E is ___ °C."\n"The station with the warmer winters is Station ___."`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `• 676 mm (1)\n• 14 °C (1)\n• W (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 11 shows hourly observations at a weather station in Wales as a depression passed on a day in November (data written for this practice question).\n0900: pressure 994 mb; temperature 12 °C; wind south-westerly; light drizzle; total cloud cover.\n1200: pressure 993 mb; temperature 12 °C; wind veering; heavy rain; cumulonimbus cloud.\n1500: pressure 999 mb; temperature 7 °C; wind north-westerly; sunny spells and showers.\n\nDescribe how the weather changed as the cold front passed the station.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid point, up to 3 marks. Data use is expected for full marks:\n• Temperature fell, from 12 °C to 7 °C (1)\n• Pressure rose after the front, from 993 to 999 mb (1)\n• Wind direction changed / veered from south-west to north-west (1)\n• Heavy rain at the front, then clearer skies with showers (1)\n• Cloud changed from total cover / cumulonimbus to broken cloud (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nOn 4–5 December 2015 a barometer in Cumbria recorded pressure falling from 1016 mb to 984 mb over 16 hours as Storm Desmond approached (pressure readings written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the mean rate of pressure fall per hour, and state what this rapid fall suggests about the weather to come.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Working: 1016 − 984 = 32 mb; 32 ÷ 16 (1)\n• Answer: 2 mb per hour (1)\n• A deep / rapidly deepening depression is approaching, bringing strong winds and heavy rain (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nPlymouth in south-west England (about 50°N) has a mean January temperature of about 8 °C. Places in eastern Canada at the same latitude often have January temperatures below −5 °C.\n\nExplain why the UK has milder winters than places in eastern Canada at the same latitude.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid explanatory point, up to 3 marks:\n• The North Atlantic Drift / Gulf Stream brings warm water from the Caribbean towards the UK (1) and warms the air above it (1)\n• The prevailing south-westerly winds blow over this warm ocean, bringing mild air to the UK (1)\n• The sea loses heat slowly in winter, so maritime areas stay milder (1)\n• Eastern Canada is affected by cold air from the continental interior / the cold Labrador Current (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 12 is a world map of climate zones. A belt of tropical rainforest climate lies within about 10° of the equator across South America (the Amazon), Central Africa (the Congo Basin) and South-East Asia. Hot desert climates lie at about 15–30° north and south, including the Sahara, Arabian, Kalahari and Australian deserts. Temperate climates cover most of Europe, the eastern USA, southern Chile and New Zealand between about 40° and 60°. Polar climates cover Antarctica, Greenland and the far north of Canada and Russia.\n\nDescribe the pattern of climate zones shown in Figure 12.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear description of the overall pattern of zones arranged in bands by latitude, with accurate use of named places and latitudes from Figure 12.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple points, e.g. 'deserts are in Africa', with little reference to latitude or pattern.\nIndicative content:\n• Zones are arranged in broad bands parallel to the equator / in a similar order north and south of the equator\n• Tropical rainforest near the equator (within 10°), e.g. Amazon, Congo Basin\n• Hot deserts at about 15–30° N and S, e.g. Sahara, Kalahari, Australia\n• Temperate zones between about 40° and 60°, e.g. Europe, New Zealand\n• Polar climates at high latitudes, e.g. Antarctica, Greenland\n• Pattern is broken by continents / zones vary in width; e.g. deserts often on the western side of continents\nNo credit for explanation.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 1 is a cross-section of the tri-cellular model of global atmospheric circulation, showing the Hadley, Ferrel and Polar cells in each hemisphere.\n\nExplain how the global circulation of the atmosphere creates belts of high and low pressure at the Earth's surface.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation linking rising air to low pressure and sinking air to high pressure, applied to at least two named latitudes.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points, e.g. 'the equator has low pressure', with limited explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Intense heating at the equator makes air rise, creating low pressure (the ITCZ)\n• The air cools and spreads out high in the atmosphere, then sinks at about 30° giving subtropical high pressure\n• Surface winds blow from high to low pressure (trade winds back to the equator; westerlies towards 60°)\n• At about 60° warm westerlies meet cold polar air and rise at the polar front, giving low pressure\n• Cold dense air sinks at the poles, giving high pressure` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 10 compares two weather stations in Britain (data written for this practice question).\nStation W (west coast): annual rainfall 1255 mm; wettest month December (150 mm); January 6 °C; July 15 °C; annual range 9 °C.\nStation E (eastern lowland): annual rainfall 579 mm; wettest months October and November (55 mm); January 4 °C; July 18 °C; annual range 14 °C.\n\nCompare the climates of Station W and Station E. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear comparisons of both temperature and rainfall, using comparative language and accurate data from Figure 10.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Separate descriptions of each station, or comparison of only one variable, with little data.\nIndicative content:\n• W is much wetter than E: 1255 mm compared with 579 mm (more than twice as much / 676 mm more)\n• W has a winter rainfall peak (150 mm in December); E's rainfall is spread more evenly through the year\n• W has milder winters (6 °C v 4 °C) but cooler summers (15 °C v 18 °C)\n• E has a larger annual temperature range (14 °C v 9 °C), so W is more maritime / E more continental` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 13 describes three synoptic charts at 12-hour intervals (data written for this practice question). At 0000 a depression of 980 mb is centred west of Ireland with its warm front over Cornwall. At 1200 the warm front has crossed Birmingham and the warm sector lies over the Midlands. At 2400 the cold front has passed Birmingham and the low is centred over the North Sea.\n\nDescribe the sequence of weather that people in Birmingham would experience during these 24 hours.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Accurate, sequenced description of weather ahead of the warm front, in the warm sector and after the cold front, covering several elements (cloud, rain, temperature, wind, pressure).\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Some correct weather features but not clearly sequenced or linked to the fronts.\nIndicative content:\n• Ahead of the warm front: pressure falling, cloud thickening and lowering (cirrus to nimbostratus), steady rain\n• Warm sector: milder temperatures, rain eases to drizzle, wind veers to south-westerly\n• Cold front: heavy rain / thunderstorms from cumulonimbus, gusty winds, temperature falls\n• After the cold front: pressure rises, colder, clearer skies with showers, north-westerly wind` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nMost of the UK's rainfall arrives with depressions that form over the North Atlantic Ocean, where warm tropical maritime air meets cold polar maritime air.\n\nExplain why depressions bring rain to the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of the frontal process, linking the uplift of warm air to cooling, condensation and rain at both fronts.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points, e.g. 'warm and cold air meet', with little explanation of the process.\nIndicative content:\n• Warm, moist air is less dense than cold air\n• At the warm front, warm air rises gently over the cold air; at the cold front, cold air undercuts and forces warm air up steeply\n• Rising air cools to its dew point and water vapour condenses to form clouds\n• Droplets grow and fall as frontal rain (steady at the warm front, heavy at the cold front)\n• Low pressure at the centre means air is rising throughout the system` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nIn December 2022 an anticyclone over the UK brought several nights below −10 °C in parts of Scotland, with dense freezing fog in the Thames Valley.\n\nExplain why winter anticyclones can bring frost and fog to the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear causal chain from sinking air and clear skies to heat loss at night and condensation near the ground.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple points with limited explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Air sinks in an anticyclone and warms, so clouds do not form / skies are clear\n• With no cloud, heat radiates away from the ground at night and long winter nights allow more cooling\n• Ground temperature falls below 0 °C, so water vapour freezes onto surfaces as frost\n• Air near the ground cools to its dew point and condenses, forming fog\n• Light winds mean the cold air does not mix; cold air drains into valleys, so fog and frost are worst there` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nStorm Desmond crossed northern Britain on 4–6 December 2015. Honister Pass in Cumbria recorded 341.4 mm of rain in 24 hours, a UK record.\n\nExplain the causes of a named extreme weather event in the UK that you have studied.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear, linked explanation of the meteorological and other causes, with specific located detail of a named event.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Generic causes, e.g. 'a lot of rain', with little or no specific detail.\nIndicative content (Storm Desmond, 2015, for example):\n• A deep Atlantic depression brought a long plume of warm, very moist tropical maritime air from the south-west (an 'atmospheric river')\n• The moist air was forced to rise over the Cumbrian fells, adding relief rainfall to frontal rainfall\n• The front stalled, so heavy rain fell on the same area for over 24 hours\n• The ground was already saturated after a wet November, so run-off was rapid into rivers such as the Eden and Kent\nCredit other named UK events, e.g. the 'Beast from the East' 2018, the July 2022 heatwave, the Somerset Levels floods 2014.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nTyphoon Haiyan struck the Philippines on 8 November 2013. A storm surge of up to about 5 m flooded the city of Tacloban on the island of Leyte.\n\nDescribe the effects on people of a named tropical storm you have studied.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A range of effects on people described with accurate, located detail and data for a named storm; may distinguish primary and secondary effects.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Generic effects, e.g. 'houses were destroyed', with little specific detail.\nIndicative content (Typhoon Haiyan, for example):\n• Over 6,000 people died, many drowned by the storm surge in Tacloban\n• About 4 million people were displaced / made homeless\n• Over 1 million houses damaged or destroyed\n• Airport, roads and power lines destroyed, so aid was delayed and electricity was lost for weeks\n• Shortages of food, clean water and medicine; looting\n• Loss of livelihoods, e.g. coconut plantations, fishing boats and rice crops destroyed\nCredit other named storms, e.g. Cyclone Idai 2019, Hurricane Katrina 2005.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 6 is a world map showing that tropical storms form over oceans between about 5° and 20° north and south of the equator, mainly in late summer and autumn.\n\nExplain why tropical storms form in these areas.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of several conditions, each linked to how it helps the storm to form or grow.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Conditions listed with little or no explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• Sea surface temperatures above about 26.5–27 °C provide heat and moisture; warm, moist air rises rapidly\n• Condensation releases latent heat, which powers the storm\n• At least 5° from the equator the Coriolis effect is strong enough to make the storm spin\n• Low wind shear allows the tall storm clouds to build vertically without being torn apart\n• Late summer and autumn is when the oceans are warmest\n• Low pressure areas linked to the ITCZ help air to rise` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nThe Met Office issues yellow, amber and red weather warnings. Before Storm Eunice in February 2022 it issued a red warning for wind in south-west England and South Wales a day in advance.\n\nExplain how forecasting and warnings can reduce the impact of extreme weather on people in the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of how warnings lead to actions that reduce impacts, with developed points.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple points, e.g. 'people know it is coming'.\nIndicative content:\n• Warnings give time to prepare, e.g. sandbags, moving belongings upstairs, securing loose objects\n• People can avoid travel / schools and events can close, reducing deaths from falling trees and debris\n• Emergency services and councils can position staff, gritters, pumps and flood barriers in advance\n• Flood warnings allow evacuation of people at risk\n• Colour-coded levels help people judge the seriousness of the risk; red means danger to life` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 14 describes weather observations at four UK stations at 0600 on a day in March (data written for this practice question).\nLerwick: 1028 mb; −2 °C; wind north-easterly 10 knots; snow showers.\nAberdeen: 1024 mb; 0 °C; wind easterly 20 knots; snow.\nCardiff: 1012 mb; 6 °C; wind southerly 15 knots; rain.\nPlymouth: 1008 mb; 9 °C; wind south-westerly 25 knots; heavy rain.\n\nChoose the correct answer in each statement.\nThe station with the highest pressure was (Lerwick / Aberdeen / Plymouth).\nThe temperature difference between Lerwick and Plymouth was (7 / 9 / 11) °C.\nThe strongest wind was recorded at (Aberdeen / Cardiff / Plymouth).\nThe air at Aberdeen was most likely to be (tropical maritime / polar continental / tropical continental).`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `• Lerwick (1)\n• 11 °C (1)\n• Plymouth (1)\n• polar continental (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nYou have carried out fieldwork to investigate how microclimate varies around your school site, for example between a car park, a playing field and a sheltered courtyard.\n\nDescribe how you would collect primary data for this investigation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear, detailed description of a workable method: what is measured, with what equipment, where, when and how sites are chosen, with reference to reliability.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic description, e.g. 'use a thermometer', with little detail.\nIndicative content:\n• Choose sites with different surfaces / shelter; sampling strategy, e.g. systematic or stratified\n• Measure temperature (digital thermometer), wind speed (anemometer), wind direction (compass / wind vane), humidity (hygrometer), cloud cover (oktas)\n• Take readings at the same height and the same time at each site, or within a short time period\n• Repeat readings and calculate a mean; record on a recording sheet with site locations on a map\n• Note conditions such as shade or surface type at each site` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nPolar maritime air reaches the UK from the north-west after crossing the North Atlantic Ocean. Western Scotland often has frequent heavy showers in this air, while eastern Scotland is drier.\n\nExplain why polar maritime air brings showers to western Scotland.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation linking the source and track of the air mass to instability, uplift and relief.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple points with limited explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• The air starts cold (polar) and picks up moisture as it crosses the Atlantic (maritime)\n• It is warmed from below by the relatively warm sea, making it unstable so it rises\n• Rising air cools, condenses and forms cumulus / cumulonimbus clouds, giving showers\n• Western Scotland is the first land reached; the Highlands force air to rise, adding relief rain\n• Air has lost much of its moisture by the time it reaches the east (rain shadow)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nHurricane Katrina (USA, 2005) killed about 1,800 people. Cyclone Nargis (Myanmar, 2008) was of similar strength at landfall but killed over 130,000 people.\n\nExplain why tropical storms often cause more deaths in LICs than in HICs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Developed explanation of several factors linked to level of development, ideally with reference to examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple points, e.g. 'they are poorer', with little development.\nIndicative content:\n• LICs have less money for forecasting, satellites and warning systems, so people get less warning\n• Fewer evacuation routes, shelters and vehicles, so fewer people can leave\n• Poorly built housing, often on low-lying coasts or deltas, is destroyed by wind and storm surge\n• Weaker emergency services and healthcare, so injured people are not treated\n• Many people depend on farming and fishing, so food shortages and disease follow\n• HICs can afford sea walls, levees and insurance, but these can still fail (e.g. New Orleans levees)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 12 is a world map of climate zones. It shows tropical rainforest near the equator, hot deserts at about 15–30° north and south, and a temperate zone, including the UK, between about 40° and 60°N.\n\nExplain how global atmospheric circulation leads to the very different climates of the tropical rainforest, hot deserts and the UK.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed, well-linked explanation of how rising and sinking air in the Hadley and Ferrel cells, pressure belts and surface winds produce the climate of all three zones.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of at least two zones, with some links to circulation.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic points, e.g. 'it is hot at the equator', with little reference to circulation.\nIndicative content:\n• Equator: overhead sun heats the surface; air rises (ITCZ, low pressure), cools and condenses, giving daily convectional rain and high humidity all year\n• About 30°: air in the Hadley cell sinks, warming and giving high pressure; clear skies, little rain and high daytime temperatures — hot deserts\n• Trade winds blow from the subtropical highs to the equator, completing the Hadley cell\n• UK: south-westerly winds blow from the 30° high towards the 60° low; air meets polar air at the polar front, rising to form depressions and frontal rain\n• This gives the UK a mild, wet, changeable climate with rain in all seasons\n0 marks: no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nIn December 2015 Storm Desmond caused severe flooding in Carlisle and Appleby in Cumbria and in Lancaster, where a flooded electricity substation cut power to tens of thousands of homes.\n\nExplain the social and economic impacts of a named extreme weather event in the UK that you have studied.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed, well-developed explanation of both social and economic impacts, with accurate place-specific detail of a named UK event.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of impacts, with some specific detail; may be unbalanced between social and economic.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic, generic impacts with little or no named detail.\nIndicative content (Storm Desmond, for example):\n• Social: over 5,000 homes flooded in Cumbria and Lancashire; people were evacuated and some were in temporary housing for months; stress and ill health\n• Social: power cuts in Lancaster affected homes, care homes and the university; schools closed\n• Social: bridges and roads destroyed, e.g. Pooley Bridge, isolating communities\n• Economic: businesses in Carlisle city centre flooded and closed; lost trade and jobs\n• Economic: insurance claims and repair costs of hundreds of millions of pounds; increased insurance premiums\n• Economic: damage to the A591 and rail lines disrupted tourism in the Lake District\nCredit other named UK events, e.g. the 'Beast from the East' 2018 or the July 2022 heatwave.\n0 marks: no response worthy of credit.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 15 is a fact file (data written for this practice question, except where named).\n• Typhoon Haiyan, Philippines (LIC/NEE), 2013: over 6,000 deaths; about 4 million people displaced; international aid from over 40 countries.\n• Hurricane Katrina, USA (HIC), 2005: about 1,800 deaths; around 1 million people displaced; damage of over US$125 billion.\n• Cyclone Idai, Mozambique (LIC), 2019: over 1,000 deaths across southern Africa; the city of Beira was badly damaged.\n\n'The effects of tropical storms are always worse in LICs than in HICs.' How far do you agree with this statement? Use Figure 15 and examples you have studied.\n[8 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 8, command: 'How far do you agree', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–8 marks): A well-balanced, evaluative answer that considers different types of effects (deaths, homelessness, economic cost) in both LICs and HICs, uses Figure 15 and own named examples with accurate detail, and reaches a clear, justified conclusion.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): A reasonable answer with some evaluation; uses some evidence; may be unbalanced or have an unsupported conclusion.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic, mainly descriptive points with little evidence and no real judgement.\n0 marks: no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Agree: death tolls and numbers displaced are usually higher in LICs (Haiyan over 6,000, Idai over 1,000, compared with about 1,800 for Katrina)\n• Agree: weaker housing, warnings, evacuation and healthcare in LICs; recovery is slower and relies on aid; long-term loss of livelihoods\n• Disagree: economic costs are usually far higher in HICs because property and infrastructure are worth more (Katrina over US$125 billion)\n• Disagree: HICs can still suffer badly, e.g. poorer groups in New Orleans; levee failure\n• Effects also depend on storm strength, storm surge height, track, population density and preparation, not only development\n• Conclusion: e.g. human effects are usually worse in LICs, but 'always' is too strong and economic effects can be greater in HICs\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance (3): Spelling and punctuation are consistently accurate; grammar is used to control meaning; a wide range of specialist terms is used accurately.\nIntermediate performance (2): Spelling and punctuation are considerably accurate; general control of meaning; a good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold performance (1): Spelling and punctuation are reasonably accurate; no serious errors that hinder meaning; a limited range of specialist terms.\n0: no response, or errors seriously hinder meaning.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 16 shows two views about managing extreme weather in the UK (written for this practice question).\nView A (a Cumbrian resident): "Storm Desmond showed our flood defences are not high enough. The government must spend much more on walls and flood barriers."\nView B (a local councillor): "We cannot build defences against every storm. It is better to spend money on warnings, planting trees upstream and helping people make their homes flood-resilient."\n\nShould the UK spend more money on hard engineering to protect people from extreme weather? Justify your views. You may also use examples you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–8 marks): A well-justified decision supported by a balanced evaluation of both views and alternatives, using detailed evidence from named UK examples, with a clear conclusion.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): A decision with some justification and some use of examples; evaluation may be one-sided.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): A basic decision with little justification or evidence.\n0 marks: no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• For hard engineering: walls and barriers give a high level of protection to towns such as Carlisle; protect homes, businesses and infrastructure; reassure residents and insurers\n• Against: very expensive; Carlisle's defences, built after the 2005 floods, were overtopped by Desmond in 2015; can move the problem downstream; visual impact\n• Alternatives: Met Office and Environment Agency warnings, natural flood management (tree planting, leaky dams), flood-resilient homes, land-use planning to avoid building on floodplains\n• Extreme weather is not only flooding: heatwaves (40.3 °C in 2022) and snow ('Beast from the East' 2018) need other responses such as warnings, gritting and health advice\n• Climate change may make extreme events more frequent, so long-term flexible approaches may be more sustainable\n• Credit any justified decision, including a combination of approaches` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Weather and climate.\nFigure 17 summarises UK climate data (data written for this practice question).\n• January mean temperature: Penzance (south-west coast) 7 °C; Ben Nevis summit (1,345 m) −4 °C; Norwich (east) 4 °C.\n• July mean temperature: London 19 °C; Lerwick, Shetland (60°N) 12 °C.\n• Annual rainfall: Western Highlands over 3,000 mm; Fort William 2,000 mm; Cambridge 560 mm.\n• Prevailing wind: south-westerly.\n\n'Latitude is the most important factor affecting the climate of the UK.' Do you agree? Use Figure 17 and your own knowledge to justify your answer.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–8 marks): A well-reasoned evaluation of latitude against other factors (maritime influence and the North Atlantic Drift, prevailing winds and air masses, altitude, distance from the sea), using Figure 17 data accurately, with a clear, justified judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some evaluation of two or more factors with some data use; judgement may be weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic description of factors with little data and no real judgement.\n0 marks: no response worthy of credit.\nIndicative content:\n• Latitude: the south receives more intense sunlight, so summers are warmer in the south (London 19 °C v Lerwick 12 °C in July)\n• But in winter the isotherms run north–south: the west is milder than the east (Penzance 7 °C v Norwich 4 °C), showing maritime influence and the North Atlantic Drift matter more than latitude\n• Prevailing south-westerly winds and depressions bring rain to the west; relief rain and rain shadow explain 3,000 mm in the Highlands but 560 mm in Cambridge — latitude does not explain rainfall\n• Altitude: temperatures fall about 6–7 °C per 1,000 m, so Ben Nevis is −4 °C in January\n• Air masses bring different weather from day to day regardless of latitude\n• Conclusion: e.g. latitude is most important for summer temperatures, but maritime influence, prevailing winds and relief are more important for winter temperatures and rainfall` }
    ]
  },
  '2.4': {
    name: 'Climate Change — Cause and Effect',
    questions: [
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nCarbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide are greenhouse gases. Their concentration in the atmosphere has increased since the Industrial Revolution.\n\nChoose the correct definition of the term 'enhanced greenhouse effect'.\nA  The natural process by which the atmosphere traps heat and keeps the Earth warm enough for life\nB  The extra warming caused by human activities adding more greenhouse gases to the atmosphere\nC  The thinning of the ozone layer, which lets more ultraviolet radiation reach the Earth\nD  The cooling of the Earth caused by ash and gases from volcanic eruptions`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)\nDo not credit more than one answer. A describes the natural greenhouse effect; C is ozone depletion, not the greenhouse effect.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 1: a table of human sources of three greenhouse gases.\n\nGreenhouse gas | Main human sources\nCarbon dioxide | burning coal, oil and gas; deforestation\nMethane | ? ; landfill sites\nNitrous oxide | nitrogen fertilisers; vehicle exhausts\n\nGive one human source of methane that could complete Figure 1.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any one from:\n• cattle / livestock (digestion) (1)\n• rice paddies / flooded rice fields (1)\n• leaks from gas pipelines / coal mining (1)\n• animal manure (1)\nDo not credit landfill (already given).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 2: a line graph of carbon dioxide concentration in air bubbles trapped in the EPICA Dome C ice core from Antarctica, covering the last 800,000 years. The line rises and falls in a regular pattern of peaks and troughs roughly every 100,000 years. Troughs (glacial periods) are at about 180–190 ppm. Peaks (interglacial periods) are at about 260–300 ppm. No peak goes above 300 ppm until a sharp vertical rise at the very end of the record, after about 1800, to over 400 ppm today.\n\nUsing Figure 2, state the highest concentration of carbon dioxide reached before 1800.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `about 300 ppm (1)\nAllow any value in the range 290–300 ppm. Units needed.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nThe Milankovitch cycles are natural changes in the Earth's orbit and tilt. They help to explain the glacial and interglacial periods of the Quaternary period.\n\nWhich Milankovitch cycle repeats approximately every 100,000 years?\nA  Axial tilt (obliquity)\nB  Precession (wobble)\nC  Eccentricity (shape of the orbit)\nD  The sunspot cycle`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `C (1)\nTilt is about 41,000 years; precession about 26,000 years; the sunspot cycle (about 11 years) is not a Milankovitch cycle.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 3: a table of the annual mean temperature for a weather station in mid-Wales, 2018–2023 (data written for this practice question).\n\nYear | 2018 | 2019 | 2020 | 2021 | 2022 | 2023\nMean temperature (°C) | 9.5 | 9.4 | 9.6 | 9.2 | 10.0 | 9.9\n\nUsing Figure 3, calculate the range of annual mean temperature.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.8 °C (1)\n(10.0 − 9.2 = 0.8)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 4: a table of annual mean carbon dioxide concentration measured at Mauna Loa Observatory, Hawaii (approximate values rounded from the NOAA record).\n\nYear | 1960 | 1980 | 2000 | 2023\nCO₂ (ppm) | 316.9 | 338.9 | 369.7 | 421.1\n\nUsing Figure 4, calculate the percentage increase in carbon dioxide concentration between 1960 and 2023. Show your working and give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `421.1 − 316.9 = 104.2 (1)\n104.2 ÷ 316.9 × 100 = 32.9% (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Allow 32.88% for 1 mark only if not rounded to one decimal place.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 5: a map of South Asia. Bangladesh is shown on the coast of the Bay of Bengal. India surrounds it on the west, north and east, and Myanmar is on its south-east border. The Tropic of Cancer passes through the middle of the country. The rivers Ganges (Padma) and Brahmaputra (Jamuna) flow across Bangladesh and join before reaching the sea, forming a large delta.\n\nUsing Figure 5, describe the location of Bangladesh.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per accurate locational point, up to 2:\n• in South Asia (1)\n• bordered by India to the west, north and east (1)\n• borders Myanmar to the south-east (1)\n• on the Bay of Bengal / coast to the south (1)\n• on the Tropic of Cancer / about 23–24°N (1)\n• on the delta of the Ganges and Brahmaputra (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nScientists use tree rings from very old trees, such as bristlecone pines in California, as evidence of past climate. Some trees are more than 4,000 years old.\n\nSuggest how tree rings can provide evidence of past climate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 marks for a developed point:\n• one ring grows each year, so rings can be counted to date each year (1)\n• wide rings show warm / wet years with good growing conditions (1); narrow rings show cold / dry years (1)\ne.g. 'Each ring is one year of growth (1) and a thicker ring means the climate was warmer or wetter that year (1).'` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 6: a bar chart of the minimum extent of Arctic sea ice each September (approximate values, written for this practice question). 1980: 7.7 million km²; 1990: 6.1; 2000: 6.3; 2007: 4.3; 2012: 3.4; 2020: 3.9.\n\nUsing Figure 6, complete the sentences.\n"The smallest area of sea ice shown was in ________."\n"Between 1980 and 2012 the area of sea ice decreased by ________ million km²."`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `2012 (1)\n4.3 (million km²) (1)\n(7.7 − 3.4 = 4.3)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nMount Pinatubo in the Philippines erupted in June 1991. It sent about 15 million tonnes of sulphur dioxide into the stratosphere. In 1992 the average global temperature was about 0.5 °C lower than before the eruption.\n\nSuggest why the eruption of Mount Pinatubo lowered global temperatures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 marks for a developed point:\n• sulphur dioxide / ash formed a layer of particles (aerosols) high in the atmosphere (1)\n• which reflected incoming sunlight / solar radiation back to space (1)\n• so less heat reached the Earth's surface (1)\nAllow: the gas spread around the globe on high-level winds, so the effect was global (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 7: two photographs of the Rhône Glacier in Switzerland taken from the same viewpoint. In the photograph from about 1900, a thick tongue of ice fills the valley and reaches the valley floor close to the village of Gletsch. In the photograph from 2020, the ice ends high up the valley side. Below it there is bare, grey rock, a meltwater lake and a river flowing towards Gletsch.\n\nUsing Figure 7, describe how the Rhône Glacier changed between 1900 and 2020.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per point, up to 2:\n• the glacier has retreated / the snout is much further up the valley (1)\n• the ice is thinner / smaller in area (1)\n• bare rock is now exposed where there was ice (1)\n• a meltwater lake has formed in front of the ice (1)\nDo not credit explanations (e.g. 'because of global warming') without description.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nTuvalu is a small island state in the Pacific Ocean. Its highest natural point is less than 5 m above sea level and most of its land is less than 2 m above sea level.\n\nGive one way that sea-level rise could affect people living in Tuvalu.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 marks for a developed point:\n• salt water floods / contaminates fresh groundwater (1) so there is less drinking water (1)\n• salt water damages soil (1) so crops such as pulaka / taro fail (1)\n• homes and roads flooded by high tides / storm surges (1) so people may have to migrate, e.g. to New Zealand or Australia (1)\n• coastal erosion removes land (1) leaving less space to live (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nOn 19 July 2022 a temperature of 40.3 °C was recorded at Coningsby in Lincolnshire. It was the first time the UK had recorded a temperature above 40 °C.\n\nSuggest one way that heatwaves like this could affect people in the UK.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 marks for a developed point:\n• heat stroke / dehydration (1), especially for elderly people, leading to more deaths (1)\n• railway lines buckle / roads melt (1) so journeys are cancelled or delayed (1)\n• drought and hosepipe bans (1) because reservoirs fall to low levels (1)\n• wildfires on dry grassland (1) which destroy homes, e.g. Wennington, London, in July 2022 (1)\nAllow positive effects, e.g. more tourism at UK resorts (1) bringing income (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 8: a table of mean sea level at a tide gauge on the coast of Bangladesh, measured relative to the level in 2000 (data written for this practice question).\n\nYear | 2000 | 2005 | 2010 | 2015 | 2020\nSea level (mm above 2000) | 0 | 17 | 33 | 52 | 70\n\nUsing Figure 8, calculate the mean rate of sea-level rise per year between 2000 and 2020. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `70 ÷ 20 (1)\n= 3.5 mm per year (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Units needed for second mark.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 9: a pie chart of global greenhouse gas emissions by sector (data written for this practice question). Electricity and heat 32%; transport 16%; manufacturing and construction 13%; agriculture 12%; buildings 7%; land-use change and forestry 5%; other 15%.\n\nChoose the two correct statements.\nA  Transport produces more emissions than electricity and heat.\nB  Electricity and heat is the largest single sector.\nC  Agriculture and land-use change together make up 17% of emissions.\nD  Buildings produce more emissions than manufacturing and construction.\nE  Transport produces half of all emissions.`, marks: 2, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)\nC (1)\n(12% + 5% = 17%)\nIf more than two letters are chosen, deduct 1 mark for each incorrect letter.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nA household in Cardiff wants to reduce its carbon footprint. At the moment the family drives two cars, heats the house with a gas boiler and flies abroad twice a year.\n\nDescribe one way the family could reduce its carbon footprint.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 marks for a developed point:\n• use public transport / cycle instead of the car (1) so less petrol or diesel is burned (1)\n• replace one car with an electric car (1) charged from renewable electricity (1)\n• fit a heat pump / solar panels (1) so less gas is burned for heating (1)\n• insulate the loft / walls (1) so less energy is wasted (1)\n• holiday in the UK / travel by train instead of flying (1) as aircraft emit a lot of CO₂ per passenger (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nThe Woodland Trust's Nature's Calendar project collects records from members of the public of the dates when natural events happen each year in the UK. These are called phenological records.\n\nGive two pieces of phenological evidence that would suggest the UK climate is getting warmer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark each, up to 2:\n• trees (e.g. oak) coming into leaf earlier in spring (1)\n• flowers (e.g. snowdrops, bluebells) flowering earlier (1)\n• migrant birds (e.g. swallows) arriving earlier / leaving later (1)\n• frogspawn appearing earlier (1)\n• leaves falling later in autumn / longer growing season (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFresh snow and sea ice reflect about 80–90% of the sunlight that reaches them. Dark open ocean water reflects less than 10%. The area of Arctic sea ice in summer is getting smaller.\n\nSuggest how the loss of Arctic sea ice could lead to further warming.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a simple point, 2 marks for a developed point:\n• darker ocean is exposed, which absorbs more of the Sun's energy (1)\n• so the water warms and melts more ice, a positive feedback loop (1)\n• less reflection / lower albedo means less sunlight is sent back to space (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 10: a line graph of global average temperature compared with the 1850–1900 average (approximate values). 1900: 0.0 °C; 1940: +0.3 °C; 1960: +0.3 °C; 1980: +0.5 °C; 2000: +0.8 °C; 2010: +1.0 °C; 2020: +1.3 °C; 2024: +1.55 °C.\n\nUsing Figure 10, complete the sentences.\n"Global temperature stayed about the same between 1940 and ________."\n"Between 1960 and 2024 global temperature rose by ________ °C."\n"The first year shown when the temperature was 1.0 °C above the 1850–1900 average was ________."`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `1960 (1)\n1.25 (°C) (1)\n(1.55 − 0.3 = 1.25)\n2010 (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 17: a table of annual mean carbon dioxide concentration measured at Mauna Loa Observatory, Hawaii (approximate values rounded from the NOAA record).\n\nYear | 1960 | 1980 | 2000 | 2023\nCO₂ (ppm) | 316.9 | 338.9 | 369.7 | 421.1\n\nBefore the Industrial Revolution the concentration was about 280 ppm.\n\nDescribe the changes in carbon dioxide concentration shown in Figure 17. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per point, up to 3. Maximum 2 marks without data.\n• carbon dioxide concentration increased throughout / every year shown (1)\n• from 316.9 ppm in 1960 to 421.1 ppm in 2023 (1)\n• the rate of increase is getting faster (1): 22 ppm in 1960–1980 but 51.4 ppm in 2000–2023 (1)\n• today's level is about 50% higher than the pre-industrial 280 ppm (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nA group of students investigated people's attitudes to climate change in their town. They stood outside one supermarket from 10 a.m. to 11 a.m. on a Saturday and asked the first 12 people who would stop to complete a questionnaire.\n\nIdentify one weakness of the students' sampling method and suggest how it could be improved.`, marks: 3, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Weakness, 1 mark for a simple point, 2 marks if developed:\n• sample is too small (1) so results may not be representative of the whole town (1)\n• only one place and time (1) so some groups, e.g. people at work, were missed (1)\n• only people willing to stop were asked (1) which may bias results, e.g. towards people interested in the issue (1)\nImprovement, 1 mark:\n• ask more people, e.g. 50+ (1) / survey at several locations or times (1) / use stratified sampling by age group (1) / systematic sampling, e.g. every 5th person (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nBoth Bangladesh and the Netherlands have large areas of low-lying land on river deltas. Bangladesh is a lower-income country with a population of about 170 million. The Netherlands is a high-income country with a population of about 18 million.\n\nExplain why sea-level rise is likely to have a greater effect on people in Bangladesh than in the Netherlands.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point, with up to 2 marks for a developed point:\n• Bangladesh has less money (1) so it cannot afford large sea defences like the Netherlands' Delta Works (1)\n• many more people live on low-lying coastal land in Bangladesh (1) so more people would be displaced (1)\n• many people in Bangladesh depend on farming (1) which is damaged when salt water floods the land (1)\n• Bangladesh is also hit by tropical cyclones and storm surges (1) which reach further inland as sea level rises (1)\n• homes in rural Bangladesh are often less well built (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 11: a world map of projected temperature increase by 2100 compared with 1850–1900 if emissions stay high. The Arctic (northern Canada, Greenland, northern Russia and the Arctic Ocean) shows the greatest increase, over 7 °C. Most of North America, Europe, northern and central Asia show 4–6 °C. Most of Africa, South America, South Asia and Australia show 3–5 °C. The smallest increases, 1–3 °C, are over the oceans, especially the Southern Ocean around Antarctica and the North Atlantic south of Greenland.\n\nDescribe the pattern of projected temperature increase shown in Figure 11.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per descriptive point, up to 4. Credit 1 mark for use of map data / named places.\n• all areas are projected to warm (1)\n• the greatest warming is in the Arctic / high northern latitudes, over 7 °C (1)\n• land warms more than the oceans (1)\n• the northern hemisphere warms more than the southern hemisphere (1)\n• smallest warming over the Southern Ocean / North Atlantic south of Greenland, 1–3 °C (1)\n• continents such as Africa and Australia mostly 3–5 °C (1)\nDo not credit explanation.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nThe EPICA Dome C ice core was drilled more than 3 km down into the Antarctic ice sheet. The ice at the bottom formed about 800,000 years ago.\n\nExplain how ice cores provide evidence of climate change during the Quaternary period.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A clear explanation of how ice cores are used, with linked reasons.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple points about ice cores with little explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• snow falls each year and is compressed into layers of ice, so depth shows age\n• air bubbles trapped in the ice hold samples of the past atmosphere\n• CO₂ and methane levels in the bubbles can be measured; high levels match warm interglacials\n• the ratio of oxygen isotopes in the ice shows the temperature when the snow fell\n• the record shows repeated glacial–interglacial cycles about every 100,000 years\n• shows CO₂ is now higher than at any point in 800,000 years` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nDuring the Quaternary period the Earth's climate has changed many times between cold glacial periods and warmer interglacial periods. These changes happened long before humans burned fossil fuels.\n\nGive two natural causes of climate change. Explain how each one changes the climate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each cause: 1 for the cause, 1 for explanation.\n• Milankovitch cycles / changes in the Earth's orbit (eccentricity), tilt or wobble (1) change how much solar energy reaches the Earth and when in the year (1)\n• changes in solar output / sunspot activity (1) e.g. few sunspots in the Maunder Minimum (1645–1715) linked to the Little Ice Age (1)\n• large volcanic eruptions (1) put sulphur dioxide / ash into the stratosphere, reflecting sunlight and cooling the Earth for 1–3 years (1)\nAllow changes in ocean currents (1) with explanation (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nThe natural greenhouse effect keeps the Earth's average temperature at about 15 °C. Without it, the average would be about −18 °C. Human activity has increased the amount of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere.\n\nDraw an annotated diagram to explain the enhanced greenhouse effect.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A clear diagram (Sun, atmosphere, Earth's surface) with annotations that explain the sequence and the human cause.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A basic diagram with labels rather than explanations.\nIndicative content:\n• short-wave radiation from the Sun passes through the atmosphere\n• Earth's surface absorbs it and gives out long-wave (infrared) radiation\n• greenhouse gases (CO₂, methane, nitrous oxide) absorb and re-radiate this heat\n• human sources shown, e.g. power stations, cars, cattle, deforestation\n• more greenhouse gases = thicker 'blanket', more heat trapped, so temperature rises\nMaximum Level 1 if the diagram only shows the natural greenhouse effect.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nBetween 2000 and 2020 large areas of the Amazon rainforest in Brazil were cleared for cattle ranching and soya farming. Much of the cleared forest was burned.\n\nExplain why deforestation in the Amazon contributes to climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation with linked points about carbon release and loss of carbon store.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements.\nIndicative content:\n• trees store carbon; the rainforest is a major carbon sink\n• burning releases the stored carbon as CO₂\n• fewer trees, so less CO₂ is absorbed by photosynthesis\n• cattle that replace forest give off methane, a powerful greenhouse gas\n• fertilisers on soya fields release nitrous oxide\n• more greenhouse gases strengthen the enhanced greenhouse effect` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 12: a table of carbon dioxide emissions per person (approximate values, rounded).\n\nCountry | CO₂ per person per year (tonnes)\nUSA | 14.9\nUK | 4.7\nBangladesh | 0.6\n\nGive two reasons why carbon dioxide emissions per person in the UK are much higher than in Bangladesh.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks per reason: 1 for the reason, 1 for development.\n• more people in the UK own cars (1) so more petrol and diesel is burned (1)\n• UK homes use more electricity and gas for heating / appliances (1) much of it still from fossil fuels (1)\n• the UK has more industry / services using energy (1)\n• people in the UK fly more often (1)\n• many people in rural Bangladesh have no / little electricity (1) and live by subsistence farming (1)\nCredit use of data, e.g. UK emissions per person are about 8 times Bangladesh's (4.7 ÷ 0.6 ≈ 7.8) (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nIn November 2007 Cyclone Sidr hit the coast of Bangladesh, killing more than 3,000 people. Scientists predict that tropical cyclones will become more intense as sea temperatures rise.\n\nExplain the effects of climate change on people in Bangladesh.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of more than one effect, linked to climate change, with some place detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple effects, little explanation.\nIndicative content:\n• stronger cyclones and higher storm surges destroy homes and crops on the coast\n• sea-level rise floods low-lying land in the delta, e.g. the Sundarbans / Khulna area\n• salt water gets into soils and wells, so rice yields fall and drinking water is unsafe\n• heavier monsoon rain and faster Himalayan glacier melt increase river flooding\n• people lose their livelihoods and migrate to Dhaka, adding to slums\n• spread of water-borne diseases after floods` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nThe Met Office's UK climate projections (UKCP18) suggest that by 2070 UK summers will be hotter and drier and winters will be milder and wetter. Sea level around the UK will continue to rise.\n\nExplain how these changes could affect natural environments in the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of environmental effects, linked to the climate changes given.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements, or effects on people only.\nIndicative content:\n• species move north or to higher ground, e.g. mountain plants in Snowdonia / Cairngorms lose habitat\n• new species / pests arrive from the south\n• summer drought dries out wetlands and peat bogs, which may release carbon\n• higher risk of wildfires on moorland and heathland\n• sea-level rise floods salt marshes and coastal habitats (coastal squeeze)\n• wetter winters increase river flooding and soil erosion` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nThe Maldives is a small island state of over 1,000 islands in the Indian Ocean. About 80% of its land is less than 1 m above sea level. Hulhumalé, an artificial island built near the capital Malé, is about 2 m above sea level.\n\nDescribe how a small island state you have studied is adapting to climate change. Name the country.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Detailed description of more than one adaptation strategy with place detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple, generic strategies.\nIndicative content (Maldives):\n• the sea wall around Malé built after floods in 1987\n• building Hulhumalé on reclaimed land raised about 2 m above sea level, so people can move there\n• protecting and replanting coral reefs and mangroves which absorb wave energy\n• desalination plants to supply fresh water as groundwater becomes salty\n• raising buildings / harbours; considering buying land abroad for future migration\nCredit other small island states, e.g. Tuvalu, Kiribati (Kiribati bought land in Fiji), with place detail.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nIn a survey, people in the UK were asked whether climate change should be a top priority for the government. Answers differed between young people and older people, between people in cities and in rural areas, and between people working in different industries.\n\nExplain why people have different views about how to respond to climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of reasons for different views, linked to groups of people.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple reasons.\nIndicative content:\n• young people will live with the effects for longer, so may want faster action\n• people in jobs linked to fossil fuels (e.g. oil workers, steelworkers) may fear job losses\n• cost of changes such as heat pumps or electric cars worries people on lower incomes\n• rural people depend more on cars, so dislike fuel taxes\n• different access to information / the media; some people doubt the science\n• people in places at risk, e.g. flooding, may see the problem as more urgent` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nAt the COP21 meeting in 2015, almost every country in the world signed the Paris Agreement.\n\nDescribe the Paris Agreement as a global-scale strategy to mitigate climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per point, up to 4:\n• aims to keep global temperature rise well below 2 °C above pre-industrial levels (1)\n• and to try to limit it to 1.5 °C (1)\n• each country sets its own targets to cut emissions (nationally determined contributions) (1)\n• countries review and increase targets every five years (1)\n• richer countries give money (climate finance) to help poorer countries cut emissions and adapt (1)\n• aim to reach net zero emissions in the second half of the century (1)\n• targets are not legally binding / no penalties (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nPermafrost is ground that stays frozen all year. It covers large areas of Siberia, northern Canada and Alaska and contains large amounts of frozen plant remains.\n\nExplain how thawing permafrost could speed up climate change.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of the positive feedback loop with linked stages.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements.\nIndicative content:\n• rising temperatures thaw the permafrost\n• frozen plant remains start to decompose\n• releasing carbon dioxide and methane\n• methane is a much more powerful greenhouse gas than CO₂\n• this adds to the enhanced greenhouse effect, causing more warming\n• which thaws more permafrost: a positive feedback loop` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nBangladesh produces less than 0.5% of global carbon dioxide emissions, but it is one of the countries most at risk from the effects of climate change.\n\nGive two reasons why adaptation may be more important than mitigation for a country such as Bangladesh.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks per reason: 1 for the reason, 1 for development.\n• Bangladesh's emissions are very small (1) so cutting them would make little difference to global warming (1)\n• the effects are happening now (1) e.g. floods and cyclones, so people need protection immediately (1)\n• adaptation saves lives directly (1) e.g. cyclone shelters and early warning systems (1)\n• the country has limited money (1) so it must spend it where it helps people most (1)\n• climate change will continue for decades even if emissions are cut (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 13: a table of the number of days above 30 °C recorded at a weather station in southern England in each decade (data written for this practice question).\n\nDecade | 1961–70 | 1971–80 | 1981–90 | 1991–2000 | 2001–10 | 2011–20\nDays above 30 °C | 4 | 7 | 9 | 14 | 18 | 25\n\nUsing Figure 13, choose the correct answer in each statement.\nThe number of days above 30 °C increased by (19 / 21 / 25) between 1961–70 and 2011–20.\nThe largest increase between one decade and the next was between (1981–90 and 1991–2000 / 1991–2000 and 2001–10 / 2001–10 and 2011–20).\nThe mean number of days above 30 °C per decade was (12.8 / 14.0 / 15.4).\nThe number of days above 30 °C (increased / decreased / stayed the same) in every decade.`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `21 (1) (25 − 4)\n2001–10 and 2011–20 (1) (an increase of 7)\n12.8 (1) (77 ÷ 6 = 12.83)\nincreased (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nSatellite measurements show that global mean sea level is now rising by more than 3 mm per year. The Ganges–Brahmaputra delta in Bangladesh is one of the most densely populated low-lying areas in the world.\n\nExplain why global sea level is rising and why this is a problem for people living in low-lying coastal areas.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed explanation of both causes of sea-level rise AND of the problems, with clear links and place detail.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Explanation of causes and problems with some links; may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements of causes or problems.\nIndicative content:\nCauses:\n• thermal expansion: as oceans warm, the water expands\n• melting of land ice (glaciers, Greenland and Antarctic ice sheets) adds water to the oceans\n• melting sea ice does not raise sea level as it already floats (credit if stated)\nProblems:\n• permanent flooding of farmland and homes, e.g. in the Bangladesh delta\n• salt water intrusion into soils and drinking water\n• storm surges from cyclones reach further inland\n• loss of livelihoods (rice farming, shrimp ponds damaged), migration to cities such as Dhaka\n• coastal erosion and the cost of building defences` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nThe UK Climate Change Act (2008) set a legally binding target to cut greenhouse gas emissions. In 2019 the target was changed to 'net zero' by 2050. Many towns and cities in the UK and Wales have also declared a 'climate emergency'.\n\nExplain how mitigation strategies at national and local scales in the UK can reduce greenhouse gas emissions.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed explanation of strategies at both national and local scales, clearly linked to reduced emissions, with examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Explanation of some strategies; may focus on one scale.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple list of strategies.\nIndicative content:\nNational:\n• legally binding carbon budgets under the Climate Change Act\n• closing coal-fired power stations (the last, Ratcliffe-on-Soar, closed in 2024)\n• expanding offshore wind farms so less electricity comes from fossil fuels\n• grants for heat pumps and insulation; plans to end sales of new petrol and diesel cars\nLocal:\n• low-emission / clean air zones and better public transport, cycle lanes\n• council solar panels, energy-efficient public buildings\n• community tree planting and renewable energy schemes\n• recycling and food-waste collection to reduce landfill methane` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 14: two statements.\nStatement A: "The Earth's climate has always changed naturally. Changes in the Sun's output and the Earth's orbit explain the warming we see today."\nStatement B: "The rapid warming since 1950 is mainly caused by people burning fossil fuels and clearing forests."\nGlobal average temperature is now about 1.3 °C above the 1850–1900 average, and atmospheric CO₂ has risen from about 280 ppm to over 420 ppm. Satellite measurements show no long-term rise in the Sun's energy output since the 1950s.\n\nWhich statement do you agree with more? Justify your view.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) knowledge and understanding of natural and human causes; AO3 (4 marks) use of evidence to reach a judgement.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Thorough understanding of both natural and human causes. Uses evidence from the resource and own knowledge well. A clear, well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Sound understanding of causes, some use of evidence, a judgement with some support.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic points, little evidence, judgement unsupported or absent.\nIndicative content:\nFor A (natural): Milankovitch cycles explain glacial–interglacial cycles; Maunder Minimum and the Little Ice Age; volcanic eruptions such as Pinatubo cooled the Earth; climate has changed many times in the Quaternary.\nFor B (human): Milankovitch cycles act over tens of thousands of years, too slowly for today's warming; no rise in solar output since the 1950s while temperatures rose; CO₂ is far above the 300 ppm peak of the last 800,000 years; rise matches the growth of fossil fuel use and deforestation; enhanced greenhouse effect.\nCredit a judgement either way if supported, e.g. natural causes still operate but humans are now the main cause. A one-sided answer can reach Level 3 if it considers and rejects the other view.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 15: responses to climate change in Bangladesh.\n• Mitigation: millions of solar home systems have been installed in rural villages, replacing kerosene lamps; plans to build more solar farms.\n• Adaptation: thousands of raised cyclone shelters along the coast; early warning systems for cyclones; floating gardens on flooded land; salt-tolerant rice varieties; homes built on raised earth plinths.\n\n'Adaptation is a better response to climate change than mitigation for Bangladesh.' Do you agree? Justify your view. [8 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) knowledge and understanding of mitigation and adaptation; AO3 (4 marks) evaluation and a supported judgement.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Thorough understanding of both strategies, applied to Bangladesh with detail from the resource and own knowledge. A clear, well-justified conclusion.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Sound understanding, some application to Bangladesh, judgement with some support.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic points, generic, little or no judgement.\nIndicative content:\nAgree: Bangladesh emits very little, so mitigation has little global effect; effects (cyclones, floods, salinisation) are happening now; cyclone shelters and warnings have greatly cut death tolls compared with the 1970 and 1991 cyclones; floating gardens and salt-tolerant rice protect food supply; adaptation helps the poorest people directly.\nDisagree / balance: mitigation is still needed globally or adaptation will be overwhelmed; solar home systems bring development and cut kerosene use and indoor pollution; adaptation is costly and may depend on foreign aid; some places may become impossible to defend, e.g. low islands in the delta; both are needed.\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance (3 marks): spelling and punctuation are consistently accurate; grammar is used with effective control of meaning; a wide range of specialist terms is used precisely.\nIntermediate performance (2 marks): spelling and punctuation are generally accurate; grammar is used with general control of meaning; a good range of specialist terms is used appropriately.\nThreshold performance (1 mark): spelling and punctuation are reasonably accurate; grammar is used with some control; a limited range of specialist terms is used.\n0 marks: errors severely hinder meaning, or the response is not related to the question.` },
      { q: `Theme 2: Changing Environments — Climate change, cause and effect.\n\nFigure 16: three scales of response to climate change.\n• Global: the Paris Agreement (2015) — countries set their own targets to reduce emissions.\n• National: Wales's Well-being of Future Generations Act (2015) and a target of net zero emissions by 2050.\n• Local / individual: a Gwynedd village sets up a community hydro-electric scheme; families choose to cycle, recycle and eat less meat.\n\nAre global agreements more effective than local and individual actions in reducing the causes of climate change? Justify your view. You may use examples you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) knowledge and understanding of responses at different scales; AO3 (4 marks) evaluation and a supported judgement.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Thorough understanding of responses at several scales, with examples. Evaluates their effectiveness and reaches a clear, well-supported judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Sound understanding of responses, some evaluation, judgement with some support.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Basic description of responses, little evaluation or judgement.\nIndicative content:\nGlobal: climate change is a global problem so needs global action; Paris covers almost all emissions; finance for LICs; BUT targets are not legally binding, countries can withdraw (USA), current pledges are not enough to keep below 1.5 °C.\nNational: laws such as the UK Climate Change Act / Welsh net zero target are binding and lead to real change, e.g. closure of coal power stations, offshore wind; BUT governments change policy.\nLocal / individual: community hydro schemes and changes in lifestyle cut emissions directly and raise awareness; BUT one family's savings are tiny compared with large emitters; depends on choice and money.\nCredit a supported judgement, e.g. global agreements set the direction but only work if national and local actions follow.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.1': {
    name: 'How Ecosystems Function',
    questions: [
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.1: a photograph of a small pond in a UK nature reserve. The labels show reeds and water lilies at the edge, frogspawn, a dragonfly, a heron standing in shallow water, mud on the bed of the pond and sunlight on the water surface.\n\nWhich of the following is an abiotic component of the pond ecosystem shown in Figure 3.1?\n•  Water lilies\n•  Frogspawn\n•  Mud on the bed of the pond\n•  The heron`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `Mud on the bed of the pond (1).\nAccept: soil/sediment. Do not credit any living component.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.2 shows a food chain from a deciduous woodland in the UK:\noak leaves → caterpillar → blue tit → sparrowhawk\n\nName the secondary consumer in Figure 3.2.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Blue tit (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.3: a world map of biomes. Location X is marked on the Arctic coast of northern Siberia, Russia, at about 70°N. The map key shows that the land there is covered by low-growing mosses, lichens and dwarf shrubs, with no trees.\n\nName the biome found at location X.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Tundra (1).\nAccept: Arctic tundra. Do not accept: coniferous forest / taiga / boreal forest.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.4 is a climate table for a weather station in the tundra (data written for this practice question).\nMonth: J F M A M J J A S O N D\nMean temperature (°C): −26 −28 −26 −18 −6 2 5 4 −1 −10 −18 −24\nPrecipitation (mm): 5 4 4 5 5 9 22 25 15 12 6 5\n\nIdentify the month with the highest precipitation.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `August (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.5 is a climate table for Station A, in the tropical rainforest of the Amazon basin, Brazil (data written for this practice question).\nMonth: J F M A M J J A S O N D\nMean temperature (°C): 26.1 26.0 26.2 26.3 26.4 26.5 26.6 27.2 27.6 27.5 27.1 26.6\nRainfall (mm): 260 290 310 300 250 110 80 60 80 110 170 220\n\nCalculate the annual temperature range at Station A. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Highest 27.6 °C (September) − lowest 26.0 °C (February) (1)\n= 1.6 °C (1).\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.5 is a climate table for Station A, in the tropical rainforest of the Amazon basin, Brazil (data written for this practice question). Total annual rainfall is 2,240 mm.\nMonth: J F M A M J J A S O N D\nRainfall (mm): 260 290 310 300 250 110 80 60 80 110 170 220\n\nCalculate the percentage of the annual rainfall that falls in February, March and April. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `290 + 310 + 300 = 900 mm, and 900 ÷ 2,240 × 100 (1)\n= 40.2% (1).\nAccept 40%. Correct answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.6: a world map of the tropical rainforest biome. Shaded areas cover the Amazon basin in South America, Central America, the Congo basin in Central Africa, eastern Madagascar, South-East Asia (Indonesia, Malaysia, Papua New Guinea) and a small area of north-east Australia. The Equator and the Tropics of Cancer and Capricorn are marked.\n\nDescribe the location of the tropical rainforest biome shown in Figure 3.6.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `One mark per valid point, up to 2 marks:\n• Found between the Tropics of Cancer and Capricorn / mainly close to the Equator (1).\n• Named continents or regions, e.g. Amazon basin in South America, Congo basin in Africa, South-East Asia (1).\n• Largest continuous area is the Amazon basin (1).\n• Found on or near the east coasts of continents outside the Equator, e.g. eastern Madagascar, north-east Australia (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nThe world's biomes are found in broad belts, for example tropical rainforest near the Equator and tundra near the Arctic Circle.\n\nGive two abiotic factors that affect the global distribution of biomes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `One mark for each, up to 2 marks:\n• Temperature / latitude (1).\n• Precipitation / rainfall amount (1).\n• Length of the growing season / seasonality of rainfall (1).\n• Sunlight / insolation (1).\n• Soil type / altitude / relief (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.7 describes a food web for a freshwater pond in the UK:\n• Algae and pondweed are eaten by water fleas and pond snails.\n• Water fleas are eaten by sticklebacks and dragonfly nymphs.\n• Pond snails are eaten by sticklebacks.\n• Sticklebacks and dragonfly nymphs are eaten by herons.\n\nSuggest what might happen to the number of pond snails if sticklebacks were removed from the pond. Give a reason for your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• Pond snails would increase (1)\n• because they are no longer eaten by sticklebacks / fewer predators (1).\nAlso credit: the snails might later decrease because they have eaten too much algae and pondweed / more competition for food (1) – maximum 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.8: an energy pyramid for a UK grassland. The producers (grasses) form a wide base, then rabbits, then stoats, with a very narrow top level for buzzards.\n\nSuggest one reason why there are fewer buzzards than rabbits in the grassland shown in Figure 3.8.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `• Energy is lost at each trophic level (1)\n• through movement, heat/respiration, waste or parts not eaten, so only about 10% passes on and less energy is available to support top carnivores (1).\nAccept other developed reasons, e.g. each buzzard needs to eat many prey animals (1) so a large area of rabbits supports only a few buzzards (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.9: a nutrient cycle diagram for a tropical rainforest (Gersmehl model). The biomass store is drawn as a very large circle, the soil store as a medium circle and the litter store as a very small circle. The arrow from litter to soil is very thick.\n\nIdentify the largest nutrient store in Figure 3.9 and give one reason why it is so large.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `• Biomass (1).\n• Reason (1): the warm, wet climate allows year-round growth / dense vegetation takes up nutrients very quickly / nutrients are quickly taken up by roots before they are leached.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nYou have carried out fieldwork in a small-scale UK ecosystem. Students used a 1 m² quadrat to measure the percentage cover of marram grass at five random points on the yellow dunes at Studland, Dorset (data written for this practice question).\nQuadrat: 1 2 3 4 5\nMarram grass cover (%): 45 60 35 50 55\n\nCalculate the mean percentage cover of marram grass. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(45 + 60 + 35 + 50 + 55) = 245, 245 ÷ 5 (1)\n= 49% (1).\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nYou have carried out fieldwork in a small-scale UK ecosystem. Students placed 1 m² quadrats at points chosen using random number tables across an area of woodland floor.\n\nGive one reason why the students used random sampling and one way they could make their results more reliable.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `• Reason (1): to avoid bias / so every part of the area has an equal chance of being chosen / results are more representative.\n• Improvement (1): take more quadrat samples / repeat on different days or at different times / use a quadrat divided into 100 squares to estimate cover / more than one person checks each estimate.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.10: a photograph of leaves on a tree in the understorey of the Amazon rainforest. The leaves are large, waxy and end in long, pointed tips.\n\nExplain how one feature of the leaves shown in Figure 3.10 helps the plant to survive.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `One mark for the feature, one for the developed explanation:\n• Drip tips (1) allow heavy rainfall to run off quickly, stopping algae/fungi growing on the leaf and the leaf breaking under the weight of water (1).\n• Large leaves (1) capture as much of the limited light reaching the understorey as possible for photosynthesis (1).\n• Waxy surface (1) helps water run off and protects against fungi/pests (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.11: a photograph of a hedgerow beside a field in Herefordshire. It shows hawthorn bushes with red berries, ivy and brambles growing through the hedge, a nest in the branches, leaf litter and a hedgehog at the base.\n\nDescribe two ways in which the living components in Figure 3.11 depend on each other.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `One mark per valid interdependence, up to 2 marks:\n• Birds eat hawthorn berries and spread the seeds (1).\n• The hedge provides shelter / nesting sites for birds (1).\n• Ivy uses hawthorn for support to reach the light (1).\n• Hedgehogs feed on insects/worms living in the leaf litter (1).\n• Decomposers break down leaf litter, returning nutrients for the hawthorn (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.12: a climate graph for In Salah, Algeria, in the Sahara hot desert. Temperature bars rise from about 13 °C in January to about 37 °C in July. Rainfall bars are below 5 mm in every month and there is no rain at all in some months.\n\nChoose the two correct statements about Figure 3.12.\nA  The hottest month is January\nB  Rainfall is very low throughout the year\nC  There is a long wet season in summer\nD  The annual temperature range is more than 20 °C\nE  Temperatures fall below freezing in winter`, marks: 2, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)\nD (1).\nIf more than two answers are given, deduct one mark for each extra answer.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nNutrients move between three stores in an ecosystem: biomass, litter and soil.\n\nComplete the sentences using words from the list below.\nweathering   leaf fall   uptake   evaporation\nNutrients move from the biomass to the litter store by ___. Nutrients move from the soil back into plants by root ___.`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `leaf fall (1)\nuptake (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.4 is a climate table for a weather station in the tundra (data written for this practice question). Total annual precipitation is 117 mm.\nMonth: J F M A M J J A S O N D\nMean temperature (°C): −26 −28 −26 −18 −6 2 5 4 −1 −10 −18 −24\nPrecipitation (mm): 5 4 4 5 5 9 22 25 15 12 6 5\n\nComplete the sentences:\n"The annual temperature range is ___ °C."\n"Mean temperatures are above 0 °C in ___ months of the year."\n"The coldest month is ___."`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `33 (°C) (1)\n3 / three (1)\nFebruary (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nWistman's Wood on Dartmoor is a small oak woodland in the UK. In autumn the oak trees drop their leaves onto the woodland floor, where fungi, woodlice and earthworms are found.\n\nDescribe how nutrients are recycled in a deciduous woodland such as this.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `One mark per valid point, up to 3 marks:\n• Leaves/dead plants fall to form leaf litter (1).\n• Decomposers (fungi, bacteria) and detritivores (woodlice, earthworms) break down the litter (1).\n• Nutrients are released into the soil / humus forms (1).\n• Nutrients are taken up by the roots of trees and plants (1).\n• Nutrients are passed to animals when they eat plants, then return when they die or produce waste (1).\n• Nutrients are added to the soil from weathering of rock / rainfall (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.13 shows the energy available at each trophic level in a UK grassland ecosystem (data written for this practice question).\nProducers (grasses): 20,000 kJ per m² per year\nPrimary consumers (rabbits): 2,000 kJ per m² per year\nSecondary consumers (stoats): 180 kJ per m² per year\n\nCalculate the percentage of the energy at the primary consumer level that is passed on to the secondary consumers. Then give one reason why energy is lost between these levels.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 180 ÷ 2,000 × 100 (1)\n• = 9% (1).\n• Reason (1): energy is used in movement / lost as heat in respiration / lost in waste (faeces, urine) / parts of the rabbit (bones, fur) are not eaten.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nYou have carried out fieldwork in a small-scale UK ecosystem. Students walked a transect inland from the sea across the sand dunes at Studland, Dorset (data written for this practice question).\nSite: 1 2 3 4 5\nDistance from the sea (m): 0 50 100 200 400\nNumber of plant species: 2 4 7 11 15\nBare sand (%): 80 55 30 10 2\n\nDescribe the pattern shown by the data. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `One mark per valid point, up to 3 marks:\n• Number of plant species increases with distance from the sea (1).\n• Bare sand decreases with distance from the sea (1).\n• Use of data, e.g. species rise from 2 at 0 m to 15 at 400 m / bare sand falls from 80% to 2% (1).\n• The two variables have a negative (inverse) relationship – more species where there is less bare sand (1).\n• The biggest fall in bare sand is close to the sea, 0–100 m (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.14: a world map of the hot desert biome. Shaded areas include the Sahara and Arabian deserts across North Africa and the Middle East, the Thar desert in India and Pakistan, the Kalahari and Namib deserts in southern Africa, most of central and western Australia, the Atacama desert on the west coast of South America and the Sonoran and Mojave deserts in the south-west USA and Mexico. The Tropics of Cancer and Capricorn are marked.\n\nDescribe the global distribution of hot deserts shown in Figure 3.14.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `One mark per valid point, up to 4 marks. Maximum 3 marks if no named examples.\n• Found in two belts, roughly along the Tropics of Cancer and Capricorn / about 15–30° north and south of the Equator (1).\n• The largest area is the Sahara across North Africa, continuing into the Arabian desert (1).\n• Found on every continent except Europe and Antarctica (1).\n• Many are on the western sides of continents, e.g. Atacama, Namib (1).\n• Found in the interior of large continents, e.g. central Australia (1).\n• More desert in the Northern Hemisphere than the Southern Hemisphere (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.15: a transect drawn from the Equator in the Congo basin north through Europe to the Arctic. It passes in order through tropical rainforest, tropical grassland (savanna), hot desert (Sahara), Mediterranean scrub, temperate deciduous forest, coniferous forest and then tundra at about 70°N.\n\nExplain why different biomes are found at different latitudes along the transect in Figure 3.15.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A clear explanation linking at least two biomes to differences in temperature and rainfall caused by latitude/pressure. Uses geographical terms accurately.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic statements, e.g. "it gets colder further north", with limited explanation.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Near the Equator the sun is high and insolation intense, so it is hot all year.\n• Rising air at the Equator (low pressure) gives heavy convectional rain → dense tropical rainforest.\n• Sinking air at about 30°N (high pressure) gives very little rain → hot desert with sparse vegetation.\n• Savanna lies between the two and has a wet and a dry season.\n• Further north insolation is lower and the growing season shorter → deciduous then coniferous forest.\n• Near the Arctic temperatures are below freezing for most of the year and the ground is frozen (permafrost) → only low tundra plants.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.7 describes a food web for a freshwater pond in the UK:\n• Algae and pondweed are eaten by water fleas and pond snails.\n• Water fleas are eaten by sticklebacks and dragonfly nymphs.\n• Pond snails are eaten by sticklebacks.\n• Sticklebacks and dragonfly nymphs are eaten by herons.\n\nExplain why a large reduction in algae and pondweed would have an impact on the whole food web in Figure 3.7.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A developed explanation that follows the impact through more than one trophic level and recognises the role of producers. Refers to organisms in the food web.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A basic explanation of the impact on one organism.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Algae and pondweed are the producers – they are the only source of energy for the food web.\n• Water fleas and pond snails (primary consumers) would have less food and decrease.\n• Sticklebacks and dragonfly nymphs would then decrease because their prey has fallen.\n• Herons would have less food and might leave the pond or decline.\n• Competition would increase between water fleas and snails, and between sticklebacks and dragonfly nymphs.\n• Pondweed also provides oxygen and shelter, so the loss affects abiotic conditions too.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.5 shows that Station A, in the Amazon basin, has mean monthly temperatures of 26–28 °C and annual rainfall of over 2,000 mm (data written for this practice question).\n\nExplain how the climate shown at Station A leads to the dense vegetation of the tropical rainforest.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A clear explanation of how both high temperatures and high rainfall support plant growth, with links to the growing season or processes.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about heat and rain with limited links to vegetation.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Temperatures stay above about 6 °C all year, so there is a 12-month growing season.\n• High insolation near the Equator provides energy for photosynthesis.\n• Heavy, frequent rainfall means plants are never short of water.\n• Warm, wet conditions allow very fast decomposition so nutrients are quickly available.\n• Plants compete for light, producing tall trees and several layers of vegetation.\n• No cold or dry season means trees can keep their leaves all year (evergreen).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nIn the Amazon rainforest, a leaf that falls to the forest floor may decompose within a few weeks. In a UK oak woodland, a fallen leaf may take a year or more to decompose.\n\nExplain why nutrient cycling is rapid in a tropical rainforest.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A developed explanation of at least two linked reasons, using terms such as decomposers, litter, biomass, uptake, leaching.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic reasons with little development.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Hot and humid conditions all year are ideal for decomposers such as fungi, bacteria and termites.\n• Leaf litter is broken down quickly, so the litter store is very small.\n• Leaves fall throughout the year, giving a constant supply of dead matter.\n• Shallow roots and fungi (mycorrhizae) take nutrients back up quickly.\n• Fast growth of plants needs a constant supply of nutrients.\n• Heavy rainfall leaches nutrients, so plants must take them up quickly before they are lost.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nThe Amazon rainforest in South America is home to animals such as the sloth, the spider monkey, the jaguar and the poison dart frog.\n\nExplain how two animals in a large-scale biome you have studied are adapted to their environment. Name the biome.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Up to 2 marks for each animal: 1 for the adaptation, 1 for the explanation of how it helps survival. Must name a large-scale biome.\nExamples:\n• Sloth: moves very slowly and has algae in its fur (1) – camouflage so it is not seen by predators such as eagles, and it uses little energy on a low-energy leaf diet (1).\n• Spider monkey: long limbs and a prehensile (gripping) tail (1) – can move through the canopy and reach fruit without coming to the ground (1).\n• Jaguar: spotted coat (1) – camouflage in the dappled light of the forest floor to ambush prey (1).\n• Poison dart frog: bright colours and toxic skin (1) – warns predators not to eat it (1).\nCredit adaptations from another named biome, e.g. camel in a hot desert, Arctic fox in the tundra.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nSmall-scale ecosystems in the UK include ponds, hedgerows, oak woodlands and sand dunes.\n\nDescribe the main characteristics of a small-scale ecosystem in the UK you have studied. Name the ecosystem.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A detailed description of a named ecosystem, covering both biotic and abiotic characteristics, with place-specific detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A generic description that could apply to any ecosystem, or a list of plants/animals only.\n0 marks: No relevant content. Maximum 2 marks if no ecosystem is named.\nIndicative content (e.g. Studland sand dunes, Dorset):\n• Abiotic: sandy, dry, salty and windy near the sea; soils become more organic inland.\n• Sequence of zones: embryo dunes with sea couch grass, yellow dunes with marram grass, grey dunes with heather and lichens, then woodland.\n• Marram grass has long roots and rolled leaves to survive dry, windy conditions and traps sand.\n• Animals include sand lizards and smooth snakes, which are rare in the UK.\n• A freshwater lake (Little Sea) forms behind the dunes.\nCredit equivalent detail for other named ecosystems.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nIn the Amazon rainforest, tall trees intercept rainfall and protect the soil, while the soil itself is thin and poor in nutrients.\n\nExplain the interdependence between climate, soil and vegetation in a tropical rainforest.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A clear explanation of at least two links between components, showing how a change in one affects another.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple description of components with few links.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Hot, wet climate → rapid plant growth and dense vegetation.\n• Vegetation intercepts rain and roots bind the soil, reducing soil erosion.\n• Heavy rainfall leaches nutrients from the soil, so the soil is infertile.\n• Most nutrients are held in the biomass, so vegetation depends on rapid recycling of leaf litter.\n• Transpiration from the forest returns moisture to the air, helping to create the rainfall.\n• If trees are removed, rain reaches the soil directly and nutrients are washed away.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.16 shows the relative size of nutrient stores in two biomes (data written for this practice question; units are relative).\nTropical rainforest – biomass 450, litter 5, soil 250\nTemperate deciduous forest (UK) – biomass 280, litter 70, soil 350\n\nCompare the nutrient stores of the two biomes in Figure 3.16. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `One mark per valid comparative point, up to 4 marks. Maximum 3 marks without data.\n• The largest store in the rainforest is biomass, but in the deciduous forest it is the soil (1).\n• The rainforest biomass store is larger (450 v 280) (1).\n• The rainforest litter store is much smaller – 5 compared with 70, 14 times smaller (1).\n• The soil store is larger in the deciduous forest (350 v 250) (1).\n• In both biomes the litter is the smallest store (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.17: a flow diagram of a food web in the tundra. Arctic willow, mosses and lichens → box A → box B (a top predator). Arctic willow, mosses and lichens → box C. Dead remains of all organisms → box D → nutrients in the soil → Arctic willow, mosses and lichens.\n\nMatch each organism to the correct letter (A, B, C or D) in Figure 3.17:\n•  bacteria and fungi\n•  caribou (eaten by no predator in this food web)\n•  lemming\n•  snowy owl`, marks: 4, command: 'Match', markScheme: `lemming – A (1)\nsnowy owl – B (1)\ncaribou – C (1)\nbacteria and fungi – D (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.4 is a climate table for a weather station in the tundra (data written for this practice question).\nMonth: J F M A M J J A S O N D\nMean temperature (°C): −26 −28 −26 −18 −6 2 5 4 −1 −10 −18 −24\nPrecipitation (mm): 5 4 4 5 5 9 22 25 15 12 6 5\nTotal annual precipitation is 117 mm.\n\nChoose the four correct statements.\nA  The warmest month is July.\nB  The annual temperature range is 23 °C.\nC  Total annual precipitation is less than 250 mm.\nD  More than half of the year's precipitation falls in July, August and September.\nE  Temperatures are below freezing for nine months of the year.\nF  The wettest month is also the coldest month.`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `A (1)\nC (1)\nD (1) – 62 mm out of 117 mm (53%)\nE (1).\nIf more than four answers are given, deduct one mark for each extra answer.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nThe tundra of northern Canada and Alaska has a growing season of only about 50–60 days and permafrost a short distance below the surface.\n\nExplain why the tundra has low biodiversity.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A developed explanation linking abiotic conditions to limited plant growth and then to fewer animals.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements, e.g. "it is too cold".\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Very low temperatures for most of the year limit photosynthesis and plant growth.\n• Short growing season means only small, low-growing plants survive.\n• Permafrost stops deep roots and causes waterlogging, so no trees.\n• Low precipitation (often under 250 mm) limits plant growth.\n• Cold slows decomposition, so soils are poor in nutrients.\n• Few producers means short food chains supporting few consumers; many animals migrate or hibernate.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nYou have carried out fieldwork to investigate how vegetation changes across a small-scale ecosystem in the UK, such as a sand dune system or a woodland.\n\nDescribe how you would use quadrats to collect data on vegetation in this ecosystem.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `One mark per valid point, up to 4 marks:\n• Lay out a transect line with a tape measure across the area (1).\n• Place a quadrat (e.g. 1 m² or 0.5 m²) at regular intervals, e.g. every 10 m (systematic), or at random points (1).\n• Identify the plant species in each quadrat using an identification key (1).\n• Estimate percentage cover of each species / count the number of species (1).\n• Record other data at each site, e.g. % bare ground, soil depth, light or wind readings (1).\n• Repeat at several sites / take several quadrats to improve reliability (1).\n• Record results in a table with distance and site number (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.18: a diagram of the layers of the Amazon rainforest. The emergent layer reaches about 45 m with scattered tall trees; the canopy at about 30 m forms a continuous roof; the understorey at about 15 m has young trees and large-leaved plants; the forest floor is dark, with leaf litter, ferns and fungi.\n\nExplain why the tropical rainforest has a layered structure.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): A clear explanation of how competition for light and the conditions at each level produce distinct layers. Refers to at least two layers.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): A description of the layers with little explanation.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• All plants compete for sunlight, so trees grow tall and fast to reach it.\n• Emergents grow above the canopy and must cope with strong winds and sun – supported by buttress roots.\n• The canopy blocks most light (only about 2% reaches the floor), so the understorey is shady.\n• Understorey plants have large leaves to capture the light that does get through.\n• The forest floor is too dark for most plants, so it has decomposers and a thin litter layer.\n• Each layer has its own microclimate, so different animals live at different heights.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.19: a photograph of the Amazon rainforest canopy, showing a tall emergent tree with buttress roots, lianas climbing up the trunk, epiphytes (bromeliads) on the branches, and a toucan feeding on fruit.\n\nExplain how plants and animals in a large-scale biome you have studied are adapted to the environment and to each other.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of adaptations of both plants and animals, linked to specific features of the environment (climate, light, soil) and to interdependence between species. Located detail throughout.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A clear explanation of some adaptations of plants and/or animals with some links to the environment. Interdependence may be implied.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Basic descriptions of adaptations with little explanation.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content (e.g. Amazon rainforest):\n• Buttress roots support tall trees in shallow, nutrient-poor soil.\n• Drip tips shed heavy rainfall; thin, smooth bark because there is no cold season.\n• Lianas root in the ground and climb trees to reach light; epiphytes grow on branches to reach light and collect water from rain.\n• Toucans have long bills to reach fruit; they disperse seeds, so trees depend on them.\n• Fig trees and fig wasps depend on each other for pollination and food.\n• Sloths, monkeys and frogs adapted to life in the canopy (camouflage, gripping tails, toxins).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nIn a small pond in a UK nature reserve, a hot, dry summer lowered the water level and raised the water temperature. Algae then grew very quickly and covered the surface.\n\nExplain how a change to one component of a small-scale UK ecosystem you have studied can affect other components. Name the ecosystem.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): A detailed explanation of a chain of effects through both abiotic and biotic components of a named ecosystem, showing clear understanding of interdependence.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): A clear explanation of some effects, with some links between components.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements about one or two effects.\n0 marks: No relevant content. Maximum Level 2 if no ecosystem is named.\nIndicative content (e.g. a pond):\n• Higher temperature and nutrient run-off cause algal bloom.\n• Algae block light, so pondweed cannot photosynthesise and dies.\n• Decomposers use up oxygen breaking down dead plants and algae.\n• Low oxygen kills fish such as sticklebacks and invertebrates.\n• Herons and dragonflies lose food sources and decline or leave.\n• Dead matter builds up on the bed, so the pond silts up faster.\nCredit other named ecosystems, e.g. removal of a hedgerow species, trampling of marram grass on sand dunes.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.20: two climate graphs. Graph 1 is for Manaus, Brazil (tropical rainforest): temperatures 26–28 °C all year and rainfall over 2,000 mm. Graph 2 is for In Salah, Algeria (hot desert): temperatures from about 13 °C in January to 37 °C in July and rainfall under 20 mm a year. A text box notes that soils, relief and the activity of animals and decomposers also vary between biomes.\n\n"Climate is the most important factor in determining the characteristics of a biome." Do you agree? Justify your views. You may also use examples you have studied.\n[8 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) / AO3 (4 marks).\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): A well-reasoned, balanced argument that weighs climate against other factors (soil, relief, biotic interactions) and reaches a clear, justified conclusion. Accurate use of Figure 3.20 and located examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): A clear argument with some balance; a view is given with some justification. Some use of the figure or examples.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): A simple, one-sided response with limited justification.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• For: temperature and rainfall control growing season and plant growth – e.g. Manaus allows year-round growth and dense forest; In Salah's lack of rain gives sparse vegetation.\n• Climate controls decomposition and nutrient cycling (fast in TRF, slow in tundra).\n• The global pattern of biomes follows climate belts linked to latitude and pressure.\n• Against: soils affect vegetation, e.g. sandy soils on dunes, waterlogged peat on moorland.\n• Relief/altitude changes conditions within a biome (e.g. cloud forest on mountains).\n• Biotic factors – grazing, fire, decomposers, pollinators – shape vegetation (e.g. savanna kept open by grazing and fire).\n• Conclusion: climate is the main driver at a global scale, but other factors matter at a local scale.\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh (3): Spells and punctuates with consistent accuracy; uses rules of grammar with effective control of meaning; uses a wide range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nIntermediate (2): Spells and punctuates with considerable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with general control of meaning; uses a good range of specialist terms as appropriate.\nThreshold (1): Spells and punctuates with reasonable accuracy; uses rules of grammar with some control of meaning; uses a limited range of specialist terms as appropriate.\n0: Writes nothing, or the response does not relate to the question, or errors severely hinder meaning.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.21: a diagram of an oak woodland in the UK. Producers (oak trees, bluebells, bramble) are shown at the top; consumers (caterpillars, blue tits, deer, foxes) in the middle; and decomposers (fungi, bacteria, earthworms) in the leaf litter and soil, with arrows showing nutrients returning to the oak roots.\n\nWhich group – producers or decomposers – is more important for keeping an ecosystem functioning? Justify your view. You may also use examples you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) / AO3 (4 marks).\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): A well-developed argument considering the roles of both groups, recognising interdependence, with a clear and justified judgement. Detailed use of Figure 3.21 and examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): A clear argument with some consideration of both groups and some justification.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): A simple view with limited reasons, perhaps about one group only.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Producers: convert sunlight into energy by photosynthesis – the only source of energy for every food chain (oak leaves → caterpillar → blue tit).\n• Producers provide habitat, shelter and oxygen; without them consumers would die out.\n• Decomposers: break down dead matter and waste, returning nutrients to the soil.\n• Without decomposers, nutrients would be locked in the litter and producers could not grow; litter would build up.\n• In a tropical rainforest, rapid decomposition is essential because the soil is nutrient-poor.\n• Both depend on each other – producers supply dead matter for decomposers; decomposers supply nutrients for producers.\n• Judgement, e.g. producers are the starting point for energy, but decomposers keep the cycle going in the long term, so neither can function alone.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — How ecosystems function.\nFigure 3.22: two fact boxes.\nBox 1 – Studland sand dunes, Dorset: about 2 km² of dunes, heath and a freshwater lake; home to all six UK reptile species, including the rare sand lizard and smooth snake; visited by over a million people a year.\nBox 2 – the Amazon rainforest: about 5.5 million km² across nine countries; contains around 10% of the world's known species; stores huge amounts of carbon and recycles water to the atmosphere.\n\n"Small-scale ecosystems in the UK are just as important as large-scale biomes such as the tropical rainforest." Do you agree? Justify your views.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 (4 marks) / AO3 (4 marks).\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): A balanced, well-supported evaluation of the importance of both ecosystems at different scales, with a clear, justified conclusion. Detailed use of Figure 3.22 and wider knowledge.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): A clear argument with some balance and some justification. Some use of the figure.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): A simple, one-sided view with limited reasons.\n0 marks: No relevant content.\nIndicative content:\n• Amazon: global importance – very high biodiversity, carbon store regulating climate, water cycling, medicines and resources.\n• Amazon: food webs are complex, with many specialised and interdependent species.\n• Studland: habitat for rare species not found elsewhere in the UK (sand lizard, smooth snake).\n• Studland: dunes protect the coast from erosion and flooding; provide recreation and education for local people and visitors.\n• Small-scale ecosystems such as hedgerows and ponds link together to form wildlife corridors.\n• Importance depends on scale – the Amazon matters globally, Studland matters locally and nationally.\n• Conclusion may argue either way if justified, e.g. the Amazon is more important globally, but small-scale ecosystems are vital locally and easier for UK people to protect.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.2': {
    name: 'Ecosystems Under Threat',
    questions: [
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nMany ecosystems, such as tropical rainforests and coral reefs, are being damaged by human activity. Governments and conservation groups try to manage them sustainably.\n\nWhich of the following is the correct definition of sustainable management of an ecosystem?\nA  Stopping all people from entering the ecosystem\nB  Using the ecosystem to meet people's needs today without damaging it for future generations\nC  Removing as many resources as possible while prices are high\nD  Replacing the natural vegetation with a single crop`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nTable 3.1: annual deforestation in the Brazilian Legal Amazon (km² per year), rounded (data written for this practice question, based on Brazilian government satellite estimates).\n2004: 27,800\n2008: 12,900\n2012: 4,600\n2016: 7,900\n2020: 10,900\n2022: 11,600\n2024: 6,300\n\nIdentify the year in Table 3.1 with the lowest rate of deforestation.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `2012 (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.2: a photograph of ivory tusks and rosewood logs seized by customs officers at a port. Trade in elephant ivory and in some rare rainforest timbers is controlled by an international agreement signed by over 180 countries.\n\nName the international agreement that controls the trade in endangered plants and animals.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `CITES / Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species (of Wild Fauna and Flora) (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.3: two photographs of the same part of the Great Barrier Reef, Australia. In the first (taken in 2015) the coral is brightly coloured. In the second (taken in 2016) the coral is white.\n\nWhich of the following is the main cause of the change shown in Figure 3.3?\nA  A fall in the amount of plankton\nB  A rise in sea temperature\nC  An increase in the number of reef fish\nD  A fall in sea level`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nTable 3.1: annual deforestation in the Brazilian Legal Amazon (km² per year), rounded (data written for this practice question, based on Brazilian government satellite estimates).\n2004: 27,800\n2008: 12,900\n2012: 4,600\n2016: 7,900\n2020: 10,900\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in annual deforestation between 2004 and 2012. Show your working. Give your answer to one decimal place.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(27,800 − 4,600) ÷ 27,800 × 100 or 23,200 ÷ 27,800 × 100 (1)\n= 83.5% (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with or without working. Accept 83.45%.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.4: a satellite image of part of Rondônia state, Brazil. Long straight cleared strips branch off either side of a main road, making a 'fishbone' pattern. Between the strips the rainforest is still dark green. The cleared land is mostly pale grass with scattered cattle.\n\nDeforestation is a human activity that threatens the rainforest. Give one reason why people cut down trees in areas like the one shown in Figure 3.4.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason + 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• To create pasture for cattle ranching (1) — beef is sold in Brazil and exported for profit (1)\n• To grow crops such as soya (1) which are exported, e.g. as animal feed to Europe and China (1)\n• To sell valuable hardwood timber such as mahogany (1) for money / export (1)\n• Landless settlers clear land for subsistence farming (1) to grow food for their families (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nThe BR-163 highway runs about 1,700 km north–south through the Amazon from Cuiabá to Santarém. Paving the road made it usable all year round.\n\nSuggest how building roads such as the BR-163 can lead to more deforestation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a suggestion + 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• Roads open up the forest to settlers, loggers and ranchers (1) who clear land on either side of the road (1)\n• Roads make it cheaper to transport timber, beef and soya to ports (1) so clearing the forest becomes more profitable (1)\n• Side roads (often illegal) branch off the main road (1) spreading clearance deeper into the forest (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.5: a fact box about the Amazon rainforest. It covers about 5.5 million km², is home to around 10% of all known species on Earth, contains around 20% of the world's river discharge in the Amazon river system, and supports about 400 indigenous groups. Many medicines, foods and timbers come from rainforest plants.\n\nDescribe two goods that people obtain from tropical rainforests.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each good described, e.g.\n• Timber, e.g. hardwoods such as mahogany for furniture (1)\n• Food, e.g. Brazil nuts, fruit, fish, bushmeat (1)\n• Medicines, e.g. plants used to make drugs (quinine from cinchona bark) (1)\n• Raw materials, e.g. rubber, latex, rattan (1)\n• Fresh water / fish from rivers (1)\nMax 2. Do not credit services (e.g. carbon storage) unless described as a product.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nThe cod fishery on the Grand Banks off Newfoundland, Canada, collapsed in the early 1990s because of overfishing. In 1992 the Canadian government banned cod fishing (a moratorium). More than 30 years later, cod numbers have still not fully recovered.\n\nGive one reason why cod numbers did not quickly recover after fishing was banned.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason + 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• So few adult cod were left (1) that there were not enough to breed and replace the population (1)\n• Cod take several years to mature (1) so the population grows back slowly (1)\n• The food web changed (1) — other species such as shrimp, crab or seals increased and ate young cod / competed for food (1)\n• Cod are still caught as bycatch in other fisheries (1) so numbers stay low (1)\n• Changing sea temperatures / climate change (1) may have reduced food supply or breeding success (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.6: a satellite image of the coast of Queensland, Australia, after heavy rain. A brown plume of muddy water spreads out from the mouth of the Burdekin River across the sea towards the Great Barrier Reef. Inland, the river drains large areas of sugar cane farms and cattle grazing land.\n\nSuggest one way in which farming inland can damage coral reefs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a suggestion + 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• Soil eroded from farmland is carried out to sea as sediment (1) making the water cloudy so less sunlight reaches the coral / smothering the coral (1)\n• Fertiliser run-off adds nutrients to the sea (1) causing algae to grow, which blocks light / helps crown-of-thorns starfish larvae survive and eat coral (1)\n• Pesticides / herbicides wash into the sea (1) and poison coral or the algae living in it (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nStudents investigated the effect of trampling on sand dune vegetation at Studland, Dorset. They placed a 0.5 m × 0.5 m quadrat at 10 m intervals along a footpath and along a parallel line 20 m away from the path, and counted the number of plant species in each quadrat.\n\nSuggest one way the students could make their results more reliable.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a suggestion + 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• Take more quadrat samples / a longer transect (1) so that one unusual quadrat does not affect the results as much (1)\n• Repeat the survey on another day / at another path (1) to check the results are similar (1)\n• Use the same person / an identification key to identify species (1) so plants are not mis-counted (1)\n• Compare quadrats at the same distance from the sea / same dune type (1) so only trampling differs (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nTable 3.7: number of plant species in nine quadrats placed on a trampled footpath across sand dunes (data written for this practice question).\nQuadrat 1: 2\nQuadrat 2: 3\nQuadrat 3: 1\nQuadrat 4: 4\nQuadrat 5: 2\nQuadrat 6: 0\nQuadrat 7: 3\nQuadrat 8: 2\nQuadrat 9: 5\n\nCalculate the median and the range of the data in Table 3.7.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Median = 2 species (1)\nRange = 5 species (5 − 0) (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.8: a map of Australia with the Great Barrier Reef shaded. The reef stretches along the north-east coast of Queensland, from the Torres Strait near Papua New Guinea in the north to near Bundaberg in the south, a distance of about 2,300 km. It lies in the Coral Sea, part of the Pacific Ocean, roughly between 10°S and 24°S.\n\nDescribe the location of the Great Barrier Reef shown in Figure 3.8.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid locational point, e.g.\n• Off the north-east coast of Australia / Queensland (1)\n• In the Coral Sea / Pacific Ocean (1)\n• Between about 10°S and 24°S / in the tropics / south of the equator (1)\n• Stretches about 2,300 km from the Torres Strait / near Papua New Guinea southwards (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nIn 2021 the Central American country of Belize reduced its national debt by about US$190 million. In return, the government agreed to spend money on protecting its coral reefs and expand its marine protected areas to cover 30% of its ocean.\n\nDescribe how a debt-for-nature swap can help protect a threatened ecosystem.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for a basic point + 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• Part of a country's debt is cancelled / bought by an NGO or another government (1) and in return the country spends money on conservation, e.g. protected areas, rangers (1)\n• The country has less pressure to earn money by exploiting the ecosystem (e.g. logging, overfishing) to repay debt (1) so the ecosystem is used less intensively (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nBrazil's National Institute for Space Research (INPE) uses satellite images to measure how much rainforest is cleared each year. It also runs a near-real-time alert system that warns the authorities when new clearing is detected.\n\nSuggest one advantage of using satellite images to monitor deforestation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for an advantage + 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• Can cover very large / remote areas (1) that would be impossible or too expensive to survey on the ground (1)\n• Images are taken regularly (1) so change can be measured year by year / new illegal clearing can be spotted quickly and police sent (1)\n• Gives accurate, consistent data (1) so governments can see whether policies are working (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nIn 2023 and 2024 large parts of the Amazon suffered severe drought. River levels near Manaus fell to their lowest since records began in 1902, and the number of forest fires increased.\n\nGive one way in which climate change may threaten the Amazon rainforest ecosystem.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for a threat + 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• More frequent / longer droughts (1) cause trees to die and the forest to dry out (1)\n• Drier forest burns more easily (1) so fires spread further and destroy habitats (1)\n• Parts of the forest may turn into savanna grassland ('dieback') (1) so many rainforest species lose their habitat (1)\n• Higher temperatures (1) mean some species cannot survive / must move to cooler areas (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.24: a photograph of a small clearing in the Amazon rainforest used by an indigenous family. Cassava and bananas grow among tree stumps, and the surrounding forest is untouched. The family will farm the plot for about three years and then move to a new clearing, leaving the old plot to regrow.\n\nSuggest one reason why this type of farming is sustainable.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason + 1 mark for development, e.g.\n• Only small areas are cleared at a time (1) so the surrounding forest can quickly re-seed the plot (1)\n• The plot is left to regrow (fallow) for many years (1) so leaf litter builds up and soil nutrients are restored (1)\n• Tree stumps and roots are left (1) which hold the soil together and reduce erosion (1)\n• Low population density / food grown only for the family (1) so the forest is not over-used (1)\nMax 2.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nTable 3.9: cod landed from the Grand Banks fishery, Newfoundland (thousand tonnes per year), rounded (data written for this practice question, based on Canadian fisheries records).\n1960: 450\n1968: 810\n1975: 290\n1985: 250\n1990: 220\n1992: 40\n1995: 0 (fishing banned)\n\nDescribe the changes in the amount of cod landed shown in Table 3.9.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid descriptive point; max 2 without data.\n• Catch increased from 450 to a peak of 810 thousand tonnes in 1968 (1)\n• Then fell sharply to 290 thousand tonnes by 1975 (1)\n• Fairly steady / slow decline between 1975 and 1990 (290 to 220) (1)\n• Collapse between 1990 and 1992 (220 to 40) / fell to zero by 1995 when fishing was banned (1)\n• Overall fall of 95% from the 1968 peak to 1992 (1)\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nTable 3.1: annual deforestation in the Brazilian Legal Amazon (km² per year), rounded (data written for this practice question, based on Brazilian government satellite estimates).\n2004: 27,800\n2008: 12,900\n2012: 4,600\n2016: 7,900\n2020: 10,900\n2022: 11,600\n2024: 6,300\n\nDescribe the trend in deforestation shown in Table 3.1.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid descriptive point; max 2 without data.\n• Rapid fall from 27,800 km² in 2004 to a low of 4,600 km² in 2012 (1)\n• Then rose again, more than doubling to 11,600 km² by 2022 (1)\n• Fell again to 6,300 km² in 2024 (1)\n• Overall, deforestation in 2024 was much lower than in 2004 (about a quarter) (1)\n• Some deforestation still occurs every year — the forest is still shrinking (1)\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nIn a tropical rainforest most nutrients are stored in the biomass (living plants), not in the soil. Heavy rain falls almost every afternoon. When the forest is cleared and burned for farming, crop yields are usually good for only two or three years.\n\nExplain why tropical rainforest soils quickly become infertile after deforestation.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid explanatory point or development, e.g.\n• Most nutrients are held in the trees, so removing them removes the main store of nutrients (1)\n• No more leaf litter falls, so nutrients are no longer returned to the soil (1)\n• Without the tree canopy and roots, heavy rain leaches nutrients down through the soil out of reach of plants (1)\n• Bare soil is washed away by surface run-off / soil erosion removes the thin fertile topsoil (1)\n• Crops take up the remaining nutrients and are harvested, so they are not recycled (1)\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nOn Apo Island in the Philippines, villagers agreed in 1982 to set up a small no-take marine sanctuary covering about 10% of the reef around the island. Fishing is banned in the sanctuary and local volunteers patrol it. Fish numbers inside and around the sanctuary have since increased, and divers pay a fee to visit.\n\nExplain how community-run marine sanctuaries like the one on Apo Island can help to manage coral reefs sustainably.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid explanatory point or development, e.g.\n• Banning fishing in part of the reef lets fish populations recover / breed (1)\n• Fish 'spill over' into the areas outside the sanctuary, so fishermen still catch fish there (1)\n• Local people run and patrol it, so they support the rules and illegal fishing is reduced (1)\n• Diving fees give an income from the reef without damaging it, so people value keeping it healthy (1)\n• Healthy reef with more grazing fish keeps algae under control (1)\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nTable 3.1: annual deforestation in the Brazilian Legal Amazon (km² per year), rounded (data written for this practice question, based on Brazilian government satellite estimates).\n2004: 27,800\n2008: 12,900\n2012: 4,600\n2016: 7,900\n2020: 10,900\n2022: 11,600\n2024: 6,300\n\nChoose the correct answer in each statement:\nThe year with the highest deforestation was (2004 / 2012 / 2022).\nBetween 2012 and 2020 deforestation (increased / decreased / stayed the same).\nIn 2016 about (4,600 / 7,900 / 12,900) km² of forest was cleared.\nBetween 2022 and 2024 deforestation fell by approximately (3,300 / 5,300 / 7,300) km².`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `1 mark for each correct answer:\n• 2004 (1)\n• increased (1)\n• 7,900 (1)\n• 5,300 (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nTable 3.10: percentage of coral reefs threatened by local human activities (such as overfishing, coastal development and pollution) in different world regions (data based on the Reefs at Risk Revisited report, 2011). Global average: 75%.\nSouth-East Asia: 95%\nAtlantic (including the Caribbean): 75%\nIndian Ocean: 66%\nMiddle East: 65%\nPacific: 48%\nAustralia: 14%\n\nChoose the four correct statements:\nA  South-East Asia has the highest percentage of threatened reefs.\nB  Australia has the lowest percentage of threatened reefs.\nC  The Pacific is above the global average.\nD  The Indian Ocean and the Middle East have similar percentages of threatened reefs.\nE  More than half of the reefs in the Pacific are threatened.\nF  The percentage of threatened reefs in the Atlantic is the same as the global average.\nG  The Middle East has a higher percentage of threatened reefs than the Atlantic.`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `1 mark for each correct statement: A (1), B (1), D (1), F (1).\nMax 4. If more than four statements are chosen, deduct 1 mark for each incorrect one.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.11: a flow diagram showing how coral bleaching happens. "Sea temperature rises above normal for several weeks" → box A → box B → box C → box D → "Coral may die if high temperatures continue".\n\nMatch each statement to the correct letter (A, B, C or D) in Figure 3.11:\n• The coral turns white because its colourful algae have gone\n• The tiny algae (zooxanthellae) living inside the coral become stressed\n• The coral starves because it has lost its main source of food\n• The coral pushes the algae out of its tissue`, marks: 4, command: 'Match', markScheme: `1 mark for each correct match:\n• A — the tiny algae (zooxanthellae) living inside the coral become stressed (1)\n• B — the coral pushes the algae out of its tissue (1)\n• C — the coral turns white because its colourful algae have gone (1)\n• D — the coral starves because it has lost its main source of food (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.12: a fact box headed "Why rainforests matter to everyone". It states that tropical rainforests store large amounts of carbon in trees and soils, release water vapour that forms rain clouds over large areas, contain more than half of the world's land-based plant and animal species, and are the source of many medicines.\n\nExplain why tropical rainforests are important at a global scale.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 2 marks / AO2 2 marks.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear, developed explanation of two or more reasons, linked to global effects.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements, may list services with little explanation.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Carbon store / sink — trees absorb CO₂ in photosynthesis; clearing releases CO₂, adding to global climate change\n• Water cycle — evapotranspiration forms clouds and rain; affects rainfall far beyond the forest, e.g. farmland in southern Brazil\n• Biodiversity — huge gene pool; loss of species is permanent and affects the whole planet\n• Medicines and food crops — many drugs and products come from rainforest plants; undiscovered species may provide future medicines\n• Oxygen production / regulation of the global climate` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nLarge ecosystems such as tropical rainforests and coral reefs are threatened by many different human activities, including farming, logging, mining, dam building, tourism and fishing.\n\nDescribe how a large-scale ecosystem you have studied is threatened by human activity. Name the ecosystem.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `AO1 4 marks.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear description of several threats with located, specific detail of the named ecosystem.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple, generic description; ecosystem may not be named.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content (Amazon rainforest, Brazil):\n• Cattle ranching — the main cause; cleared land turned into pasture, especially in Pará, Mato Grosso and Rondônia\n• Commercial soya farming in Mato Grosso, with crops exported as animal feed\n• Logging, including illegal logging of hardwoods such as mahogany\n• Mining, e.g. Carajás iron ore mine; illegal gold mining polluting rivers with mercury\n• Roads (BR-163, Trans-Amazonian Highway) opening up the forest to settlers\n• HEP dams such as Belo Monte on the Xingu River flooding forest\nCredit other valid named ecosystems, e.g. the Great Barrier Reef (bleaching, run-off, crown-of-thorns starfish, tourism, shipping), Borneo (palm oil).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.13: a diagram of the water cycle in a rainforest before and after deforestation. Before: rain is intercepted by the canopy, much water is returned to the air by evapotranspiration and rivers flow steadily. After: bare ground, arrows showing more surface run-off, less evapotranspiration, and a muddy river that floods quickly.\n\nExplain how deforestation can change the water cycle in a tropical rainforest.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 2 marks / AO2 2 marks.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear, linked explanation of several changes to the water cycle, using the figure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements, mostly description of the figure.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Less interception — no canopy, so rain hits the ground directly\n• Less infiltration — soil compacted and exposed, so more surface run-off\n• Faster run-off reaches rivers quickly, so rivers flood more often and carry more sediment\n• Less evapotranspiration, so less water vapour returns to the air\n• Fewer clouds form and local rainfall decreases, making the climate drier and droughts more likely\n• Less water stored in vegetation and soil, so rivers may fall very low in the dry season` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.14: a timeline of mass coral bleaching events on the Great Barrier Reef: 1998, 2002, 2016, 2017, 2020, 2022 and 2024. A line graph beneath shows summer sea-surface temperatures on the reef rising since 1900, with the warmest summers in recent years.\n\nExplain how climate change threatens coral reefs. Use Figure 3.14.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO2 2 marks / AO3 2 marks.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear, linked explanation of several threats, using evidence from the figure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements; little use of the figure.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Rising sea temperatures cause corals to expel their algae and bleach; bleaching events are becoming more frequent (two in 1998–2002, five between 2016 and 2024)\n• Less time between events means reefs cannot recover before the next bleaching\n• Ocean acidification — the sea absorbs CO₂, making it harder for corals to build their calcium carbonate skeletons\n• Stronger tropical storms / cyclones may physically break up reefs\n• Sea level rise may leave some corals too deep for enough sunlight\n• Loss of coral reduces habitat for fish, affecting the whole food web` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.15: a photograph of a large factory trawler hauling in a full net. The fact box says that modern trawlers use sonar to find shoals of fish and can catch hundreds of tonnes in one trip, and that bottom trawling drags heavy nets across the sea bed.\n\nGive two reasons why overfishing damages marine ecosystems.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for a reason + 1 mark for development):\n• Fish are caught faster than they can breed (1) so populations collapse, e.g. Grand Banks cod in 1992 (1)\n• Removing a species changes the food web (1) — predators that eat the fish lose their food, while species the fish ate increase (1)\n• Bycatch (1) — dolphins, turtles, sharks and young fish are caught and killed accidentally (1)\n• Bottom trawling damages the sea bed (1) destroying habitats such as cold-water corals (1)\n• Removing grazing fish from coral reefs (1) lets seaweed / algae overgrow the coral (1)\nMax 4.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nSmall-scale ecosystems in the UK, such as heathlands, sand dunes, woodlands and wetlands, are often managed by organisations such as the National Trust, the RSPB or the Wildlife Trusts.\n\nDescribe how a small-scale UK ecosystem you have studied is being managed. Name the ecosystem.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `AO1 4 marks.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear description of several management strategies with specific, located detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple, generic description; ecosystem may not be named.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content (e.g. Studland heath and dunes, Dorset, National Trust):\n• Boardwalks and marked paths to reduce trampling of dune plants\n• Fencing off eroding dunes and planting marram grass to stabilise sand\n• Grazing by cattle / ponies and cutting to stop heathland turning into woodland (succession)\n• Removing invasive species such as rhododendron and pine\n• Controlled burning or fire-watch patrols to reduce wildfire risk\n• Information boards, visitor centre and rangers to educate visitors\n• Part of a National Nature Reserve / SSSI with legal protection\nCredit other valid named UK ecosystems, e.g. Wicken Fen, Epping Forest, Braunton Burrows.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.16: four views about the Amazon rainforest.\nCattle rancher: "I need more land to feed my family and pay off my loans. Beef sells for a good price."\nIndigenous leader: "The forest is our home. Our people have lived here sustainably for thousands of years."\nBrazilian government official: "Mining, farming and dams bring jobs and help Brazil pay its debts."\nInternational conservation charity: "If the Amazon is destroyed, the whole planet will suffer from climate change."\n\nExplain why different groups of people have different views about the use of the Amazon rainforest. Use Figure 3.16.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO2 2 marks / AO3 2 marks.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of why views differ, linked to the needs, values or interests of different groups, using the figure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple description of one or more views with little explanation.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Groups gain or lose in different ways — ranchers and companies earn money from clearing; indigenous people lose their homes, food sources and culture\n• Different priorities — economic development and jobs (government) vs environmental protection (charity)\n• Different scales — local people think about their own living; international groups think about global climate and biodiversity\n• Short term vs long term — profits now vs keeping the forest for future generations\n• Some HIC groups are criticised for asking Brazil not to develop in ways HICs already did` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.17: a fact box about global agreements. CITES (1975) controls trade in endangered species. The Ramsar Convention (1971) protects wetlands of international importance. Under REDD+, richer countries and companies pay countries such as Brazil and Indonesia to reduce deforestation. Norway has given over US$1 billion to Brazil's Amazon Fund.\n\nExplain how international agreements can help to manage ecosystems at a global scale.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 2 marks / AO2 2 marks.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of how two or more agreements protect ecosystems, with examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements or a list of agreements.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• CITES bans or controls trade, so poaching / illegal logging becomes less profitable (e.g. ivory ban)\n• Ramsar designates protected wetlands, so governments must conserve them\n• REDD+ / Amazon Fund give money for keeping forests standing, making conservation worth more than clearing\n• Money pays for monitoring, rangers, sustainable livelihoods for local people\n• Countries work together on threats that cross borders, e.g. migratory species, climate change, ocean fishing\n• Limitations may be credited as development: agreements depend on enforcement; some countries do not sign` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat. You have carried out fieldwork on a small-scale ecosystem.\nStudents used systematic sampling to study sand dune vegetation at Studland, Dorset. They placed a quadrat every 10 m along a transect from the beach to the back of the dunes and estimated the percentage cover of plants.\n\nEvaluate one strength and one weakness of the students' sampling method.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `2 × (1 mark for strength / weakness + 1 mark for evaluation):\nStrengths\n• Systematic sampling covers the whole transect evenly (1) so changes from the beach to the back of the dunes are shown (1)\n• Quick and easy to repeat (1) so other students can check the results (1)\n• Avoids bias (1) because students do not choose 'interesting' spots (1)\nWeaknesses\n• May miss important changes between sample points (1), e.g. a small dune slack between two quadrats (1)\n• Estimating percentage cover is subjective (1) so different students may record different values (1)\n• Only one transect (1) may not be representative of the whole dune system (1)\nMax 2 for strengths, max 2 for weaknesses.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.18: a map of Borneo with oil palm plantations shaded across large areas of Sabah and Sarawak (Malaysia) and Kalimantan (Indonesia). Fact box: Indonesia and Malaysia produce about 85% of the world's palm oil, which is used in foods, cosmetics and biofuel. The Bornean orangutan is listed as critically endangered.\n\nExplain why the growth of palm oil plantations is a threat to the rainforest ecosystem in Borneo.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 2 marks / AO2 2 marks.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear, linked explanation of several threats, with reference to Borneo.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Large areas of rainforest are cleared and replaced by a monoculture of oil palms, so biodiversity falls sharply\n• Loss of habitat for species such as orangutans, pygmy elephants, hornbills — populations fall, some critically endangered\n• Forest fragmented into small patches, so animals cannot move between areas to find food or mates\n• Burning to clear land releases CO₂ and causes smoke haze; draining peat swamp forest releases large amounts of stored carbon\n• Fertiliser and pesticide run-off pollutes rivers\n• Driven by high global demand for cheap palm oil, so pressure continues` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nLough Neagh in Northern Ireland is the largest freshwater lake in the UK. In summer 2023 a huge bloom of blue-green algae covered much of the lake, turning the water green. Scientists linked it to nutrients from farm fertiliser and slurry, sewage, warmer temperatures and invasive zebra mussels, which make the water clearer so algae grow better.\n\nExplain how human activity can threaten a small-scale freshwater ecosystem such as Lough Neagh.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 2 marks / AO2 2 marks.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear, linked explanation of how human activities harm the ecosystem, using the example.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• Fertiliser and slurry run-off / sewage add nitrates and phosphates (eutrophication)\n• Algae grow rapidly and block sunlight, so plants under the water die\n• Bacteria decompose dead algae and use up oxygen, so fish and invertebrates die\n• Some blue-green algae are toxic — harmful to pets, livestock and people; water supply affected\n• Invasive species introduced by people (zebra mussels) change the food web\n• Climate change — warmer summers help algae grow\n• Impacts on people, e.g. eel fishery and tourism` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.19: two photographs of the Xingu region of the Brazilian Amazon. The first shows cleared land with cattle grazing and a muddy river. The second shows smoke rising from a forest fire next to an indigenous village.\n\nExplain the impacts of deforestation on people and the environment in a tropical rainforest you have studied. Name the rainforest.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 3 marks / AO2 3 marks.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): detailed, well-explained impacts on both people and the environment, with specific, located detail of the named rainforest.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): some explanation of impacts; may be unbalanced between people and environment; some place detail.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple, generic statements; rainforest may not be named.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content (Amazon, Brazil):\nEnvironment\n• Loss of habitat and biodiversity; species extinction\n• Soil erosion and loss of nutrients; rivers silted up\n• Less evapotranspiration — drier local climate, more fires\n• Release of CO₂ from burning, adding to climate change; parts of the Amazon now release more carbon than they absorb\nPositive and negative impacts on people\n• Indigenous groups (e.g. Kayapó) lose land, food sources and culture; conflict and violence over land\n• Disease and mercury pollution from gold mining\n• Jobs and income in farming, logging and mining; exports earn money for Brazil (beef, soya)\n• Roads and HEP (Belo Monte) bring electricity and development but displace communities\nCredit other valid named rainforests, e.g. Borneo, the Congo Basin.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.20: a fact box about world fisheries. About one-third of the world's fish stocks are now fished at unsustainable levels. Illegal, unreported and unregulated fishing may take up to 26 million tonnes of fish a year. Most of the ocean beyond 200 nautical miles from the coast is 'high seas', which no single country controls. The EU and UK set yearly catch quotas for their own waters.\n\nExplain why it is difficult to manage the world's fisheries sustainably. Use Figure 3.20 and examples you have studied.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO2 3 marks / AO3 3 marks.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): detailed, well-linked explanation of several difficulties, using the figure and examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): some explanation of difficulties; limited use of the figure or examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements, mostly copied from the figure.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\n• High seas are not owned by any country, so rules are hard to agree and enforce ('tragedy of the commons')\n• Illegal fishing is difficult to detect over huge ocean areas; patrols and satellite monitoring are expensive\n• Fish move across borders, so countries must cooperate — disputes over quotas (e.g. mackerel in the North Atlantic)\n• Fishing communities depend on catches for jobs and food; governments may set quotas higher than scientists advise\n• Rising global population and demand for fish increases pressure\n• Hard to count fish stocks accurately, so quotas may be wrong; once stocks collapse they may not recover (Grand Banks cod)\n• Some success may be credited: marine protected areas, MSC certification, no-take zones such as Apo Island` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.21 shows two views about protecting threatened ecosystems.\nView A — global scale: "Only international action can save ecosystems. Agreements such as CITES and funds such as REDD+ and the Amazon Fund give countries the money and rules they need."\nView B — local scale: "Local people know their environment best. Community projects such as the no-take marine sanctuary on Apo Island in the Philippines work because local people run them and benefit from them."\n\nIs managing ecosystems at a global scale or at a local scale more effective? Justify your views. You may also use examples you have studied. [8 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): thorough evaluation of both global and local management, using the figure and own examples; reaches a well-supported, justified decision.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): some evaluation of both, or detailed on one; a decision with some justification.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements; little or no justification.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\nGlobal scale\n• Deals with threats that cross borders, e.g. climate change, ocean fishing, trade in endangered species\n• Provides large sums of money, e.g. Norway's payments to the Amazon Fund; debt-for-nature swaps (Belize 2021)\n• CITES ivory ban reduced legal trade\n• But depends on governments signing and enforcing; slow to agree; money may not reach local people; enforcement in remote areas is weak\nLocal scale\n• Local people have knowledge and a direct interest, so rules are respected (Apo Island fish numbers increased)\n• Cheap, flexible, can start quickly; brings income, e.g. ecotourism fees\n• UK examples: National Trust management of Studland heath and dunes\n• But small areas only; cannot stop global threats such as coral bleaching from warming seas; may lack money and legal power\nJudgement may favour either, or argue that both are needed (global funding and rules, local action and enforcement).\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh (3): spelling and punctuation consistently accurate; meaning always clear; wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate (2): spelling and punctuation considerable accuracy; meaning clear overall; good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold (1): reasonable accuracy; does not hinder meaning; limited specialist terms.\n0: errors severely hinder meaning, or answer not related to the question.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.22: a fact box about the Great Barrier Reef, Australia. It is about 2,300 km long and is a World Heritage Site. Threats include mass coral bleaching (2016, 2017, 2020, 2022, 2024), outbreaks of crown-of-thorns starfish, sediment and fertiliser run-off from farms, coastal development, shipping and about 2 million tourists a year. The Great Barrier Reef Marine Park Authority zones the reef, and about one-third of it is closed to fishing.\n\n'Climate change is a greater threat to coral reefs than local human activities.' To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use Figure 3.22 and examples you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): balanced, well-supported evaluation of climate change and local threats, using the figure and located evidence; clear, justified conclusion about the extent of agreement.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): both types of threat considered with some evidence; conclusion partly justified.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements; one-sided; little or no conclusion.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\nClimate change\n• Rising sea temperatures cause mass bleaching; five events in nine years on the Great Barrier Reef, with less time to recover\n• Ocean acidification weakens coral skeletons\n• Stronger cyclones break reefs\n• Global problem — even well-managed, remote reefs are affected; cannot be solved by Australia alone\nLocal human activities\n• Run-off from sugar cane and cattle farms (sediment, fertiliser) — helps crown-of-thorns starfish outbreaks\n• Overfishing removes grazing fish, so algae overgrow coral; blast fishing in South-East Asia (95% of reefs threatened locally)\n• Coastal development, dredging, shipping accidents, tourist damage (anchors, trampling)\n• Local threats make reefs less able to recover from bleaching\nConclusion may depend on location: in Australia, where local threats are relatively well managed, climate change is the greatest threat; in South-East Asia local threats may matter more; the two threats combine.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Ecosystems under threat.\nFigure 3.23: a fact box about Costa Rica, Central America. Forest cover fell to about one-fifth of the country in the 1980s, mainly because of cattle ranching. Since then it has recovered to over half of the country. About a quarter of Costa Rica's land is now protected in national parks and reserves. Since 1997 the government has paid landowners to protect and replant forest, and tourism, much of it ecotourism, is now one of the country's largest earners.\n\nShould ecotourism be used as the main way to manage threatened ecosystems sustainably? Justify your views. You may also use examples you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 4 marks / AO3 4 marks.\nLevel 3 (6–8 marks): thorough evaluation of the advantages and disadvantages of ecotourism compared with other strategies, using the figure and own examples; reaches a well-supported, justified decision.\nLevel 2 (3–5 marks): some evaluation of ecotourism, possibly one-sided; a decision with some justification.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): simple statements; little or no justification.\n0: nothing creditworthy.\nIndicative content:\nFor ecotourism\n• Earns money from keeping the ecosystem intact, so forests and reefs are worth more standing than cleared\n• Provides jobs for local people as guides, in lodges, selling crafts, reducing the need for logging or hunting\n• Entrance fees pay for rangers and conservation; visitors are educated about the environment\n• Costa Rica: forest cover recovered alongside growth of ecotourism\nAgainst / limitations\n• Visitors still cause damage — trampling, waste, disturbance of wildlife; long-haul flights add to climate change\n• Only suits attractive, accessible places; numbers must be limited, so income is limited\n• Income can fall sharply, e.g. during the COVID-19 pandemic\n• Profits may go to foreign tour companies rather than local people\n• Does not tackle the main drivers such as global demand for beef, soya or palm oil\nOther strategies to compare: payments for ecosystem services (Costa Rica since 1997), protected areas, sustainable forestry (selective logging, FSC certification), REDD+, debt-for-nature swaps, marine protected areas, international agreements (CITES)\nJudgement may support ecotourism as one part of a wider management plan rather than the only strategy.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.3': {
    name: 'Water Resources and Management',
    questions: [
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nWater stress is a growing problem in many parts of the world.\n\nWhich of the following is the correct definition of water stress?\n\nA  When a country has more water than it needs\nB  When the demand for water is greater than the amount of good-quality water available\nC  When water is polluted by sewage from towns and cities\nD  When rainfall is higher than average for several months`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `B (1)\nDo not credit more than one answer.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 1: two pie charts of the world's water. Chart 1 shows all water on Earth: salt water 97.5%, fresh water 2.5%. Chart 2 shows where the fresh water is found: ice caps and glaciers 68.7%, groundwater 30.1%, surface water and other 1.2%.\n\nUsing Figure 1, calculate the percentage of the world's fresh water that is not stored as ice.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `31.3% (1)\n(100 − 68.7 = 31.3, or 30.1 + 1.2 = 31.3)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nAn OS map at a scale of 1:50,000 shows a reservoir in mid-Wales. Measured on the map, the reservoir is 7 cm long from the dam wall to its northern end.\n\nCalculate the actual length of the reservoir in kilometres.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `3.5 km (1)\n(7 × 50,000 = 350,000 cm = 3.5 km)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nA student carried out a survey of how water is used in one household. They divided the total daily use into six activities: showers and baths, toilet flushing, kitchen taps, washing machine, garden and car washing, other.\n\nWhich graph would be most suitable to present this data?\n\nA  Line graph\nB  Pie chart\nC  Scatter graph`, marks: 1, command: 'Which', markScheme: `B (1)\nPie chart (shows parts of a whole).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nIn the UK, most rain falls in the north and west, but most people live in the south and east. Some water is moved from areas of surplus to areas of deficit.\n\nName one water transfer scheme in the UK.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Any one from (1):\n• Elan Valley reservoirs to Birmingham (Elan aqueduct)\n• Kielder Water transfer (to the Rivers Wear and Tees)\n• Lake Vyrnwy to Liverpool\n• Thirlmere / Haweswater to Manchester\nAllow any other valid named UK transfer scheme.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 2: a table of the total renewable water available per person each year in seven countries, A to G (data written for this practice question).\n\nCountry | Water available (m³ per person per year)\nA | 450\nB | 1,200\nC | 3,100\nD | 820\nE | 2,400\nF | 1,650\nG | 600\n\nUsing Figure 2, calculate the median amount of water available per person. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Values placed in order: 450, 600, 820, 1,200, 1,650, 2,400, 3,100 (1)\nMedian = 1,200 m³ per person per year (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 3: a line graph of the amount of water stored in reservoirs in one region of England during 2022, as a percentage of full capacity (data written for this practice question). January 90%, March 92%, May 85%, July 65%, August 58%, September 60%, November 78%.\n\nUsing Figure 3, complete the sentences:\n"Reservoir stocks were lowest in ______."\n"Between March and August reservoir stocks fell by ______ percentage points."`, marks: 2, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `August (1)\n34 (percentage points) (1)\n(92 − 58 = 34)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nA reservoir in the south of England held 180 billion litres of water in April. After a very dry summer it held 117 billion litres in September (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage change in the volume of water stored. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(117 − 180) ÷ 180 × 100 (1)\n= −35% / a 35% decrease (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks. Allow 35% if 'decrease' is clear.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nChina is a NIC whose cities and industries have grown rapidly since the 1980s.\n\nSuggest two reasons why the demand for water in a NIC such as China is increasing.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Any two from (1 each):\n• population growth / more people needing drinking water\n• urbanisation – people in cities use more water (piped supply, flushing toilets, showers)\n• industrial growth – factories use water for cooling and processing\n• more electricity generation (power stations need cooling water; HEP)\n• more irrigation to grow food for a larger population\n• rising incomes / changing diets, e.g. more meat, which needs more water to produce\nAllow any other valid reason.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 4: a map of Wales and the English Midlands. The Elan Valley reservoirs are shown in mid-Wales, about 8 km west of the town of Rhayader and about 100 km west of Birmingham. An aqueduct runs east from the reservoirs to Birmingham.\n\nUsing Figure 4, describe the location of the Elan Valley reservoirs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Any two from (1 each):\n• in mid-Wales / central Wales\n• (about 8 km) west of Rhayader\n• about 100 km west of Birmingham\n• in the uplands / Cambrian Mountains (credit valid place knowledge)\nAllow direction and distance used together for 2 marks, e.g. 'about 100 km west of Birmingham in mid-Wales'.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nThe Elan aqueduct carries water about 117 km from the Elan Valley reservoirs to Birmingham using gravity alone. The water falls about 52 m in height along its whole length.\n\nCalculate the average gradient of the aqueduct. Give your answer as a ratio in the form 1 : n.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `117 km = 117,000 m; 117,000 ÷ 52 (1)\n= 1 : 2,250 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 5: two maps of the UK. Map 1 shows average annual rainfall: over 2,000 mm in the Scottish Highlands, the Lake District and Snowdonia; 1,000–2,000 mm across most of western Britain and Northern Ireland; under 700 mm in East Anglia, Essex and around London. Map 2 shows population density: highest in London and the south-east, the West Midlands, Greater Manchester and central Scotland; lowest in the Scottish Highlands and mid-Wales.\n\nChoose the two correct statements:\nA  The wettest areas of the UK are in the east.\nB  London has less than 700 mm of rainfall each year.\nC  The Scottish Highlands have a high population density and high rainfall.\nD  The south-east has a high population density and low rainfall.\nE  Mid-Wales has low rainfall and a high population density.`, marks: 2, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)\nD (1)\nMaximum 2 marks. If more than two letters are chosen, deduct 1 mark for each incorrect letter.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 6: a photograph of a field of tomatoes in Spain. A thin black plastic pipe runs along the bottom of each row of plants, and small drops of water fall from holes in the pipe next to each plant stem.\n\nThe farmer in Figure 6 uses drip irrigation. Give one reason why drip irrigation uses less water than spraying fields.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• water is delivered straight to the roots (1) so little is wasted on bare soil between the rows / less runoff (1)\n• water is not sprayed into the air (1) so less is lost by evaporation, especially in hot weather (1)\n• small, controlled amounts are given (1) so the soil is not over-watered and water does not drain away below the roots (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nA student asked eight households in a town in Kent to record how many litres of water each person used per day (data written for this practice question).\n\nHousehold | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8\nLitres per person per day | 128 | 145 | 152 | 110 | 160 | 138 | 171 | 132\n\nCalculate the mean water use per person per day. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `128 + 145 + 152 + 110 + 160 + 138 + 171 + 132 = 1,136; 1,136 ÷ 8 (1)\n= 142 litres per person per day (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 7: a world map of water scarcity. Physical water scarcity (not enough water to meet demand) covers North Africa, the Middle East, northern China and the south-west of the USA. Economic water scarcity (water is available but people cannot afford to collect, treat and pipe it) covers most of sub-Saharan Africa and parts of South and South-East Asia. Little or no water scarcity is shown across most of Europe, Canada and South America.\n\nUsing Figure 7, identify one area with physical water scarcity and one area with economic water scarcity.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Physical: any one of North Africa / Middle East / northern China / south-west USA (1)\nEconomic: any one of sub-Saharan Africa / parts of South Asia / parts of South-East Asia (1)\nCredit named countries that clearly fall in these areas, e.g. Egypt or Saudi Arabia (physical); Ethiopia or Uganda (economic).` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nYou have carried out fieldwork on water use. You gave a questionnaire to 10 people outside a supermarket on a weekday morning to find out how much water households in your town use.\n\nSuggest one reason why your sample may not be representative of all households in the town.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• sample too small (1) – 10 people cannot show the range of household sizes and habits in a whole town (1)\n• weekday morning (1) – people at work or school are missed, so families / working adults may be under-represented (1)\n• one location only (1) – people from other parts of the town, e.g. larger houses with gardens, may not use that supermarket (1)\n• people may estimate or guess their use (1) so the data may not be accurate (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 8: a table of two water security indicators for Uganda (data written for this practice question).\n\nIndicator | Value\nPeople with safe drinking water at home | 40%\nAverage time to collect water each day | 30 minutes\n\nGive two additional pieces of data that could be added to Figure 8 to help show how water insecurity affects people in Uganda.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Any two from (1 each):\n• number / percentage of people with access to a toilet / improved sanitation\n• number of cases of / deaths from water-borne diseases, e.g. cholera, diarrhoea\n• percentage of children (girls) missing school to collect water\n• water available per person per year (m³)\n• cost of water / percentage of income spent on water\n• crop yields or number of people affected by drought\nAllow any other relevant indicator.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nIsrael and the United Arab Emirates (UAE) get a large share of their drinking water from desalination plants, which remove salt from seawater.\n\nSuggest one reason why desalination is less often used to supply water in LICs.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development, e.g.:\n• desalination plants are very expensive to build (1) and LICs may not be able to afford the capital cost / need loans (1)\n• the process uses large amounts of energy (1) which is expensive and may not be available in LICs with unreliable electricity supplies (1)\n• many LICs are landlocked / far from the coast (1) so seawater would have to be pumped long distances inland (1)\n• the main problem in many LICs is economic scarcity (1) – they need cheap local wells and pipes rather than more water (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 9: a divided bar chart showing how water is used in two groups of countries (data written for this practice question).\n\nCountry group | Agriculture | Industry | Domestic\nLICs | 87% | 5% | 8%\nHICs | 41% | 42% | 17%\n\nUsing Figure 9, complete the sentences:\n"The largest use of water in LICs is ______."\n"In HICs, industry uses ______% of water."\n"Domestic use in HICs is ______ percentage points higher than in LICs."`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `agriculture (1)\n42 (1)\n9 (1)\n(17 − 8 = 9)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nMany geographers say a country is under water stress if it has less than 1,700 m³ of renewable water per person each year, and faces water scarcity if it has less than 1,000 m³ per person.\n\nCountry X has 50 billion m³ of renewable water each year and a population of 40 million (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the renewable water available per person each year in Country X, and state whether it is under water stress, water scarcity or neither.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `50,000,000,000 ÷ 40,000,000 (1)\n= 1,250 m³ per person per year (1)\nWater stress (between 1,000 and 1,700 m³) (1)\nAllow the final mark if correctly classified from an incorrect value carried forward.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 18: a map of UK average annual rainfall: over 2,000 mm in the Scottish Highlands, the Lake District and Snowdonia; 1,000–2,000 mm across most of western Britain and Northern Ireland; under 700 mm in East Anglia, Essex and around London.\n\nDescribe the pattern of rainfall in the UK shown in Figure 18.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark per valid point, up to 3:\n• rainfall is higher in the west / north-west and lower in the east / south-east (1)\n• highest rainfall (over 2,000 mm) in upland areas, e.g. Scottish Highlands / Lake District / Snowdonia (1)\n• lowest rainfall (under 700 mm) in East Anglia / around London (1)\n• use of data, e.g. over 2,000 mm compared with under 700 mm – about three times as much (1)\nMaximum 2 marks without use of map evidence.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 10: a cross-section of a sand dam in Kitui County, Kenya. A concrete wall has been built across a seasonal river bed. Behind the wall, sand washed down by the river during the rainy season has filled the channel. Water is stored between the grains of sand. A pipe from the bottom of the sand leads to a hand pump on the bank.\n\nUsing Figure 10, explain how a sand dam provides water for people during the dry season.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark per valid point, up to 3:\n• during the rainy season the wall traps sand carried by the river (1)\n• water is stored in the spaces between the sand grains (1)\n• the sand covers the water so little is lost by evaporation in the dry season (1)\n• the sand filters the water so it is cleaner / safer to drink (1)\n• people collect it through the pipe and hand pump / by digging scoop holes (1)\n• water also soaks into the banks, raising the water table for wells and crops (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 11: a world map of water stress by country (the share of available fresh water that is used each year). Extremely high water stress (over 80%) is shown across North Africa, the Middle East, Pakistan and northern India. High stress (40–80%) covers much of southern Europe, Mexico, the south-west USA, northern China and South Africa. Low stress (under 10%) covers Canada, most of South America, central Africa, Russia and Scandinavia.\n\nDescribe the global pattern of water stress shown in Figure 11.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Award 1 mark per valid point, up to 4. Credit:\n• extremely high stress is concentrated in North Africa and the Middle East (1)\n• also in South Asia – Pakistan / northern India (1)\n• high stress in hot, dry areas between about 20° and 40° north, e.g. Mexico, south-west USA, southern Europe (1)\n• northern China and South Africa also have high stress (1)\n• low stress in Canada, South America, central Africa, Russia (1)\n• low stress areas are generally either cold / far north or wet equatorial regions (1)\n• use of map data, e.g. over 80% in the Middle East compared with under 10% in Canada (1)\nMaximum 3 marks if no use of data or named areas from the map.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 12: a table of the average water used per person per day in one UK home (data written for this practice question).\n\nActivity | Litres per person per day\nShowers and baths | 45\nToilet flushing | 31\nKitchen taps and washing up | 28\nWashing machine | 20\nOther | 10\nGarden and car washing | 6\nTotal | 140\n\nUsing Figure 12, choose the correct answer in each statement:\nThe activity that uses the most water is (toilet flushing / showers and baths / washing machine).\nToilet flushing is about (10% / 22% / 45%) of the total.\nThe washing machine and garden and car washing together use (16 / 26 / 36) litres.\nIf shower and bath use fell by one third, each person would save (10 / 15 / 30) litres per day.`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `showers and baths (1)\n22% (1) (31 ÷ 140 × 100 = 22.1%)\n26 (1) (20 + 6)\n15 (1) (45 ÷ 3)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nWorld demand for fresh water has more than doubled since the 1960s and is expected to keep rising.\n\nExplain why the global demand for water is increasing.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, well-developed explanation; points are linked and applied to the context.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic, largely descriptive points with little development.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\n\nIndicative content:\n• population growth – the world population passed 8 billion in 2022, so more water is needed for drinking, cooking and washing\n• agriculture uses about 70% of fresh water; more irrigation is needed to feed more people\n• changing diets as incomes rise – more meat and dairy, which have large water footprints\n• industrialisation in NICs, e.g. China and India – factories, cooling and processing\n• energy – power stations need cooling water; more HEP and biofuels\n• urbanisation and rising living standards – piped water, flushing toilets, showers, washing machines, swimming pools\nLevel 2 requires explanation of why each factor increases water use, not just a list.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nThe Elan Valley reservoirs in mid-Wales were built between 1893 and 1904 to supply water to Birmingham, about 117 km away. Several farms, a school and a church were flooded when the valley was dammed.\n\nGive two reasons why water transfer schemes such as the Elan Valley scheme can be controversial.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `Two reasons, each 1 mark for the reason + 1 mark for development:\n• people lose homes and land (1) – communities were moved and farms, a school and a church were drowned in the Elan Valley (1)\n• water is taken from one area / country to benefit another (1) – many people in Wales felt Welsh water was used for English cities without enough benefit to local people (1)\n• environmental damage (1) – flooding a valley destroys habitats; changes river flow / ecosystems downstream (1)\n• very high cost (1) – building dams and pipelines is expensive and water bills may rise (1)\n• transfer may not be needed if demand is cut (1) – e.g. reducing leakage or metering may be cheaper (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nLarge-scale water management schemes such as dams and water transfers are often built in LICs and NICs to increase water supply.\n\nDescribe how a large-scale water management scheme in an LIC or NIC you have studied supplies water. Name the scheme.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, well-developed explanation; points are linked and applied to the context.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic, largely descriptive points with little development.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\n\nNo name of a scheme – maximum Level 1.\nIndicative content, e.g.:\n• South-North Water Transfer Project, China (NIC): moves water from the wetter south (Yangtze basin) to the dry north; the central route carries water from the Danjiangkou Reservoir over 1,200 km in canals and tunnels to Beijing and Tianjin; the eastern route uses the old Grand Canal and pumping stations; the central route opened in 2014\n• Three Gorges Dam, China: dam across the Yangtze creates a reservoir hundreds of km long; stores flood water; supplies water and HEP\n• Lesotho Highlands Water Project: Katse and Mohale dams store water in the mountains of Lesotho; tunnels transfer it to Gauteng (Johannesburg), South Africa; HEP generated at Muela\n• Itaipu Dam on the Paraná River (Brazil/Paraguay): reservoir stores water and generates HEP\nLevel 2 requires a named scheme with specific located detail of how water is stored and/or moved.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 13: two satellite images of the Aral Sea in Central Asia. In 1989 it is a large inland sea. In 2014 the eastern basin has dried out completely and only small, separate lakes remain. Rivers flowing into the sea, the Amu Darya and Syr Darya, have been used since the 1960s to irrigate cotton fields.\n\nExplain the impacts of over-abstraction of water shown in Figure 13.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, well-developed explanation; points are linked and applied to the context.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic, largely descriptive points with little development.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\n\nIndicative content:\n• so much water was taken from the Amu Darya and Syr Darya for irrigation that little reached the sea, so it shrank\n• fishing industry collapsed (e.g. at Moynaq / Muynak) – boats stranded, jobs lost\n• exposed sea bed left salt, dust and pesticide residues – dust storms cause breathing problems and damage crops\n• the remaining water became saltier, killing fish and wildlife\n• local climate changed – hotter summers, colder winters without the sea's influence\n• farmland suffered salinisation from poor irrigation\nLevel 2 requires explanation linking the abstraction to its consequences.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nThe UK Met Office predicts that UK summers will become hotter and drier and that winter rainfall will become heavier.\n\nExplain how climate change may affect water supply in the UK.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, well-developed explanation; points are linked and applied to the context.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic, largely descriptive points with little development.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\n\nIndicative content:\n• drier summers mean less rain to refill reservoirs and rivers, so more droughts and hosepipe bans (e.g. 2022)\n• hotter weather increases evaporation from reservoirs and soils\n• demand rises in hot weather – gardens, showers, farmers irrigating\n• heavy winter rain falls quickly and runs off rather than soaking in, so aquifers may not recharge fully\n• the south-east, already water stressed, may face the greatest shortfall\n• low river flows concentrate pollution, making water harder to treat\nLevel 2 requires clear links between the climate change and the effect on supply.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nSmall-scale sustainable schemes such as wells with hand pumps, rainwater harvesting and sand dams are often run by NGOs such as WaterAid.\n\nDescribe how a small-scale water scheme in an LIC you have studied has improved people's lives. Name the scheme or place.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, well-developed explanation; points are linked and applied to the context.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic, largely descriptive points with little development.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\n\nNo named scheme or place – maximum Level 1.\nIndicative content, e.g. sand dams in Kitui County, Kenya, or WaterAid wells in Ethiopia / Malawi / Uganda:\n• clean water close to homes – less time spent walking to collect water\n• girls can go to school instead of collecting water; women can earn money\n• fewer water-borne diseases, e.g. diarrhoea, cholera\n• water for vegetable gardens / livestock improves food supply and income\n• local people trained to maintain pumps – cheap, uses appropriate technology\nLevel 2 requires specific detail about how the named scheme helped people.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nThe UK has a high average rainfall, yet the Environment Agency classifies large parts of south-east England as 'seriously water stressed'.\n\nExplain why south-east England suffers from water stress.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, well-developed explanation; points are linked and applied to the context.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic, largely descriptive points with little development.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\n\nIndicative content:\n• low rainfall – under 700 mm a year in places, much less than in the north-west\n• it is in the rain shadow of upland areas to the west; prevailing south-westerly winds drop most rain over the west\n• high population density – London and the south-east have millions of people\n• high demand from homes, offices and industry; new house building increases demand\n• hot summers – high evaporation and more garden watering\n• leaking pipes lose large amounts of water\n• relies heavily on groundwater (chalk aquifers) and rivers such as the Thames, which are low in drought\nLevel 2 requires both physical and human reasons, explained.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 14: a table comparing water use in two countries (data written for this practice question).\n\nCountry | Domestic water use (litres per person per day) | People with safe water at home (%)\nUnited Kingdom | 140 | 100\nMozambique | 20 | 30\n\nUsing Figure 14, compare water use in the UK and Mozambique. Use data in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Award 1 mark per valid comparative point, up to 4:\n• the UK uses much more water per person per day than Mozambique (1)\n• 140 compared with 20 litres – a difference of 120 litres (1)\n• UK use is 7 times that of Mozambique (140 ÷ 20 = 7) (1)\n• everyone in the UK has safe water at home, compared with only 30% in Mozambique (1)\n• 70 percentage points more people in the UK have safe water at home (1)\nMaximum 2 marks if no comparative language is used. Maximum 2 marks without data.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nYou have carried out a questionnaire survey to investigate how much water households use and whether they try to save water.\n\nEvaluate one strength and one weakness of the method you used to collect your data.`, marks: 4, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Strength (2 marks: 1 for the strength + 1 for development), e.g.:\n• questionnaires collect primary data directly from residents (1) so the data is up to date and relevant to the local area (1)\n• the same questions were asked to everyone (1) so the answers can be compared fairly (1)\nWeakness (2 marks: 1 for the weakness + 1 for development), e.g.:\n• people often do not know how much water they use (1) so they guess and the data may be inaccurate (1)\n• people may give the answer they think is expected, e.g. saying they save water (1) which makes results biased (1)\n• small sample (1) so results may not represent the area (1)\nCandidates must refer to their own method; maximum 2 marks for a generic answer.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nIn the UK each person uses about 140 litres of water per day at home. Water companies are encouraging customers to save water.\n\nExplain how households in the UK can reduce the amount of water they use.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark per valid point and 1 further mark for development, up to 4. Credit, e.g.:\n• fitting a water meter (1) – people pay for what they use, so they are encouraged to use less (1)\n• water butts collect rainwater from roofs (1) which can be used to water gardens instead of tap water (1)\n• grey water recycling (1) – water from baths and sinks is reused to flush toilets (1)\n• dual-flush or low-flush toilets / water-saving shower heads (1) use fewer litres each time (1)\n• shorter showers instead of baths; turning off taps while cleaning teeth (1)\n• fixing leaking taps and pipes (1)\n• using washing machines / dishwashers only when full (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nIn many parts of sub-Saharan Africa, women and children walk several kilometres each day to collect water from rivers or open wells.\n\nExplain the consequences of a lack of safe water for people in LICs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, well-developed explanation; points are linked and applied to the context.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic, largely descriptive points with little development.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\n\nIndicative content:\n• water-borne diseases such as cholera, typhoid and diarrhoea, which kill many young children\n• time spent collecting water means girls miss school and women cannot work, keeping families in poverty\n• poor sanitation and hygiene spread disease\n• less water for crops and livestock – lower yields, food shortages, malnutrition\n• carrying heavy containers causes injuries; risk of attack on long journeys\n• ill health reduces the workforce and slows development\nLevel 2 requires a range of consequences that are explained, not just listed.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 15: a fact box about the River Nile.\n• The Nile flows through 11 countries.\n• Egypt gets over 90% of its fresh water from the Nile and has very little rainfall.\n• Most of the Nile's water comes from rain falling on the Ethiopian Highlands.\n• Ethiopia has built the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam (GERD) on the Blue Nile to generate HEP.\n\nUsing Figure 15, explain why water can cause conflict between countries.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 2 (3–4 marks): clear, well-developed explanation; points are linked and applied to the context.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic, largely descriptive points with little development.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\n\nIndicative content:\n• the Nile is shared – countries upstream control how much water flows downstream\n• Egypt depends almost entirely on the Nile, so any fall in flow threatens its farming and drinking water\n• Ethiopia wants to use 'its' water for HEP and development\n• filling the GERD reservoir reduces flow downstream, especially during droughts\n• rising populations and demand in all Nile countries increase competition\n• there is no agreement that all countries accept about sharing the water\nLevel 2 requires use of the resource and clear explanation of why sharing leads to tension.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 16: a fact box about the Three Gorges Dam, China.\n• The dam is across the Yangtze River and was completed in 2006.\n• Its reservoir stretches over 600 km upstream.\n• It can generate up to 22,500 MW of HEP.\n• Over 1.3 million people were moved from towns and villages flooded by the reservoir.\n• The dam traps sediment that used to be carried downstream.\n\nExplain why large dams such as the Three Gorges Dam have both positive and negative effects on the environment.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): thorough, well-developed explanation covering a range of points; clear use of located detail.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of some points; some located detail; may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points, mainly descriptive, with little or no located detail.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\n\nIndicative content:\nPositive:\n• HEP is renewable, so less coal is burned – lower CO₂ emissions and air pollution\n• flood control protects farmland and wildlife downstream\n• reservoir provides a regular water supply in dry seasons\nNegative:\n• flooding of land destroyed habitats and farmland along the valley\n• sediment trapped behind the dam – less fertile silt downstream; coastline / delta erosion\n• fish migration blocked; river ecosystems changed (e.g. decline of river dolphins and fish species)\n• water quality problems – pollution builds up in the slow-moving reservoir\n• landslides on the steep valley sides as water levels change\nCredit use of Figure 16. Level 3 requires both positive and negative effects, explained.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nWater companies in England have warned that, without action, demand for water could be greater than supply in some regions in the coming decades.\n\nExplain how the UK is managing water supply to meet future demand.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Level 3 (5–6 marks): thorough, well-developed explanation covering a range of points; clear use of located detail.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): clear explanation of some points; some located detail; may be unbalanced.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): basic points, mainly descriptive, with little or no located detail.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\n\nIndicative content:\n• increasing supply: building new reservoirs (e.g. proposed reservoir near Abingdon, Oxfordshire); transferring water between regions (e.g. proposed Severn–Thames transfer; existing Kielder and Elan Valley schemes); desalination (e.g. Beckton plant, London)\n• reducing demand: compulsory water meters in water-stressed areas; education campaigns; water-efficient appliances in new homes\n• reducing waste: repairing leaking pipes – a large share of supply is lost through leaks\n• reusing water: grey water systems; treated wastewater returned to rivers\n• hosepipe bans during droughts\nLevel 3 requires a range of supply and demand strategies with located UK detail.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nLarge-scale schemes such as the Three Gorges Dam (China) and the Lesotho Highlands Water Project can store and move huge volumes of water. Small-scale schemes such as sand dams (Kenya), rainwater harvesting and wells with hand pumps are cheaper and run by local communities.\n\nAre large-scale or small-scale schemes the more sustainable way to manage water in LICs and NICs? Justify your views. You may use examples you have studied. [8 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–8 marks): thorough evaluation with a well-supported judgement that is reached through weighing up both sides; accurate, detailed use of examples (AO2 and AO3 both strong).\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion of more than one side with some supporting detail; a judgement is given but may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic, one-sided or generalised points; little or no judgement; little use of examples.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\nAO split: AO2 4 marks, AO3 4 marks.\n\nIndicative content:\nLarge-scale – for: supply very large populations and cities; also provide HEP and flood control; can transfer water from surplus to deficit areas (e.g. Lesotho to Gauteng; South-North transfer in China).\nLarge-scale – against: very expensive, often needing loans/foreign investment; displace people (over 1.3 million at Three Gorges); flood habitats; trap sediment; benefits may go to cities or industry rather than the rural poor.\nSmall-scale – for: cheap, appropriate technology; local people trained to maintain them; benefits go directly to the poorest; little environmental damage; e.g. sand dams in Kitui County.\nSmall-scale – against: supply only a village; may fail in long droughts; rely on NGO funding; cannot supply large cities or industry.\nA judgement may argue that sustainability depends on scale of need, or that a combination is best.\nLevel 3 requires a clear, supported judgement using located examples.\n\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh performance (3): spelling and punctuation are consistently accurate; grammar is used to control meaning; a wide range of specialist terms is used precisely.\nIntermediate performance (2): spelling and punctuation are considerably accurate; meaning is generally controlled; a good range of specialist terms is used.\nThreshold performance (1): spelling and punctuation are reasonably accurate; any errors do not hinder meaning overall; a limited range of specialist terms is used.\n0: writes nothing, or the response does not relate to the question, or errors severely hinder meaning.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nFigure 17: a fact box about water supply in south-east England.\n• London has an average rainfall of about 600 mm a year.\n• The population of London and the south-east is expected to keep growing.\n• Water companies have proposed a Severn–Thames transfer to move water from the River Severn into the River Thames during dry periods.\n• Some environmental groups argue that fixing leaks and fitting water meters would be cheaper and do less harm.\n\nShould the UK build more large water transfer schemes to supply south-east England? Justify your views.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–8 marks): thorough evaluation with a well-supported judgement that is reached through weighing up both sides; accurate, detailed use of examples (AO2 and AO3 both strong).\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion of more than one side with some supporting detail; a judgement is given but may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic, one-sided or generalised points; little or no judgement; little use of examples.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\nAO split: AO2 4 marks, AO3 4 marks.\n\nIndicative content:\nFor: south-east has low rainfall and high, rising demand; reliable supply in droughts; uses surplus water from wetter areas in the west/north; past schemes (e.g. Elan Valley, Kielder) have supplied water for decades.\nAgainst: very expensive – costs passed on in water bills; environmental impacts on rivers (e.g. changes to water temperature and ecosystems in the Thames, spread of invasive species); the source areas may themselves suffer drought as the climate changes; reducing demand and leakage may be cheaper and more sustainable; local opposition.\nAlternatives: metering, leakage repair, new reservoirs, desalination, grey water recycling.\nLevel 3 requires a clear judgement supported by use of Figure 17 and own knowledge.` },
      { q: `Theme 3: Environmental Challenges — Water resources and management.\n\nLarge-scale water management schemes are often built in LICs and NICs to supply water and electricity.\n\nEvaluate the success of a large-scale water management scheme in an LIC or NIC you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Level 3 (7–8 marks): thorough evaluation with a well-supported judgement that is reached through weighing up both sides; accurate, detailed use of examples (AO2 and AO3 both strong).\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): clear discussion of more than one side with some supporting detail; a judgement is given but may be partly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): basic, one-sided or generalised points; little or no judgement; little use of examples.\n0 marks: no creditable response.\nAO split: AO2 4 marks, AO3 4 marks.\n\nIf no named scheme – maximum Level 1.\nIndicative content, e.g. Three Gorges Dam, China:\nSuccesses: up to 22,500 MW of HEP, reducing China's reliance on coal; flood control on the Yangtze protects millions downstream; larger ships can reach Chongqing, boosting trade; reliable water supply for industry and farming.\nProblems: over 1.3 million people resettled, some to poorer land; cultural and archaeological sites flooded; sediment trapped, reducing fertility downstream; landslides along the reservoir; pollution builds up in the slow-moving water; very high cost.\nOther valid schemes: South-North Water Transfer Project (China); Lesotho Highlands Water Project; Itaipu Dam (Brazil/Paraguay); Aswan High Dam (Egypt).\nLevel 3 requires a supported judgement about how successful the scheme has been, weighing benefits against costs, with specific detail.` }
    ]
  },
  '3.4': {
    name: 'Desertification',
    questions: [
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nDrylands cover about 41% of the Earth's land surface and are home to about 2 billion people. Many dryland areas are at risk of desertification.\n\nChoose the correct definition of the term 'desertification'.\nA  The natural movement of sand dunes across a hot desert\nB  The degradation of land in arid, semi-arid and dry sub-humid areas caused by climatic variations and human activities\nC  The removal of all trees from an area of tropical rainforest\nD  A long period with no rainfall in a temperate climate`, marks: 1, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `B (1)\nThe degradation of land in arid, semi-arid and dry sub-humid areas caused by climatic variations and human activities.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nTable 1 shows how drylands are classified using the aridity index (average annual precipitation ÷ potential evapotranspiration).\nHyper-arid: less than 0.05\nArid: 0.05 to less than 0.20\nSemi-arid: 0.20 to less than 0.50\nDry sub-humid: 0.50 to less than 0.65\nWeather station X (data written for this practice question) has an average annual precipitation of 450 mm and a potential evapotranspiration of 1,500 mm.\n\nIdentify the dryland class of weather station X.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Semi-arid (1)\n(450 ÷ 1,500 = 0.30, which falls in the 0.20 to less than 0.50 class.)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nTable 2 shows millet yields (kg per hectare) recorded on seven farms in central Burkina Faso in one year (data written for this practice question):\n420, 510, 380, 610, 450, 395, 700\n\nCalculate the range of millet yields shown in Table 2.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `320 kg per hectare (1)\n(700 − 380 = 320)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFigure 1 is a photograph of a field in the Yatenga region of northern Burkina Faso in the dry season. The hard, crusted soil has been dug into rows of small round holes, each about 20–30 cm across and 10–15 cm deep. A handful of manure has been placed in each hole, ready for seeds to be planted when the rains arrive.\n\nName the traditional farming technique shown in Figure 1.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `Zaï pits / zaï holes / planting pits (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFigure 2 is a map of Africa showing the Sahel. It is shaded as a narrow belt running west to east across the continent, roughly between latitudes 12°N and 18°N. It stretches from Senegal and Mauritania on the Atlantic coast to Sudan and Eritrea near the Red Sea. The Sahara Desert lies to the north of the belt and the tropical savanna grasslands lie to the south.\n\nDescribe the location of the Sahel shown in Figure 2.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid locational point, max 2:\n• A belt across (northern) Africa / runs west to east (1)\n• Between about 12°N and 18°N / north of the Equator (1)\n• South of the Sahara Desert / on the southern edge of the Sahara (1)\n• North of the savanna (1)\n• From the Atlantic coast (Senegal/Mauritania) to the Red Sea (Sudan/Eritrea) (1)\nDo not credit "in Africa" on its own.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFigure 3 is a climate graph for Niamey, Niger, in the Sahel. Average monthly maximum temperatures are above 30 °C in every month, peaking at about 40 °C in April and May. Total annual rainfall is about 540 mm. Almost no rain falls from November to April; most rain falls between June and September, with the highest total, about 190 mm, in August.\n\nUsing Figure 3, give two characteristics of the climate of Niamey.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark per characteristic, max 2:\n• Hot all year / temperatures above 30 °C every month (1)\n• Highest temperatures in April/May, about 40 °C (1)\n• Low annual rainfall, about 540 mm (1)\n• Long dry season, November to April (1)\n• Rain is seasonal / concentrated in June–September / wettest month August (1)\nCredit use of figures from the resource.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nTable 3 shows annual rainfall (mm) at a weather station in northern Senegal, 2015–2022 (data written for this practice question):\n2015: 412\n2016: 365\n2017: 298\n2018: 455\n2019: 330\n2020: 270\n2021: 388\n2022: 342\n\nCalculate the mean annual rainfall for 2015–2022. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: 412 + 365 + 298 + 455 + 330 + 270 + 388 + 342 = 2,860; 2,860 ÷ 8 (1)\nAnswer: 357.5 mm (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 2 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nIn one district of central Mali, the number of cattle grazing on communal land was 48,000 in 1990 and 81,600 in 2020 (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage change in the number of cattle between 1990 and 2020. Show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: (81,600 − 48,000) ÷ 48,000 × 100 or 33,600 ÷ 48,000 × 100 (1)\nAnswer: +70% / an increase of 70% (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nIn a trial in Burkina Faso, sorghum was grown on fields with and without stone lines. Fields without stone lines produced an average of 380 kg per hectare. Fields with stone lines produced an average of 650 kg per hectare (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in yield on the fields with stone lines. Give your answer to the nearest whole number.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Working: (650 − 380) ÷ 380 × 100 or 270 ÷ 380 × 100 (1)\nAnswer: 71% (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nIn the Sahel, total rainfall can change greatly from one year to the next. At one station in Niger the driest year in a decade had 180 mm of rain and the wettest had 720 mm (data written for this practice question).\n\nSuggest one reason why the mean may not be a useful way of summarising rainfall for farmers in the Sahel.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development:\n• The mean is affected by extreme values / very wet or very dry years (1), so it may give an amount of rain that rarely actually falls (1).\n• Rainfall is so variable (180–720 mm) (1) that farmers cannot plan for the 'average' year; they need to know how often droughts happen (1).\n• The mean hides when the rain falls (1); a late or short rainy season can ruin crops even in a year with average total rainfall (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nIn rural Niger most households cook using firewood. Around many villages, trees and shrubs have been cut down over a wide area.\n\nExplain one way that deforestation can lead to desertification.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for the point, 1 mark for development:\n• Roots no longer bind the soil (1), so the soil is more easily eroded by wind and heavy rain (1).\n• Less leaf fall / organic matter added to the soil (1), so the soil loses nutrients and becomes infertile (1).\n• The soil is exposed to the sun (1), so it dries out and forms a hard crust that rain cannot infiltrate (1).\n• Less transpiration / less shade (1), so the local climate becomes drier (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFigure 4 is a photograph of a farm in the Negev Desert, Israel. Rows of young crops are watered by thin black plastic pipes that drip water directly onto the soil at the base of each plant. The ground between the rows is dry.\n\nSuggest how the method shown in Figure 4 could help to reduce desertification.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark per point or 1 + 1 for a developed point, max 2:\n• Drip irrigation delivers water straight to the roots (1), so less water is lost through evaporation / less is wasted (1).\n• Uses less water than flood irrigation (1), so groundwater is not over-abstracted (1).\n• Avoids over-watering (1), so salinisation of the soil is reduced (1).\n• Keeps plants growing (1), which protects the soil from erosion (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nA student collected annual rainfall totals for a weather station in Chad for each year from 1960 to 2020 (data written for this practice question). She wants to present the data to show how rainfall has changed over time.\n\nSuggest the most appropriate graph to present the data. Give one reason for your choice.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Graph (1): line graph / bar graph (or a bar chart of rainfall for each year).\nReason (1): the data are continuous over time / shows the trend over time / shows year-to-year change clearly / a trend line can be added.\nDo not credit pie chart or scatter graph.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nIn parts of northern Nigeria, farmers used to leave fields fallow (unused) for around 10 years so the soil could recover. Because of population growth, fields are now often left fallow for only 1 or 2 years (data written for this practice question).\n\nGive two ways that over-cultivation can lead to desertification.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark per way, max 2:\n• The soil loses nutrients / becomes infertile because it has no time to recover (1)\n• Crops remove nutrients faster than they are replaced (1)\n• Ploughing breaks up the soil structure so it is blown or washed away (1)\n• Bare soil is left between harvests / in the dry season and is exposed to wind erosion (1)\n• Cultivation spreads onto marginal land that is too dry for crops (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFigure 5 is a photograph of land near a village in northern Burkina Faso. The ground is bare and covered in a hard, cracked crust. Small gullies have been cut into the slope. Several dead acacia trees stand with no leaves, and sand has piled up against the wall of a hut.\n\nIdentify two pieces of evidence from Figure 5 that this land is affected by desertification.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `1 mark per piece of evidence, max 2:\n• Bare ground / little or no vegetation (1)\n• Hard, crusted / cracked soil (1)\n• Gullies, showing soil erosion by water (1)\n• Dead trees (1)\n• Sand piled against the hut, showing wind erosion / blowing sand (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFact box: the Great Green Wall was launched by the African Union in 2007. It was first planned as a continuous line of trees about 15 km wide across the Sahel from Senegal to Djibouti. It has since become a 'mosaic' of different land uses, including farmland, grazing land, tree planting and protecting trees that grow naturally.\n\nSuggest one reason why the Great Green Wall changed from a line of trees to a mosaic of land uses.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a reason, 1 mark for development:\n• Many planted trees died in the dry conditions (1), so protecting trees that regrow naturally was more successful (1).\n• Local people need land for farming and grazing (1), so a mosaic gives them food and income and they are more likely to support it (1).\n• A single line of trees cannot stop desertification on its own (1), because degradation is caused by land use across the whole region, not by the desert 'advancing' (1).\n• A mosaic is cheaper / more flexible (1) and can be adapted to local conditions in each country (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nIn many dryland areas temperatures are high for most of the year and the rainfall that does fall often comes in short, heavy storms.\n\nGive two physical causes of desertification.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1 mark per cause, max 2:\n• Drought / a long period of below-average rainfall (1)\n• Rainfall variability / unreliable rainfall (1)\n• High temperatures causing high evaporation (1)\n• Intense storms that erode the soil / cause run-off (1)\n• Strong winds that blow away dry topsoil (1)\n• Climate change causing reduced rainfall or higher temperatures (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nTable 4 shows the average annual rainfall in the western Sahel for each decade (data written for this practice question):\n1950s: 540 mm\n1960s: 510 mm\n1970s: 400 mm\n1980s: 360 mm\n1990s: 420 mm\n2000s: 450 mm\n2010s: 470 mm\n\nUsing Table 4, complete the sentences:\n"The wettest decade was the ___."\n"The difference in average rainfall between the 1950s and the 1980s was ___ mm."\n"Between the 1950s and the 1980s average rainfall fell by ___ %."`, marks: 3, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `1 mark for each correct answer:\n• 1950s (1)\n• 180 mm (540 − 360) (1)\n• 33% / 33.3% (180 ÷ 540 × 100) (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nA region in the Sahel has an area of 52,000 km². Its population was 1.3 million in 1990 and 3.12 million in 2020 (data written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the change in population density between 1990 and 2020. Show your working.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• 1990 density: 1,300,000 ÷ 52,000 = 25 people per km² (1)\n• 2020 density: 3,120,000 ÷ 52,000 = 60 people per km² (1)\n• Change: an increase of 35 people per km² (1)\nCorrect final answer with no working = 3 marks.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFigure 6 is a bar graph showing Sahel rainfall anomalies (difference from the long-term average) from 1950 to 2020. From 1950 to the mid-1960s most bars are above the average line. From the late 1960s almost every bar is below the average, and the largest negative bars are in the early to mid-1980s. From the mid-1990s the bars are mixed: there are more years above average than in the 1980s, but many years are still below average and the bars vary greatly in size from year to year.\n\nDescribe the changes in Sahel rainfall shown in Figure 6.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid descriptive point, max 3:\n• Wetter than average from 1950 to the mid-1960s (1)\n• Rainfall fell below average from the late 1960s (1)\n• The driest period was the early/mid-1980s (1)\n• Partial recovery / wetter since the mid-1990s (1)\n• But rainfall has not returned to 1950s levels / many years are still below average (1)\n• Rainfall is very variable from year to year (1)\nCredit accurate use of dates from the resource.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFarmers in the Yatenga region of Burkina Faso place lines of stones, about 25 cm high, along the contours of their gently sloping fields.\n\nExplain how lines of stones can help to improve crop yields.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point, with further marks for development, max 3:\n• The stones slow down surface run-off after heavy rain (1)\n• so more water infiltrates into the soil / soil moisture increases (1), giving plants more water during dry spells (1).\n• The stones trap soil and organic matter washed down the slope (1), so topsoil and nutrients are kept on the field (1).\n• Less soil erosion means the field stays fertile over time (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFigure 7 is a world map showing vulnerability to desertification. Areas of high and very high vulnerability form a band across North Africa along the southern edge of the Sahara (the Sahel), and also cover parts of southern Africa around the Kalahari, the Middle East, Central Asia, north-west India and Pakistan, and northern China and Mongolia. Smaller areas are found in the western USA and northern Mexico, north-east Brazil, Argentina, the Mediterranean coast of Spain, and around the edges of the Australian interior. The large hot deserts themselves, the tropical rainforests and the high latitudes of North America, northern Europe and Russia are shown as not vulnerable.\n\nDescribe the global distribution of areas vulnerable to desertification shown in Figure 7.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point; credit use of named places/continents from the resource, max 4. Max 2 if no named examples.\n• Found on every inhabited continent (1)\n• The largest area is across North Africa / the Sahel, south of the Sahara (1)\n• Mostly on the edges / margins of the hot deserts (1)\n• Mainly in the tropics and sub-tropics / between about 15° and 40° north and south (1)\n• Large areas across Asia, e.g. Middle East, Central Asia, India/Pakistan, China/Mongolia (1)\n• Smaller areas in the Americas, e.g. western USA, north-east Brazil, Argentina (1)\n• Few or none in high latitudes / rainforests / near the Equator (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nTable 5 shows data for five Sahel countries (data written for this practice question):\nSenegal: population 2000 9.8 million; population 2020 16.4 million; land degraded 34%\nMali: population 2000 11.0 million; population 2020 20.3 million; land degraded 59%\nNiger: population 2000 11.3 million; population 2020 24.2 million; land degraded 49%\nChad: population 2000 8.4 million; population 2020 16.4 million; land degraded 45%\nMauritania: population 2000 2.6 million; population 2020 4.5 million; land degraded 23%\n\nUsing Table 5, choose the four correct statements.\nA  Niger had the largest population in 2020.\nB  Mauritania had the highest percentage of land degraded.\nC  Niger's population more than doubled between 2000 and 2020.\nD  Senegal's population increased by 6.6 million between 2000 and 2020.\nE  Mali had the highest percentage of land degraded.\nF  Chad's population grew by a smaller percentage than Mauritania's.\nG  The country with the smallest population had the highest percentage of land degraded.`, marks: 4, command: 'Choose', markScheme: `1 mark for each correct statement, max 4:\nA (1)  C (1)  D (1)  E (1)\nChecks: Niger 24.2 ÷ 11.3 = 2.14 (more than doubled); Senegal 16.4 − 9.8 = 6.6 million; Chad grew by about 95%, Mauritania by about 73%, so F is false; Mauritania had the lowest (23%), so B and G are false.\nIf more than four letters are given, deduct 1 mark for each extra letter.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nIn northern Burkina Faso, herders keep large numbers of cattle and goats on communal grazing land. Herd sizes are often increased after wet years, but the animals stay on the same land when drought returns.\n\nExplain how overgrazing can lead to desertification.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Too many animals for the carrying capacity of the land; grass and shrubs eaten faster than they regrow.\n• Vegetation cover removed; roots no longer hold the soil.\n• Trampling by hooves compacts the soil / breaks up the surface, so rain runs off instead of infiltrating.\n• Bare soil eroded by wind and by heavy rain; fertile topsoil lost.\n• Less organic matter; soil becomes infertile so plants cannot regrow — a cycle that worsens in drought.\n\nBand 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation of how overgrazing leads to desertification, with a developed chain of reasoning linking vegetation loss to soil degradation.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements with limited explanation, e.g. "animals eat the grass".\n0: No creditworthy response.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nNiger has one of the fastest-growing populations in the world. Women in Niger have, on average, about 6 to 7 children each, and most people depend on farming and herding for their food and income.\n\nExplain why population growth increases the risk of desertification.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• More people need more food, so more land is cultivated, including marginal land that is too dry.\n• Fallow periods are shortened, so soil does not recover its nutrients.\n• Larger herds are kept, leading to overgrazing.\n• More fuelwood and building timber is needed, leading to deforestation.\n• More water is taken from wells and rivers, lowering water tables.\n• All of these remove vegetation and expose soil to erosion, especially in drought years.\n\nBand 2 (3–4 marks): Clear, developed explanation linking population growth to at least two pressures on the land and to land degradation.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): Simple, undeveloped statements, e.g. "more people need more food".\n0: No creditworthy response.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nThe Sahel is a semi-arid region across Africa where about 80% of people depend on farming or herding. Desertification affects large areas of countries such as Niger, Mali and Burkina Faso.\n\nDescribe the impacts of desertification on people living in the Sahel.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid impact, with a second mark for development or exemplification, max 4:\n• Lower crop yields / crop failure (1), leading to food shortages, malnutrition or famine (1).\n• Less grazing land so livestock die (1), so herders lose their wealth and income (1).\n• Water shortages / wells dry up (1), so women and children walk further to collect water (1).\n• Less fuelwood (1), so people spend more time collecting it or more money buying it (1).\n• Migration to cities such as Niamey or Nouakchott or abroad (1), splitting up families (1).\n• Conflict between farmers and herders over land and water (1).\n• Dust storms cause breathing problems / damage to homes (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nScientists report that average temperatures in the Sahel have risen faster than the global average and that rainfall has become less reliable, with more frequent droughts and more intense storms.\n\nExplain how climate variability and climate change can cause desertification.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Droughts / several dry years in a row: vegetation dies back, leaving bare soil.\n• Unreliable rainfall: crops fail and plants cannot re-establish.\n• Higher temperatures increase evaporation and evapotranspiration, so soils dry out further.\n• Intense storms on dry, crusted soil cause rapid run-off and gully erosion instead of infiltration.\n• Dry, loose soil is blown away by wind; loss of topsoil and nutrients.\n• Climate stress makes human pressures (grazing, cultivation) more damaging.\n\nBand 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation with developed links between changes in climate and land degradation.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements, e.g. "less rain so plants die".\n0: No creditworthy response.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nTable 6 shows data for a village in Niger before and after a soil and water conservation project (zaï pits, stone lines and protecting young trees) (data written for this practice question):\nSorghum yield: 2005 350 kg per hectare; 2020 780 kg per hectare\nDepth to water in the village well: 2005 18 m; 2020 12 m\nTree cover on village land: 2005 3%; 2020 15%\nHouseholds (out of 150) with a member leaving each dry season to find work: 2005 40; 2020 12\n\nUsing Table 6, describe the changes in the village between 2005 and 2020.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `1 mark per valid change; at least two changes must use data for full marks, max 4:\n• Sorghum yield more than doubled / rose by 430 kg per hectare (about 123%) (1)\n• The water table rose / water is 6 m closer to the surface (1)\n• Tree cover increased five times / by 12 percentage points (1)\n• Fewer households sent people away to work: down from 40 to 12 (by 28) (1)\n• Overall the village became more food-secure / conditions improved (1)\nMax 2 if no data used.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFact box: the Great Green Wall aims to restore 100 million hectares of degraded land across the Sahel by 2030 and create 10 million jobs. A UN report in 2020 found that only a small proportion of the target had been reached. Some countries along the route, including Mali and Burkina Faso, have experienced armed conflict.\n\nGive two reasons why the Great Green Wall has made slow progress.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `For each reason: 1 mark for the reason, 1 mark for development, 2 × 2:\n• Lack of money / funding promised has not all arrived (1), so fewer trees planted / projects stopped (1).\n• Conflict and insecurity (1) make it unsafe for workers and prevent planting / monitoring in places like Mali and Burkina Faso (1).\n• Many planted seedlings die (1) because of drought, high temperatures or being eaten by livestock (1).\n• Lack of involvement of local people at first (1), so trees were not cared for or were cut down (1).\n• The area is very large / crosses many countries (1), making organisation and co-operation difficult (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nSince the 1980s, farmers in the Maradi and Zinder regions of Niger have used farmer-managed natural regeneration. Instead of clearing all tree stumps from their fields, they protect and prune the shoots that grow from living roots. Over 5 million hectares of farmland have been 're-greened' in this way.\n\nExplain how sustainable farming methods such as this can reduce desertification.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Trees regrow from existing roots, so they survive drought better than planted seedlings and cost little.\n• Roots bind the soil; tree cover acts as a windbreak, reducing wind erosion.\n• Leaf litter adds organic matter and nutrients; some trees fix nitrogen, so crop yields rise.\n• Shade reduces evaporation and keeps soil moist.\n• Prunings provide fuelwood and fodder, reducing pressure to cut other trees.\n• Farmers own and benefit from the trees, so the method is sustainable in the long term.\n\nBand 2 (3–4 marks): Clear, developed explanation of how the method protects or improves the soil and why it is sustainable.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements, e.g. "trees stop erosion".\n0: No creditworthy response.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFigure 8 is a flow diagram showing how desertification can develop in a dryland village: "Population grows" → box A → "Vegetation is removed" → box B → "Soil is eroded by wind and rain" → box C → box D → back to "Population grows" (the remaining land is put under even more pressure).\n\nComplete Figure 8 by matching each statement to the correct letter (A, B, C or D):\n• Yields fall and some land is abandoned\n• More animals are grazed and more trees are cut for fuel\n• Soil is left bare and exposed to the sun\n• Soil loses its fertility`, marks: 4, command: 'Complete', markScheme: `1 mark for each correct match:\nA — More animals are grazed and more trees are cut for fuel (1)\nB — Soil is left bare and exposed to the sun (1)\nC — Soil loses its fertility (1)\nD — Yields fall and some land is abandoned (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nNouakchott, the capital of Mauritania, grew from a small town in the 1960s into a city of over one million people. Much of the early growth came from herders and farmers who left the countryside during the Sahel droughts of the 1970s and 1980s.\n\nExplain why desertification can lead to migration.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Land becomes too degraded to farm; crops fail; food shortages.\n• Livestock die from lack of grazing and water; herders lose their livelihood.\n• No income in rural areas, so people move to cities (rural–urban migration) for paid work or food aid.\n• Seasonal migration: young men leave in the dry season to send remittances home.\n• Competition and conflict over scarce land and water push people out.\n• International migration, e.g. to coastal West African countries or Europe.\n\nBand 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation with developed links between land degradation, loss of livelihood and movement of people.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements, e.g. "people move to find food".\n0: No creditworthy response.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nFact box (costs written for this practice question): building stone lines on one hectare of farmland costs about US$40, mainly for transporting stones, and is done by local people using simple tools. Installing centre-pivot irrigation on one hectare costs over US$2,000 and needs a reliable electricity or fuel supply, imported spare parts and trained engineers.\n\nSuggest why appropriate technology such as stone lines is more suitable than high-technology solutions in rural areas of the Sahel.`, marks: 4, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark per valid point, with a second mark for development, max 4:\n• Much cheaper (US$40 compared with over US$2,000 per hectare) (1), so poor farmers can afford it without loans (1).\n• Uses local materials and skills / simple tools (1), so it can be repaired and maintained locally (1).\n• Does not need electricity, fuel or imported parts (1), which are unreliable or unavailable in remote areas (1).\n• Does not use extra water (1), so it does not lower the water table (1).\n• Local communities build and own it (1), so it is more sustainable in the long term (1).` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nStudents investigated soil infiltration at two sites in a semi-arid area. They timed how long 500 ml of water took to soak into the ground inside a metal ring (data written for this practice question):\nSite 1 (grass and shrubs): 45 s, 52 s, 38 s\nSite 2 (bare, crusted soil): 210 s, 185 s, 240 s\nThe mean for Site 1 is 45 s and the mean for Site 2 is 212 s (to the nearest second).\n\nCompare the infiltration times at the two sites and suggest reasons for the differences.`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Comparison (max 2):\n• Water infiltrated much faster at Site 1 / slower at Site 2 (1)\n• Use of data, e.g. means of 45 s and 212 s / Site 2 took about 167 s longer / nearly five times as long (1)\n• All Site 2 readings were slower than all Site 1 readings / no overlap (1)\nReasons (max 2):\n• Plant roots create channels in the soil, letting water in (1)\n• Vegetation and organic matter make the soil less compacted / more porous (1)\n• Bare soil forms a hard crust (from raindrop impact or trampling), so water runs off the surface (1)` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nThe Sahel in Africa and parts of the Murray–Darling Basin in Australia both have semi-arid climates with unreliable rainfall.\n\nExplain why people in dryland areas of LICs are often more vulnerable to desertification than people in dryland areas of HICs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• In LICs most people depend directly on the land (subsistence farming, herding) for food and income; in HICs fewer people farm.\n• Lack of money for irrigation, fertilisers, drought-resistant seed or insurance.\n• Rapid population growth increases pressure on land.\n• Limited government support, early warning systems or aid in remote areas.\n• Few alternative jobs, so people cannot easily change livelihood.\n• HICs can import food and fund land management and compensation for farmers.\n\nBand 2 (3–4 marks): Clear explanation with developed reasons and a clear contrast between LICs and HICs.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): Simple statements, e.g. "LICs are poorer".\n0: No creditworthy response.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nThe Sahel lies on the southern edge of the Sahara. Rainfall is low, seasonal and unreliable, the population is growing rapidly, and most people depend on farming, herding and firewood.\n\nExplain why the Sahel is particularly vulnerable to desertification.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Physical: semi-arid climate with low rainfall (roughly 200–600 mm); long dry season; high temperatures and evaporation; very variable rainfall with droughts (late 1960s–1980s); intense storms causing soil erosion; fragile, thin soils with little organic matter; location next to the Sahara exposes it to dry winds.\n• Human: rapid population growth; overgrazing by large herds; over-cultivation and shorter fallow periods; deforestation for fuelwood and farmland; over-abstraction of groundwater; poverty limits investment in conservation; conflict disrupts land management.\n• Interaction: human pressures make the land less able to recover from droughts, creating a cycle of degradation.\n\nBand 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough explanation of a range of physical and human factors with clear links to land degradation; recognises how the factors interact. Accurate use of geographical terms.\nBand 2 (3–4 marks): Sound explanation of some factors; may be unbalanced between physical and human; some links made.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): Basic, mainly descriptive points with limited explanation.\n0: No creditworthy response.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nIn Senegal, part of the Great Green Wall has been planted with drought-resistant trees such as Acacia senegal, which produces gum arabic that local people can sell. Villagers also grow vegetables in community gardens inside the planted areas.\n\nExplain how tree-planting projects such as the Great Green Wall can reduce desertification.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Roots bind the soil and reduce erosion by wind and water; trees act as windbreaks and trap blowing sand.\n• Leaf litter adds organic matter and nutrients; acacias fix nitrogen, improving fertility.\n• Shade lowers soil temperature and reduces evaporation; more infiltration helps recharge groundwater.\n• Transpiration may increase local humidity.\n• Products (gum arabic, fruit, fodder, fuelwood) give income, so people protect the trees and are less likely to clear land; jobs reduce migration.\n• Community gardens give food security, reducing pressure on marginal land.\n• Limitations: seedlings may die in drought; trees need protection from livestock; success depends on local involvement and funding.\n\nBand 3 (5–6 marks): Thorough, well-developed explanation of how tree planting reduces degradation, linking environmental and social benefits; may recognise limits. Accurate terminology.\nBand 2 (3–4 marks): Sound explanation of some ways; limited development or links.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): Basic statements with little explanation.\n0: No creditworthy response.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nTwo views about desertification in the Sahel:\nView 1: "The droughts of the 1970s and 1980s show that desertification is mainly a natural process caused by changes in climate."\nView 2: "Desertification is mainly caused by people: overgrazing, over-cultivation and cutting down trees."\n\n"Human activity is the main cause of desertification." To what extent do you agree with this statement? Use examples you have studied.\n[8 marks + 3 marks for SPaG]`, marks: 8, command: 'To what extent', markScheme: `AO2 (4) AO3 (4). Indicative content:\n• Human causes: overgrazing (larger herds, trampling), over-cultivation and shorter fallow periods, deforestation for fuelwood, over-abstraction of water, population growth, poor land management, conflict; examples from the Sahel (e.g. Niger, Burkina Faso, Mali).\n• Physical causes: rainfall variability and drought (late 1960s–1980s), high temperatures and evaporation, intense storms, wind erosion; climate change (partly human-caused) is making droughts more frequent.\n• Interaction: drought turns pressure on the land into degradation; human activity reduces the land's ability to recover; poverty and population growth increase vulnerability.\n• Evidence that people can reverse it (re-greening in Niger, zaï pits) suggests human factors are decisive.\n• Judgement: e.g. climate triggers degradation but human activity is the main cause of long-term desertification — or a balanced conclusion that the causes cannot be separated.\n\nBand 3 (6–8 marks): Detailed, accurate knowledge of both human and physical causes, applied to named examples. A balanced, well-argued response reaching a clear, supported judgement on the extent of agreement.\nBand 2 (3–5 marks): Sound knowledge of causes; some application to examples; partial evaluation with a judgement that is only partly supported.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): Limited knowledge; mainly descriptive; little or no judgement.\n0: No creditworthy response.\n\nSPaG (3 marks):\nHigh (3): Spelling, punctuation and grammar consistently accurate; a wide range of specialist terms used precisely.\nIntermediate (2): Considerable accuracy in spelling, punctuation and grammar; a good range of specialist terms.\nThreshold (1): Reasonable accuracy; a limited range of specialist terms.\n0: Errors severely hinder meaning, or no response.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nTwo strategies used to reduce desertification in the Sahel:\nStrategy 1 — the Great Green Wall: an international project, launched in 2007 by the African Union, to restore 100 million hectares of land across about 8,000 km of the Sahel by 2030, funded by international donors.\nStrategy 2 — local soil and water conservation: villagers in Burkina Faso build stone lines and dig zaï pits on their own fields, using local stones, manure and simple tools.\n\nWhich strategy is more sustainable for reducing desertification in the Sahel? Justify your views.`, marks: 8, command: 'Justify', markScheme: `AO2 (4) AO3 (4). Indicative content:\n• Great Green Wall: large scale; tackles degradation across many countries; restores land, stores carbon, creates jobs; raises global awareness and funding. But slow progress, many planted seedlings die, depends on outside funding, disrupted by conflict, early top-down approach lacked local involvement; now a mosaic approach that includes local methods.\n• Stone lines and zaï pits: cheap appropriate technology using local materials; reduce run-off and soil erosion, increase infiltration and yields; owned and maintained by local people; can be copied by neighbouring farmers. But labour-intensive, cover only small areas, depend on rainfall, cannot tackle causes such as population growth.\n• Sustainability: economic (cost, income), social (community involvement, food security, reducing migration), environmental (soil, water, vegetation).\n• Justified conclusion: e.g. local strategies are more sustainable because communities own them, or the Great Green Wall is needed to work at the scale of the problem, or both are needed together.\n\nBand 3 (6–8 marks): Detailed understanding of both strategies applied to the Sahel. Evaluates sustainability (economic, social and environmental) and reaches a clear, well-justified decision.\nBand 2 (3–5 marks): Sound understanding of the strategies with some evaluation; decision partly justified.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): Limited understanding; mainly descriptive; decision absent or unsupported.\n0: No creditworthy response.` },
      { q: `Theme 3 (optional) — Desertification.\nIn the 1980s, many villages on the Central Plateau of Burkina Faso were losing farmland to desertification and people were leaving. Since then, farmers have used stone lines, zaï pits and protection of naturally regenerating trees. Some fields that had been abandoned are being farmed again, but the region is still affected by drought, rapid population growth and, more recently, armed conflict.\n\nEvaluate how successful strategies have been in reducing desertification in an area you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO2 (4) AO3 (4). Indicative content (credit any appropriate named area, e.g. Burkina Faso, Niger, Mali, Senegal, northern China):\n• Successes: abandoned land brought back into use; higher yields; rising water tables; more tree cover (e.g. over 5 million hectares re-greened in Niger); less seasonal migration; low-cost methods spread from farmer to farmer.\n• Limitations: strategies cover only part of the degraded area; labour-intensive; yields still fall in drought years; population growth continues to increase pressure; conflict stops projects and displaces people; large-scale projects such as the Great Green Wall behind target.\n• Criteria for success: scale of land restored, benefits to people, cost, long-term sustainability, whether causes are tackled.\n• Judgement: e.g. strategies have been successful locally but have not yet reversed desertification across the whole region.\n\nBand 3 (6–8 marks): Detailed, accurate knowledge of strategies in a named area. Evaluates success against clear criteria, weighing successes and limitations, and reaches a well-supported conclusion.\nBand 2 (3–5 marks): Sound knowledge with some evaluation; conclusion partly supported. May lack detail on the named area.\nBand 1 (1–2 marks): Limited, general knowledge; mainly descriptive; little or no evaluation.\n0: No creditworthy response.` }
    ]
  }
};
